window.AKT_UNIT({"unit":"Respiratory health","slug":"respiratory","notes":[{"id":"rs-asthma-dx","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Asthma: making the diagnosis","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Asthma needs three things: variable symptoms, no better explanation, and objective proof of variable airflow or airway inflammation.","Proof on spirometry: FEV1 rises by at least 12% and at least 200 mL after a bronchodilator.","Test before starting a preventer where possible. Do not delay treatment that is needed now.","Normal spirometry in a person who is well does not exclude asthma. Normal spirometry while symptomatic points elsewhere.","Work-related symptoms are referred straight away."],"curriculum":["Diagnose asthma in adolescents and adults using history, examination and objective testing.","Perform and interpret spirometry, including bronchodilator responsiveness.","Recognise conditions that mimic asthma and know when to refer."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook, version 3.0. Melbourne: National Asthma Council Australia; September 2025. Diagnosing asthma in adults and adolescents.","url":"https://www.asthmahandbook.org.au"},{"n":2,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Asthma diagnosis: children 6 years and older, adolescents and adults. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Melbourne: Therapeutic Guidelines Limited; published December 2020 (under review, June 2026).","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"criteria","h":"What makes the diagnosis","body":[{"p":"No single symptom or test proves asthma. The diagnosis is made when all three of the following are present, and the basis is written in the record. [1,2]"},{"tb":{"h":["Requirement","What counts"],"r":[["Variable symptoms","Wheeze, breathlessness, chest tightness or cough that comes and goes or varies in intensity"],["No better explanation","Other causes are unlikely after history and examination"],["Objective evidence","Variable expiratory airflow limitation on testing, or a raised exhaled nitric oxide (FeNO)"]]}},{"ul":["Wheeze and breathlessness are the symptoms most reliably linked to asthma. [1]","The examination is usually normal, so a normal chest does not exclude asthma. [1]","Evidence documented in the past counts. Variable airflow can be hard to show once a person is on an inhaled corticosteroid. [1]"]},{"key":"Symptoms suggest asthma. Only a test confirms it."}]},{"id":"history","h":"History that raises or lowers the odds","body":[{"tb":{"h":["More likely asthma","Less likely asthma"],"r":[["More than one of wheeze, breathlessness, chest tightness and cough","Chronic productive cough with no wheeze or breathlessness"],["Worse at night or in the early morning","Normal spirometry or a clear chest while symptomatic"],["Triggered by exercise, cold air or allergens","Voice change or throat tightness; worse with talking or laughing"],["Follows aspirin, NSAIDs or beta blockers","Dizziness, light-headedness or tingling fingers"],["Personal or family history of atopy","Symptoms only with colds, and none between"],["Widespread wheeze; unexplained blood eosinophilia","More than 20 pack-years of smoking; no response to a treatment trial"]]}},{"ul":["Pack-years: years smoked multiplied by cigarettes per day, divided by 20. [2]","Ask about home and work exposures, smoking, vaping and cannabis. [1]","Adolescents: see the young person alone for part of the visit to ask about smoking and vaping. [1]","Recurrent 'bronchitis' or a cough that drags on after infections should raise suspicion. [1]"]}]},{"id":"spirometry","h":"Spirometry with bronchodilator","body":[{"ul":["Who: every person aged 6 or over with suspected asthma. [2]","When: before a regular preventer is started where possible. Treatment makes the diagnosis harder to confirm later. [1,2]","How: FEV1 before, then 10 to 15 minutes after salbutamol 200 to 400 micrograms, with at least three blows each time. [1,2]","No spirometer in the practice: refer to an accredited laboratory. [1]"]},{"tb":{"h":["Finding","Meaning"],"r":[["FEV1 up by 12% or more and 200 mL or more","Positive bronchodilator response: supports asthma"],["FEV1 up by more than 400 mL","Strongly supports asthma"],["Reduced FEV1/FVC ratio","Expiratory airflow limitation"],["Low FEV1 alone","Non-specific"],["Normal result while well","Does not exclude asthma: repeat when symptomatic"],["Normal result while symptomatic","Consider another diagnosis"]]}},{"ul":["Significant symptoms or exacerbations: start treatment now and test as soon as possible. [1,2]","New-onset symptoms in an acute presentation: treat as acute asthma, then investigate after recovery. [1]","Seasonal symptoms: test during the pollen season, and consider the risk of thunderstorm asthma. [2]"]},{"key":"Both parts are needed: 12% and 200 mL."}]},{"id":"next","h":"When the first test does not settle it","body":[{"p":"If asthma is still suspected after a non-diagnostic first test, the options are stepped. [1]"},{"tb":{"h":["Step","Detail"],"r":[["Repeat spirometry","On another day, ideally while symptomatic or in the early morning"],["FeNO","Measures airway inflammation. In adults, 40 ppb or more supports asthma when the picture fits. A normal result does not exclude it"],["Peak flow diary","Use if spirometry is delayed. Morning and about 4 pm for 2 to 4 weeks, best of three. Average daily variation over 10% supports asthma"],["Bronchial provocation","Accredited laboratory only. Not required for diagnosis"],["Specialist referral","If the diagnosis remains uncertain and symptoms persist"]]}},{"ul":["Daily peak flow variation: the day's highest minus lowest, divided by the mean of the two. [1]","Always use the same peak flow meter. A peak flow meter does not replace spirometry. [2]","Bronchial provocation is contraindicated in pregnancy and when FEV1 is under 60% of predicted. [1]","Before provocation testing, withhold salbutamol for 4 hours and formoterol or salmeterol for 24 hours. [1]"]}]},{"id":"trial","h":"Treatment trial","body":[{"ul":["Use: when a person cannot perform spirometry, or suspicion is strong and spirometry is normal. [2]","Method: a reliever alone, or with a regular low-dose inhaled corticosteroid. [2]","Supports asthma: a clear improvement in symptoms or lung function after at least 4 weeks of inhaled corticosteroid. [2]","No improvement after 4 weeks: consider another diagnosis. [2]","FEV1 rising by 200 mL and 12% after 4 weeks of inhaled corticosteroid also counts as objective evidence. [2]"]},{"key":"A treatment trial is a fallback, and it is judged at 4 weeks."}]},{"id":"nottests","h":"Tests that do not make the diagnosis","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Test","Role"],"r":[["Chest X-ray or CT","Not needed for diagnosis. Used for unusual features or to look for another cause"],["Blood eosinophil count","Not a primary diagnostic test. May support asthma in a symptomatic acute presentation"],["Allergy testing","Does not confirm or exclude asthma. Guides trigger management only"],["Peak flow meter alone","Cannot show reversibility or a reduced ratio"]]}}]},{"id":"mimics","h":"Mimics and special groups","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Clue","Think of"],"r":[["Throat tightness, voice change, inspiratory noise","Inducible laryngeal obstruction (vocal cord dysfunction)"],["Tingling, light-headedness, sighing","Dysfunctional breathing or anxiety"],["Over 20 pack-years, fixed obstruction","COPD, alone or with asthma"],["Dry cough on an ACE inhibitor","Drug-induced cough"],["Daily wet cough","Bronchiectasis or chronic bronchitis"],["Orthopnoea, oedema, crackles","Heart failure"],["Unilateral wheeze in a child","Inhaled foreign body"]]}},{"ul":["Work-related symptoms: refer immediately. Occupational asthma can cause rapid, permanent loss of lung function. [1]","Reaction to aspirin or NSAIDs: consider aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease and refer. [1]","Older adults: asthma can start at any age and may present as cough alone. [1]","Asthma with COPD features: an inhaled corticosteroid is needed. Long-acting bronchodilators alone carry higher risk. [1]","Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander adults in remote areas: consider bronchiectasis, which needs high-resolution CT. [1]","Competitive athletes: refer, because sporting bodies set their own testing requirements. [1]"]}]},{"id":"differ","h":"Where the sources differ","body":[{"p":"The Therapeutic Guidelines respiratory topics date from 2020 and are under review. This page follows the 2025 Australian Asthma Handbook where the two differ."},{"tb":{"h":["Point","Australian Asthma Handbook 2025","Therapeutic Guidelines 2020"],"r":[["FeNO","Recommended with spirometry; 40 ppb or more supports asthma in adults","A specialist test, not readily available in primary care"],["Peak flow diary","2 to 4 weeks; average daily variation over 10%","14 days; over 20% between the two highest and two lowest readings"]]}}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["A young adult with episodic wheeze and normal spirometry on a well day: what next.","Reading a bronchodilator response: is 9% and 250 mL positive.","A person already started on a preventer elsewhere with no objective test on record.","New wheeze in a baker or spray painter.","Throat tightness and normal spirometry during symptoms.","Choosing between chest X-ray, allergy tests and spirometry as the first test."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-lungfn","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Spirometry, oximetry and blood gases","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Obstruction is a low FEV1/FVC ratio. Restriction is a low FVC with a normal or high ratio.","Spirometry is deferred for a week after a heart attack or eye, sinus or middle ear surgery, and for 4 weeks after chest or abdominal surgery.","A pulse oximeter says nothing about carbon dioxide, and reads falsely high in carbon monoxide poisoning.","An abnormal venous gas is followed by an arterial gas."],"curriculum":["Perform and interpret spirometry.","Know the uses and limits of peak flow, pulse oximetry and blood gas analysis.","Know which lung function tests need a respiratory laboratory."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Pulmonary function testing. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Melbourne: Therapeutic Guidelines Limited; published December 2020.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"measures","h":"What spirometry measures","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Measure","Meaning"],"r":[["FEV1","Volume breathed out in the first second of a forced breath"],["FVC","Total volume breathed out in a forced breath"],["FEV1/FVC ratio","FEV1 as a fraction of FVC, for example 0.7 or 70%"]]}},{"ul":["The manoeuvre: breathe in fully, then blow out as hard and as long as possible. [1]","Results are compared with reference values matched for age, sex, height and ethnicity. [1]","Spirometry can be done and read in general practice. Operator training is recommended first. [1]","Quality depends on maintained equipment, operator technique and patient effort with a good lip seal. [1]","Not every child can perform it reliably, and unwell patients may not manage the effort. [1]"]}]},{"id":"patterns","h":"Reading the pattern","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Pattern","Spirometry","Flow-volume loop","Examples"],"r":[["Obstructive","Low FEV1/FVC ratio","Concave (scooped) expiratory limb","Asthma, COPD, asthma with COPD"],["Restrictive","Low FVC; ratio normal or raised","Normal shape, small volume","Interstitial lung disease, respiratory muscle weakness, kyphoscoliosis, obesity"],["Obstruction with low FVC","Low ratio and low FVC","Scooped and small","Gas trapping, or mixed disease"]]}},{"ul":["Reversible obstruction: the loop returns almost to normal after a bronchodilator. [1]","Age changes the normal loop, so results are read against age-matched values. [1]","Suspected restriction is assessed further with static lung volumes in a laboratory. [1]"]},{"key":"Read the ratio first. Low ratio means obstruction. Normal ratio with a low FVC suggests restriction."}]},{"id":"contra","h":"When to defer spirometry","body":[{"p":"These are relative contraindications. Weigh risk against benefit. A respiratory laboratory can test higher-risk patients, and the referral should name the concern. [1]"},{"tb":{"h":["Timeframe","Condition"],"r":[["Previous week","Heart attack; eye, sinus or middle ear surgery; middle ear infection"],["Previous 4 weeks","Chest or abdominal surgery"],["Current or recent","Pneumothorax; haemoptysis; concussion with ongoing symptoms"],["Any time","Past fainting with forced breathing out or coughing"]]}},{"ul":["Current respiratory infection: apply infection control, such as filters and single-use mouthpieces. [1]"]}]},{"id":"pef","h":"Peak flow","body":[{"ul":["What it is: the fastest flow during a forced breath out, in litres per minute. [1]","Use: tracking variable obstruction and response to treatment in asthma. [1]","Same meter every time: readings differ a lot between meters. [1]","Not for diagnosis in place of spirometry: it cannot show reversibility or a reduced ratio. [1]","Not useful in COPD, where the obstruction does not vary. [1]","Unreliable in children. [1]"]},{"key":"Peak flow monitors known asthma. It does not diagnose it, and it has no role in COPD."}]},{"id":"oximetry","h":"Pulse oximetry and its traps","body":[{"p":"A pulse oximeter estimates haemoglobin oxygen saturation. It does not measure ventilation or carbon dioxide. [1]"},{"tb":{"h":["Situation","Effect on the reading"],"r":[["Carbon monoxide exposure, heavy smoking","Falsely high"],["Methaemoglobinaemia (dapsone, benzocaine, lidocaine, nitrates, some antimalarials)","Falsely high or low"],["Poor perfusion, cold hands, low cardiac output","Unreliable"],["Nail polish","Unreliable: turn the probe side to side on the finger"],["Dark skin","Less accurate"],["Severe anaemia, cyanide poisoning","Reading is correct but falsely reassuring: oxygen delivery is poor"]]}},{"key":"A normal saturation does not exclude carbon dioxide retention or carbon monoxide poisoning."}]},{"id":"gases","h":"Blood gases","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Measure","Normal arterial value"],"r":[["pH","7.35 to 7.45"],["PaO2","80 to 100 mmHg (varies with age and altitude)"],["PaCO2","35 to 45 mmHg"],["Bicarbonate","22 to 26 mmol/L"],["Base excess","-2 to +2 mmol/L"]]}},{"ul":["Arterial gas uses: severe acute illness in hospital, chronic respiratory failure, and assessment for home oxygen. [1]","Venous gas: values do not track arterial values directly, and its usefulness is debated. [1]","Venous gas alone is not enough to assess respiratory failure. If it is abnormal, take an arterial sample. [1]"]}]},{"id":"lab","h":"Tests that need a respiratory laboratory","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Test","What it shows","Typical use"],"r":[["Gas transfer (DLCO)","How well gas crosses from air sacs to blood","Unexplained breathlessness; baseline before amiodarone, methotrexate or leflunomide"],["Static lung volumes","Total lung capacity and residual volume","Confirming restriction or hyperinflation; before lung resection"],["Bronchial provocation","Airway hyperresponsiveness","Uncertain asthma; some pre-employment checks"],["Exercise testing","Cause and degree of exercise limitation","Unexplained breathlessness; fitness for surgery"],["Hypoxic challenge","Oxygen level in simulated altitude","Fitness to fly"]]}},{"ul":["Beta agonists, antimuscarinics and antihistamines interfere with provocation tests. Ask the laboratory what to withhold. [1]"]}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["A printed spirometry result: obstructive, restrictive or normal.","A request for spirometry five days after cataract surgery or two weeks after a laparoscopy.","A house-fire survivor with a headache and a saturation of 99%.","A drowsy patient with COPD and a normal saturation on oxygen.","Which test to order before starting amiodarone or methotrexate.","A patient with COPD asking for a peak flow meter."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-asthma-adult","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Asthma in adults and adolescents: choosing and adjusting treatment","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Every person aged 12 or over with asthma gets an inhaled corticosteroid. A blue reliever alone is no longer acceptable.","Most start on low-dose budesonide-formoterol taken only when needed.","Next step up is the same inhaler used every day and for relief (MART).","Check adherence, technique, triggers and the diagnosis before any step up.","Still uncontrolled on a medium-dose regimen: check blood eosinophils and refer."],"curriculum":["Start and adjust asthma treatment in adolescents and adults in line with current Australian guidelines.","Explain anti-inflammatory reliever and maintenance-and-reliever regimens.","Recognise difficult-to-treat asthma and refer appropriately."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook, version 3.0. Melbourne: National Asthma Council Australia; September 2025. Treatment levels; Initial asthma treatment after diagnosis; Adjusting treatment (adults and adolescents); tables of approved reliever and maintenance-and-reliever products and of inhaled corticosteroid doses.","url":"https://www.asthmahandbook.org.au"},{"n":2,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Melbourne: Therapeutic Guidelines Limited; published December 2020 (under review, June 2026).","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"principles","h":"The 2025 change","body":[{"ul":["Inhaled corticosteroid for all: every treatment level for people aged 12 and over contains one. [1]","No reliever-only treatment: salbutamol or terbutaline alone is not recommended, even for infrequent symptoms. [1]","Why: reliever-only treatment carries a higher risk of severe flare-ups than as-needed budesonide-formoterol. [1]","Never a long-acting beta agonist without an inhaled corticosteroid. [1]","Three or more salbutamol canisters a year signals higher flare-up risk. Twelve or more signals higher risk of asthma death. [1]"]},{"tb":{"h":["Term","Meaning"],"r":[["AIR (anti-inflammatory reliever)","A corticosteroid-formoterol inhaler taken only when symptoms occur"],["MART (maintenance and reliever therapy)","The same corticosteroid-formoterol inhaler taken every day and also for relief"],["Reliever-only treatment","Salbutamol or terbutaline with no corticosteroid: not recommended"]]}},{"key":"The blue puffer alone is out. The reliever of choice now carries a corticosteroid."}]},{"id":"levels","h":"Treatment levels","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Level","Recommended","Alternative"],"r":[["1","Low-dose budesonide-formoterol as needed","Daily low-dose corticosteroid plus salbutamol as needed"],["2","Low-dose MART","Daily low-dose corticosteroid-LABA plus salbutamol as needed"],["3","Medium-dose MART","Daily medium-dose corticosteroid-LABA plus salbutamol as needed"],["4","Specialist-directed: corticosteroid-LABA with add-ons such as a LAMA or a monoclonal antibody",""]]}},{"ul":["Medium-dose MART: more daily maintenance puffs of the low-dose inhaler. The reliever dose stays low. [1]","Only budesonide-formoterol is approved for as-needed use without maintenance. [1]","Only certain budesonide-formoterol and beclometasone-formoterol inhalers can be used as MART. Other combinations cannot. [1]","Daily low-dose corticosteroid alone is often poorly adhered to when symptoms are infrequent, which is why it is the alternative. [1]"]}]},{"id":"start","h":"Starting treatment","body":[{"tb":{"h":["At diagnosis","Start with"],"r":[["Most people","Low-dose budesonide-formoterol as needed"],["Frequent or severe symptoms","Low-dose or medium-dose MART"],["A flare-up needing oral corticosteroids in the past 12 months","MART"],["Any past life-threatening flare-up","MART"],["Diagnosed during a severe acute episode","MART, even if an oral corticosteroid course is given"]]}},{"ul":["Carry the reliever at all times. [1]","Before exercise: one dose of the usual reliever 15 minutes beforehand. No separate salbutamol inhaler is added. [1]","PBS: MART is not subsidised as the first treatment after diagnosis. Check restrictions. [1]","Spacer, not nebuliser: a pressurised inhaler with a spacer works as well and faster. [1]","Give a written action plan that matches the regimen, and check inhaler technique. [1]","Review within 2 to 3 months of starting. [1]"]},{"key":"Default start: budesonide-formoterol as needed. Start higher if symptoms are frequent or there has been a serious flare-up."}]},{"id":"doses","h":"Doses and daily limits","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Inhaler","As-needed dose","Low-dose MART maintenance","Maximum"],"r":[["Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 dry powder","1 inhalation","2 a day, in one or two doses","6 at a time; 8 a day (12 briefly)"],["Budesonide-formoterol 100/3 pressurised","2 inhalations","2 twice a day","12 at a time; 16 a day (24 briefly)"],["Beclometasone-formoterol 100/6 pressurised (MART only, adults)","1 inhalation","1 twice a day","6 at a time; 8 a day"]]}},{"ul":["Medium-dose MART: 200/6 dry powder 2 twice a day, or 100/3 pressurised 4 twice a day. [1]","The daily maximum is rarely needed. Worsening over 3 days despite extra doses needs medical review. [1]","Most products are approved from age 12. Some are approved from 18 only. [1]"]},{"tb":{"h":["Inhaled corticosteroid","Low daily dose (micrograms)","Medium","High"],"r":[["Beclometasone (extra-fine)","100 to 200","250 to 400","over 400"],["Budesonide","200 to 400","500 to 800","over 800"],["Ciclesonide","80 to 160","240 to 320","over 320"],["Fluticasone propionate","100 to 200","250 to 500","over 500"]]}},{"p":"Most of the benefit of an inhaled corticosteroid comes at low dose. Long-term high doses add risk, including cataract and pneumonia. [1]"}]},{"id":"stepup","h":"Before and during a step up","body":[{"p":"Poor control is not stepped up until four things are checked. [1]"},{"tb":{"h":["Check","What to look for"],"r":[["Adherence","Is the preventer actually being taken? Dispensing records help"],["Technique","Watch the person use the inhaler"],["Triggers","Smoke, allergens, viral infections, work exposures"],["Diagnosis and comorbidity","Rhinitis, obesity, deconditioning, heart disease, laryngeal obstruction"]]}},{"ul":["Move one level at a time. [1]","On a fixed corticosteroid-LABA with salbutamol: switch to MART before raising the dose. [1]","On as-needed budesonide-formoterol and needing it 3 or more times a week: add daily maintenance (low-dose MART). [1]","A LAMA add-on helps most when lung function is reduced. Spirometry guides it. [1]"]}]},{"id":"refer","h":"When to refer","body":[{"ul":["Uncontrolled on medium-dose MART or medium-dose corticosteroid-LABA, with good adherence and technique: refer. [1]","Order a blood eosinophil count while waiting. Add laboratory spirometry if the wait is long. [1]","Eosinophils 150 per microlitre or more on medium-dose treatment suggest persistent type 2 inflammation, which may suit a monoclonal antibody. [1]","Eosinophils 1,500 per microlitre or more need urgent specialist assessment for other disease. [1]","Level 4 treatment is specialist-directed. [1]"]},{"key":"Medium-dose regimen, taken properly, still uncontrolled: eosinophils and referral, not a higher steroid dose."}]},{"id":"stepdown","h":"Stepping down","body":[{"ul":["When: good control for 2 to 3 months and low flare-up risk. [1]","How: down one level, then review in 4 to 8 weeks. [1]","Not now: during a respiratory infection, allergen season, travel or pregnancy. [1]","Floor: never stop the inhaled corticosteroid entirely. The lowest treatment is as-needed budesonide-formoterol. [1]","Low-dose MART steps down to as-needed use with the same inhaler. [1]"]}]},{"id":"differ","h":"Where the sources differ","body":[{"p":"This page follows the 2025 Australian Asthma Handbook. The Therapeutic Guidelines asthma topics date from 2020 and are under review."},{"tb":{"h":["Point","Australian Asthma Handbook 2025","Therapeutic Guidelines 2020"],"r":[["Reliever alone","Not recommended for anyone aged 12 or over","Accepted as one option for symptoms less than twice a month"],["First treatment","As-needed budesonide-formoterol","As-needed reliever, or daily low-dose corticosteroid, or as-needed budesonide-formoterol"],["Structure","Four levels, each with a MART or AIR option first","Five steps"]]}}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["A newly diagnosed adult with symptoms twice a month: what to prescribe.","A patient who has used salbutamol alone for years and is 'fine'.","Poor control on low-dose fluticasone-salmeterol: the step before a higher dose.","Uncontrolled on medium-dose MART with good technique.","How many extra puffs are allowed in a day.","A request to stop the preventer after three good months, or during pregnancy."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-asthma-review","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Asthma in adults and adolescents: review, action plans and pregnancy","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Good control over 4 weeks: symptoms and reliever on 2 days a week or fewer, no night waking, no limits on activity.","Any one of those failing is poor control.","Everyone has a written action plan that says when to add oral corticosteroids and when to call an ambulance.","Flare-up not settling: prednisolone 37.5 to 50 mg each morning for 5 to 10 days, no taper.","Pregnancy: keep treating, step up if needed, do not step down."],"curriculum":["Assess asthma control and future risk at review.","Write and update an asthma action plan.","Manage asthma in pregnancy and address comorbidities and triggers."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook, version 3.0. Melbourne: National Asthma Council Australia; September 2025. Assessing and reviewing asthma in adults and adolescents; Adjusting treatment for adults and adolescents.","url":"https://www.asthmahandbook.org.au"},{"n":2,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Melbourne: Therapeutic Guidelines Limited; published December 2020 (under review, June 2026).","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"control","h":"Is it controlled?","body":[{"p":"Control is judged over the previous 4 weeks. [1]"},{"tb":{"h":["Measure","Good control","Poor control (any one)"],"r":[["Daytime symptoms","2 days a week or fewer","More than 2 days a week"],["Reliever use (not counting before exercise)","2 days a week or fewer","More than 2 days a week"],["Night or waking symptoms","None","Any"],["Activity limitation","None","Any"]]}},{"ul":["Also ask about days off work or school, use of the action plan, and urgent visits. [1]","Reliever needed on more than 2 days a week: review soon, not at the next routine visit. [1]","Validated tools: the Asthma Control Test and the Asthma Control Questionnaire. [1]","Adolescents under-report. Ask about avoided sport, vaping and missed school. [1]"]},{"key":"Any night waking or any activity limit means poor control."}]},{"id":"risk","h":"Who is at risk of a flare-up","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Group","Risk factors"],"r":[["History","Poor symptom control; a severe flare-up in the past year; any past intensive care admission or intubation"],["Medicines","Reliever overuse; no corticosteroid; poor adherence or technique"],["Comorbidity","Obesity, chronic rhinosinusitis, reflux, confirmed food allergy, pregnancy"],["Exposure","Smoking, vaping, allergens, air pollution"],["Tests","Low FEV1; large bronchodilator response; raised eosinophils or FeNO"]]}},{"ul":["The two strongest predictors are poor symptom control and a severe flare-up in the past year. [1]","Food allergy rarely triggers asthma, but a confirmed food allergy is a risk factor for asthma death. [2]"]}]},{"id":"review","h":"The review visit","body":[{"ul":["How often: at least once a year, and 2 to 3 months after any new treatment. [1]","Every review: control, flare-ups, triggers, adherence, inhaler technique, side effects, comorbidities, and the action plan. [1]","Spirometry: at diagnosis, when control is poor, and about every 2 years even when well. [1]","Monitoring spirometry does not need bronchodilators withheld. Diagnostic spirometry does. [1]","Clinic peak flow is not used to assess lung function. [1]","Mismatch between symptoms and spirometry: reconsider the diagnosis or refer. [1]","Smoking history in everyone. Consider coexisting COPD in current and former smokers. [1]"]}]},{"id":"plan","h":"Written action plans","body":[{"p":"Every person with asthma has a written plan matched to their regimen. It is reviewed yearly and whenever treatment changes. [1,2]"},{"tb":{"h":["The plan states","Detail"],"r":[["Usual treatment","Maintenance and reliever doses"],["Reliever limits","Maximum per occasion and per 24 hours for that inhaler"],["Signs of worsening","What the person will notice"],["Oral corticosteroids","When to start a course"],["Help","When to see a doctor and when to call an ambulance"]]}},{"ul":["Symptom-based plans suit most people. [2]","Peak-flow plans: for poor perceivers, over-perceivers and people with sudden severe attacks. Use personal best, not predicted. [2]","On budesonide-formoterol: extra doses raise the corticosteroid automatically. Add prednisolone if that fails. [2]","On corticosteroid alone: quadruple the dose for 1 to 2 weeks. Doubling is not enough. [2]","On a fixed corticosteroid-LABA: a prednisolone course is usually more practical than adding a second inhaler. [2]"]}]},{"id":"ocs","h":"Oral corticosteroids for a flare-up","body":[{"ul":["When: symptoms return within 4 hours of the reliever, or do not settle promptly. [1]","Adults: prednisolone 37.5 to 50 mg each morning for 5 to 10 days. [1,2]","Adolescents: 1 mg/kg (maximum 50 mg) once daily for 3 days. [1]","No taper is needed after a short course. [1]","Prescribe the exact quantity with no repeats, and track how often courses are needed. [1]","Repeated courses carry long-term harm: osteoporosis, diabetes, cataract, weight gain. [1]"]},{"key":"A short course stops without a taper. Repeated courses are a reason to step up or refer."}]},{"id":"pregnancy","h":"Pregnancy","body":[{"ul":["Manage as for any adult, aiming for the best possible control. [1]","Step up when needed. Do not step down during pregnancy. [1]","Do not stop the inhaled corticosteroid. [1]","About 40% of pregnant women with asthma get worse, and at least 20% have a flare-up needing medical care. [1]","Flare-ups are linked to low birth weight, preterm birth and small-for-dates babies. [1]","Assess control at antenatal visits. [1]"]},{"key":"Uncontrolled asthma is the risk to the baby. The inhalers are not."}]},{"id":"general","h":"Comorbidities, triggers and lifestyle","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Issue","What to do"],"r":[["Smoking and vaping","Always address. Cigarette smoke is the one trigger to avoid completely"],["Obesity","Weight loss of 5 to 10% can improve control. Confirm asthma objectively before escalating"],["Rhinitis","Present in most people with allergic asthma. Treating it helps control"],["Reflux","Common, but treating it does not reliably improve asthma"],["Anxiety and depression","Screen. Both worsen control and adherence"],["Beta blockers","If one becomes indicated, start low under supervision"],["NSAIDs","Avoid in aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease"]]}},{"ul":["Exercise is encouraged. Asthma is not a reason to avoid it. [2]","Breathlessness that worsens in the minutes after stopping exercise suggests asthma rather than poor fitness. [2]","Vitamin D and other supplements have no proven benefit. [2]","Hoarseness and thrush: use a spacer, and rinse and spit after maintenance doses. [1]","Montelukast: an alternative that is less effective than an inhaled corticosteroid. Warn about mood and behaviour change, and stop it if they occur. [2]"]}]},{"id":"differ","h":"Where the sources differ","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Point","Australian Asthma Handbook 2025","Therapeutic Guidelines 2020"],"r":[["Adolescent oral corticosteroid course","1 mg/kg (maximum 50 mg) for 3 days","Not read"],["Raising inhaled corticosteroid in a plan","Not read on the pages reviewed","Quadruple for 1 to 2 weeks if on corticosteroid alone"]]}},{"p":"The two sources agree on the adult prednisolone dose and duration."}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["Classifying control from a 4-week history that includes one night waking.","What an action plan must contain.","A flare-up on budesonide-formoterol that has not settled after extra doses.","A pregnant woman who has stopped her preventer.","A request for a 'standby' prednisolone script with repeats.","A patient with asthma who now needs a beta blocker after a heart attack."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-asthma-acute","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Acute asthma in adults and adolescents","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Sort by speech and saturation: whole sentences and over 94% is mild to moderate.","Salbutamol through a spacer: 4 to 12 puffs if mild to moderate, 12 puffs if severe, every 20 minutes for the first hour.","Cannot finish a sentence, or saturation 94% or less: call an ambulance while treating.","Oral corticosteroids for every adult who presents, started within the hour.","Do not send home if reliever is still needed more often than every 4 hours. Review in 3 to 5 days."],"curriculum":["Assess and manage an acute asthma exacerbation in the community, including first aid.","Recognise severe and life-threatening asthma and arrange transfer.","Arrange post-acute care and follow-up, and advise people at risk of thunderstorm asthma."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook, version 3.0. Melbourne: National Asthma Council Australia; September 2025. Managing acute asthma in adults and adolescents in primary care, with tables of immediate treatment and secondary severity assessment; Thunderstorm asthma.","url":"https://www.asthmahandbook.org.au"},{"n":2,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Acute asthma. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Melbourne: Therapeutic Guidelines Limited; published December 2020 (under review, June 2026).","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"firstaid","h":"First aid in the community","body":[{"p":"The 4 x 4 x 4 plan is the standard community first aid for an asthma attack. [2]"},{"tb":{"h":["Step","Action"],"r":[["1","Give 4 separate puffs of salbutamol through a spacer, with 4 breaths after each puff"],["2","Wait 4 minutes. If no better, give 4 more puffs"],["3","Still no better: call an ambulance and say it is a severe asthma attack"],["4","Keep giving 4 puffs every 4 minutes until help arrives (up to 8 puffs at a time for an adult)"]]}},{"ul":["No salbutamol to hand, age 12 or over: budesonide-formoterol 2 inhalations, then 1 more every 4 minutes, to 6 in total. [2]","Reliever needed more often than every 3 to 4 hours after settling: same-day medical review. [2]"]}]},{"id":"severity","h":"Rapid severity assessment","body":[{"p":"Assess by looking, listening to speech and measuring saturation on room air. [1]"},{"tb":{"h":["Severity","Features","Where"],"r":[["Mild to moderate (all of)","Can walk; whole sentences in one breath; saturation over 94%","Manage in the practice"],["Severe (any of)","Cannot finish a sentence; accessory muscles, tracheal tug or recession; obvious distress; saturation 94% or less","Ambulance; treat while waiting"],["Life-threatening (any of)","Drowsy, collapsed or exhausted; cyanosis; poor effort; soft or absent breath sounds; saturation under 92%","Ambulance; treat while waiting"]]}},{"tb":{"h":["Sign on fuller assessment","Mild to moderate","Severe","Life-threatening"],"r":[["Respiratory rate","Under 25","25 or more","Slow (exhaustion)"],["Heart rate","Under 110","110 or more","Arrhythmia or bradycardia"],["Chest","Wheeze or normal","","Silent chest"],["Posture","Can lie flat","Cannot lie flat; hunched forward","Collapsed"]]}},{"key":"A quiet chest, a slowing pulse or a slowing breathing rate in a tiring patient is a sign of impending arrest, not recovery."}]},{"id":"treat","h":"Immediate treatment","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Severity","Salbutamol 100 microgram puffs through a spacer","Repeat"],"r":[["Mild to moderate","4 to 12 puffs","Every 20 to 30 minutes in the first hour if needed"],["Severe","12 puffs","At least every 20 minutes for the first hour"],["Life-threatening","12 puffs, or 2 x 5 mg nebules continuously, driven by oxygen","Continuous until help arrives"]]}},{"ul":["Technique: one puff at a time into the spacer, with normal (tidal) breathing. [1]","Nebuliser: only if the person cannot use a spacer. Use 5 mg for a severe attack. [1]","Nebulisers generate aerosols: apply infection control if a viral infection is likely. [1]","Not relieved within 5 minutes: repeat the dose and arrange transfer to an emergency department. [1]","Ipratropium: added to salbutamol in severe and life-threatening attacks, 8 puffs of 21 micrograms through a spacer. [2]","Magnesium sulfate 10 mmol intravenously over 20 minutes is a hospital step for attacks that do not respond. [2]"]},{"key":"A spacer with 12 puffs matches a nebuliser. Reach for the nebuliser only when a spacer cannot be used."}]},{"id":"oxygen","h":"Oxygen","body":[{"ul":["Start: when saturation is under 92%. [1,2]","Target: 92 to 96%. [1,2]","At risk of carbon dioxide retention: target 88 to 92%. [1]","Do not over-oxygenate adults. Saturation above the target range raises the risk of carbon dioxide retention. [1]"]}]},{"id":"steroids","h":"Corticosteroids","body":[{"ul":["Who: every adult and adolescent presenting with acute asthma, whatever the severity at first assessment. [1]","When: as soon as practical, and within the first hour. [2]","Adults: prednisolone 37.5 to 50 mg, then each morning for a total of 5 to 10 days. [1,2]","Adolescents: 1 mg/kg (maximum 50 mg) once daily for 3 to 5 days. [1]","Alternative for adults: dexamethasone 16 mg, repeated once the next day. [2]","Cannot swallow: hydrocortisone 100 mg intravenously every 6 hours. [2]"]},{"key":"Corticosteroids go in early for everyone who presents. They are not held back for the severe cases."}]},{"id":"anaphylaxis","h":"Is it anaphylaxis?","body":[{"ul":["Sudden breathlessness with hives, flushing or swelling is anaphylaxis. [2]","Also suspect it when sudden breathlessness comes with dizziness, low blood pressure, vomiting or diarrhoea, even without a rash. [2]","Treatment: intramuscular adrenaline first. Give it if anaphylaxis cannot be excluded. [1,2]","Food allergy rarely triggers asthma, but confirmed food allergy is a risk factor for fatal asthma. [1]"]},{"key":"Wheeze plus skin, gut or circulation signs: give adrenaline."}]},{"id":"after","h":"Observation, discharge and follow-up","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Step","Detail"],"r":[["Observe","At least 4 hours after distress has settled, in the practice or with a responsible adult"],["Do not send home if","Reliever is still needed more often than every 4 hours"],["Admit","Any life-threatening feature, or severe features that persist after first treatment"],["Also weigh","Whether the person can lie flat without breathlessness, time of day, distance from help, phone access, past severe attacks"],["First follow-up","Within 3 to 5 days"],["Full review","About 2 weeks later"]]}},{"ul":["Written instructions: preventer, reliever as needed (not by the clock), prednisolone timing, and when to call an ambulance. [1]","Preventer after an attack: start or continue a maintenance corticosteroid-LABA with at least a medium corticosteroid dose. [1]","Before leaving: check technique, adherence and triggers, and give an interim action plan. [1,2]","Consider specialist referral after a first presentation or any hospital admission. [2]","Near-fatal asthma: specialist follow-up for life. [2]"]}]},{"id":"storm","h":"Thunderstorm asthma","body":[{"ul":["What: spring and early summer storms in high grass-pollen regions break pollen into tiny particles that reach the small airways. [1]","Who: people with ryegrass allergy or springtime hay fever, including those who have never had asthma. [1]","Highest risk: asthma with grass pollen allergy and no inhaled corticosteroid, such as people self-treating with pharmacy salbutamol. [1]","Protect: an inhaled corticosteroid-containing regimen through the pollen season. [1]","Check the reliever is in date and has enough doses. [1]","On high-pollen storm days: stay inside with windows shut, especially in the gusts before the rain. [1]","Turn off evaporative coolers. In a car, close windows and recirculate. [1]","Nasal corticosteroid sprays: it is not known whether they protect. [1]"]},{"key":"Springtime hay fever is the risk marker. The protection is an inhaled corticosteroid."}]},{"id":"differ","h":"Where the sources differ","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Point","Australian Asthma Handbook 2025","Therapeutic Guidelines 2020"],"r":[["Oxygen target","93 to 95% in the text; 92 to 96% in its table","92 to 96%"],["Corticosteroids","All who present, regardless of severity","All but the mildest cases"],["Ipratropium","Not on the primary care page","With salbutamol for severe and life-threatening attacks"],["First follow-up","Within 3 to 5 days","Within 2 to 3 days"],["Adolescent prednisolone","3 to 5 days","3 days (children)"]]}},{"p":"This page uses the 92 to 96% target, which both sources print."}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["Classifying an attack from speech, saturation and pulse.","The salbutamol dose and interval for a severe attack in the treatment room.","A patient who has gone quiet and drowsy with a silent chest.","Wheeze with hives after a meal.","Whether a patient who settled after one dose needs prednisolone.","Who can go home, and when to see them again.","Advice for a hay fever sufferer before storm season."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-asthma-kids","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Wheeze and asthma in children: diagnosis and long-term treatment","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Asthma is not diagnosed or treated under 12 months. Persistent wheeze in an infant is referred.","Ages 1 to 5: no lung function test is possible. The evidence is a rapid response to salbutamol.","Ages 6 to 11: spirometry with bronchodilator, or FeNO of 25 ppb or more.","Under 12, salbutamol as needed is still level 1. Add low-dose inhaled corticosteroid for frequent symptoms or flare-ups.","No nebulisers at home, no oral salbutamol, no theophylline."],"curriculum":["Assess the wheezy child and diagnose asthma by age group.","Start and step asthma treatment in children, including when to refer.","Recognise features that point to another diagnosis."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook, version 3.0. Melbourne: National Asthma Council Australia; September 2025. Diagnosing asthma in children 1 to 5 years and 6 to 11 years; Treatment levels and Initial asthma treatment for both age groups; tables of inhaled corticosteroid doses, alternative diagnoses and probability features.","url":"https://www.asthmahandbook.org.au"},{"n":2,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Asthma diagnosis: children 6 years and older, adolescents and adults. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Published December 2020 (under review, June 2026).","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"ages","h":"Three age bands","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Age","How asthma is confirmed","Notes"],"r":[["Under 12 months","It is not diagnosed","Wheeze is usually bronchiolitis. Refer persistent wheeze. No asthma treatment"],["1 to 5 years","Signs that clear within minutes of salbutamol, then a trial of treatment","Lung function testing is not possible"],["6 to 11 years","Spirometry with bronchodilator, or FeNO 25 ppb or more","Peak flow is not used to diagnose"]]}},{"key":"The younger the child, the more the diagnosis rests on watching what salbutamol does."}]},{"id":"history","h":"History and examination","body":[{"ul":["Ask: wheeze, hard breathing, chest tightness or cough, and how often by day and by night. [1]","Ask whether symptoms come only with colds, or also between them. [1]","Describe increased work of breathing to parents: sucking in under the ribs or at the neck. [1]","Ask about smoke and vape exposure, pets, eczema, hay fever, food allergy and family history. [1]","Ask about prematurity, neonatal breathing problems and hospital stays for bronchiolitis. [1]","Examine: growth on a centile chart, chest shape, clubbing, eczema and signs of rhinitis. [1]"]},{"tb":{"h":["Asthma more likely","Asthma less likely"],"r":[["More than one typical symptom, and often","Isolated cough with no wheeze or hard breathing"],["Worse at night and early morning","A moist cough"],["Set off by exercise, pets, cold air, laughing or emotion","Noisy breathing in during exercise"],["Symptoms between colds as well","Symptoms only with colds, never between"],["Atopy in the child or family","Wheeze in one spot; normal chest when symptomatic"],["Clear response to reliever","No response to an adequate trial"]]}},{"p":"Wheeze only with colds makes asthma less likely in a preschooler but does not rule it out. Viral infections are the commonest trigger of severe attacks at every age. [1]"}]},{"id":"redflags","h":"Clues to another diagnosis","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Finding","Consider"],"r":[["Symptoms from birth","Cystic fibrosis, airway malformation, primary ciliary dyskinesia"],["Sudden-onset cough; wheeze on one side","Inhaled foreign body: urgent"],["Persistent wet cough","Protracted bacterial bronchitis, bronchiectasis, cystic fibrosis"],["Dry cough in the daytime only","Habit (somatic) cough"],["Coughing blood","Urgent referral"],["Clubbing, nasal polyps, poor growth","Cystic fibrosis, immunodeficiency"],["Stridor","Croup, laryngeal obstruction"],["Wheeze that never varies","Structural airway problem"],["Heart murmur","Congenital heart disease"]]}},{"key":"One-sided wheeze or coughing blood in a child is referred urgently."}]},{"id":"young","h":"Ages 1 to 5: confirming and starting treatment","body":[{"ul":["Confirm a response: signs settle within minutes of salbutamol by spacer, seen by parents, the practice or an emergency department. [1]","Reliever: salbutamol 2 puffs by spacer, with a mask if needed. Give 2 more if not settled in a few minutes. [1]","Unclear diagnosis with significant symptoms: refer. [1]"]},{"p":"Start a daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid, with salbutamol as needed, if any of these apply. [1]"},{"tb":{"h":["Indication","Threshold"],"r":[["Frequent symptoms","Daytime twice a week or more, or night-time twice a month or more"],["Restricted life","Activity or sleep limited when the child is otherwise well"],["Flare-ups","Two or more treated with oral corticosteroids"]]}},{"ul":["Dose: fluticasone propionate 50 micrograms twice daily. Higher doses are for specialists. [1]","Review at about 3 months. [1]","Good response: consider a pause to see whether symptoms return. Some children outgrow it. [1]","No response: check technique, adherence and triggers such as household smoke, then question the diagnosis. [1]"]}]},{"id":"levels","h":"Treatment levels under 12","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Level","Ages 1 to 5","Ages 6 to 11"],"r":[["1","Salbutamol as needed","Salbutamol as needed"],["2","Low-dose inhaled corticosteroid","Low-dose inhaled corticosteroid"],["3","Medium-dose inhaled corticosteroid","Low-dose corticosteroid-LABA (or medium-dose corticosteroid)"],["4","Specialist","Medium-dose corticosteroid-LABA"],["5","","Specialist"]]}},{"ul":["Ages 6 to 11, starting a preventer: same symptom thresholds, or one flare-up needing oral corticosteroids in the past 12 months. Review in 8 to 12 weeks. [1]","Reliever, ages 6 to 11: 2 to 4 puffs for cough or wheeze; 6 to 12 puffs if working hard to breathe. [1]","Low daily doses, ages 6 to 11: fluticasone propionate 100 micrograms, budesonide 100 to 200, ciclesonide 80. [1]","Montelukast: an alternative at level 2 only if parents decline a corticosteroid. Warn about mood and behaviour change. [1]","A LABA combination is an option from age 4. [1]","Every child has a written action plan, with a copy for school or day care. [1]"]},{"key":"Under 12, the reliever is still salbutamol. Budesonide-formoterol as needed starts at 12."}]},{"id":"never","h":"What not to prescribe","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Avoid","Why or instead"],"r":[["Nebulised medicines for home use","Use a spacer, with a mask for small children"],["Oral salbutamol","Not recommended"],["Theophylline","Not recommended"],["Any asthma treatment under 12 months","Refer persistent wheeze"],["Oral corticosteroids for mild episodes","Keep for severe or life-threatening attacks"]]}}]},{"id":"differ","h":"Where the sources differ","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Point","Australian Asthma Handbook 2025","Therapeutic Guidelines 2020"],"r":[["FeNO in ages 6 to 11","Used: 25 ppb or more supports asthma","A specialist test"],["Peak flow in ages 6 to 11","Not used to diagnose","A 14-day diary is an option when spirometry is non-diagnostic"]]}},{"p":"Within the Handbook, the corticosteroid-treated flare-up threshold for starting a preventer is two or more at ages 1 to 5 and one in 12 months at ages 6 to 11."}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["A 9-month-old with recurrent wheeze whose parents ask for a puffer.","A 3-year-old with night cough twice a week: does she need a preventer.","A 4-year-old who improved on a 3-month trial: what next.","A 7-year-old with a wet cough every day for six weeks.","A toddler with sudden cough and wheeze on one side.","Parents asking for a home nebuliser or a salbutamol syrup.","Choosing between salbutamol and budesonide-formoterol for an 8-year-old."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-asthma-kids-acute","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Acute asthma in children","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Severe if the child cannot finish a sentence, is using accessory muscles, or has a saturation of 94% or less.","Salbutamol by spacer: ages 1 to 5, 2 to 6 puffs (6 if severe). Ages 6 to 11, 4 to 12 puffs (12 if severe).","Severe attacks: repeat every 20 minutes for an hour and call an ambulance.","Oral corticosteroids: ages 6 to 11 unless the attack is mild; ages 1 to 5 only if going to hospital.","Prednisolone 1 mg/kg each morning for 3 days."],"curriculum":["Assess and manage acute asthma and wheeze in children in the community.","Use spacers and masks, and dose salbutamol by age.","Decide on corticosteroids, transfer and follow-up."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook, version 3.0. Melbourne: National Asthma Council Australia; September 2025. Managing acute asthma in children 1 to 5 years in primary care; Managing acute asthma in children 6 to 11 years in primary care; tables of immediate treatment and secondary severity assessment.","url":"https://www.asthmahandbook.org.au"},{"n":2,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Acute asthma. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Published December 2020 (under review, June 2026).","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"severity","h":"How sick is the child?","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Severity","Features"],"r":[["Mild to moderate (all of)","Walking or moving about; talking in sentences or phrases (a toddler vocalises); saturation over 94%"],["Severe (any of)","Cannot finish a sentence; accessory muscles, tracheal tug or recession; obvious distress; saturation 94% or less"],["Life-threatening (any of)","Drowsy, collapsed or exhausted; cyanosis; poor effort; soft or absent breath sounds; saturation under 90%"]]}},{"ul":["Assess while preparing the salbutamol. Do not delay the first dose. [1]","Slow breathing, a slow pulse or a silent chest in a tiring child means arrest is near. [1]","The life-threatening saturation cut-off is under 90% in children and under 92% in adults. [1]"]},{"key":"Recession, a sentence left unfinished, or 94% or less: this is severe."}]},{"id":"dose","h":"Salbutamol by age","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Severity","Ages 1 to 5","Ages 6 to 11"],"r":[["Mild to moderate","2 to 6 puffs","4 to 12 puffs"],["Severe","6 puffs","12 puffs"],["Life-threatening","2 x 2.5 mg nebules, continuous, with oxygen","2 x 5 mg nebules, continuous, with oxygen"],["Nebule if a spacer cannot be used","2.5 mg","5 mg"]]}},{"ul":["Device: pressurised inhaler and spacer, one puff at a time, normal breathing. Add a mask for small children. [1]","Repeat: severe attacks at least every 20 minutes for the first hour. Milder attacks every 20 to 30 minutes if needed. [1]","Not relieved within 5 minutes: repeat and arrange transfer to an emergency department. [1]","Never oral or intravenous salbutamol. [1]","High doses can cause a fast pulse, fast breathing, low potassium and acidosis. [1]","Severe attacks also get ipratropium: 4 puffs of 21 micrograms at ages 1 to 5, and 8 puffs from age 6. [2]"]},{"key":"Small child, small dose: 6 puffs is the severe dose under 6 years, 12 puffs from 6 years."}]},{"id":"oxygen","h":"Oxygen","body":[{"ul":["Start when saturation is under 92% on room air. [1]","Target 92 to 96%. [1]","Use a simple mask at 5 L/min or nasal prongs at 3 L/min. [1]"]}]},{"id":"steroids","h":"Corticosteroids","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Age","Who gets them","Dose"],"r":[["1 to 5 years","Only children who need hospital admission","Prednisolone 1 mg/kg each morning for 3 days"],["6 to 11 years","All except attacks graded mild at first assessment","Prednisolone 1 mg/kg (maximum 50 mg) each morning for 3 days"]]}},{"ul":["Why hold back in preschoolers: in mild to moderate wheeze they may give no benefit. [2]","Alternative: dexamethasone 0.6 mg/kg (maximum 16 mg), repeated the next day if needed. [2]","Start within the first hour when indicated. [2]"]},{"key":"A preschooler with mild to moderate wheeze who is going home does not need prednisolone."}]},{"id":"transfer","h":"When to call an ambulance","body":[{"ul":["Any severe or life-threatening feature: call while starting treatment. [1]","A child who cannot speak in whole sentences, or has a saturation of 94% or less, goes to an acute care facility. [1]","No relief within 5 minutes of the first dose: repeat and transfer. [1]","Sudden wheeze with hives, swelling, vomiting or collapse is anaphylaxis: intramuscular adrenaline. [2]","Hospital steps for attacks that do not respond include intravenous magnesium sulfate, which is not used under 2 years. [2]"]}]},{"id":"after","h":"After the attack","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Step","Detail"],"r":[["Observe","At least 4 hours after the breathing has settled"],["Not ready for home","Reliever still needed more often than every 4 hours"],["Follow-up","Within 3 to 5 days, then a full review about 2 weeks later"],["Written plan","Preventer dose, reliever as needed, prednisolone course, when to call an ambulance"]]}},{"ul":["Review inhaler and spacer technique, adherence and triggers, including smoke and vape exposure at home. [1,2]","Decide whether a regular preventer is now needed. Many children have only one episode. [2]","Confirmed food allergy is a risk factor for fatal asthma. [1]"]}]},{"id":"differ","h":"Where the sources differ","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Point","Australian Asthma Handbook 2025","Therapeutic Guidelines 2020"],"r":[["Corticosteroids at ages 1 to 5","Only if hospital admission is required","For severe wheeze; may be withheld in mild to moderate wheeze"],["Corticosteroids at ages 6 to 11","All except attacks graded mild","All but the mildest cases"],["Repeat dose at ages 1 to 5, mild to moderate","Its table lists 4 to 12 puffs for repeat doses","2 to 6 puffs, repeated as required"],["Ipratropium","Not on the primary care pages","Added in severe and life-threatening attacks"]]}},{"p":"This page uses 2 to 6 puffs for repeat doses at ages 1 to 5, which matches the first dose in both sources."}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["The salbutamol dose for a 3-year-old with recession and a saturation of 93%.","A 4-year-old with mild wheeze who is well after 4 puffs: prednisolone or not.","A 9-year-old with a moderate attack: which corticosteroid, what dose, how long.","A child who goes quiet and floppy during treatment.","A parent asking for salbutamol syrup.","When a child who has improved can go home."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-copd","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"COPD: diagnosis and long-term management","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["COPD is diagnosed on spirometry: post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC under 0.7.","Stopping smoking does more than any inhaler. Pulmonary rehabilitation is for everyone with symptoms.","Inhalers go in order: reliever, then one long-acting bronchodilator, then two, then add a corticosteroid.","A corticosteroid is added only for exacerbations (one hospital admission or two courses in a year) with ongoing symptoms on two bronchodilators.","Refer anyone with COPD before 40, and anyone with a resting saturation of 90% or less for oxygen assessment."],"curriculum":["Diagnose COPD and grade its severity.","Apply stepwise drug and non-drug management, including smoking cessation, rehabilitation and vaccination.","Recognise indications for referral, home oxygen and palliative care."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Handbook: summary clinical practice guidelines for the management of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. Version 1. Milton, Queensland: Lung Foundation Australia; 2024.","url":"https://lungfoundation.com.au"},{"n":2,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Stable chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Melbourne: Therapeutic Guidelines Limited; published December 2020.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"who","h":"Who to test","body":[{"ul":["Spirometry for anyone aged 35 or over with a smoking history, or another exposure, and any respiratory symptom. [1,2]","Symptoms: breathlessness, cough, sputum, wheeze or recurrent chest infections. [1,2]","Breathlessness may be the only symptom, and it creeps up over years. [2]","Other exposures: workplace dust and fumes, second-hand smoke, indoor cooking smoke, prematurity and childhood lung illness. [1,2]","About half of smokers develop some airflow limitation. [2]","Coughing blood, chest pain or weight loss need urgent investigation for another cause. [1]"]}]},{"id":"dx","h":"Diagnosis","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Test","Role"],"r":[["Spirometry after bronchodilator","Required. FEV1/FVC under 0.7 confirms COPD"],["Chest X-ray","Excludes other causes of breathlessness. Does not diagnose COPD"],["CT chest","Not needed for diagnosis. Shows emphysema and bronchiectasis"],["Lung volumes and gas transfer","Not routine. Show gas trapping and reduced transfer"],["Alpha-1 antitrypsin","Suspect deficiency when COPD starts before 40, especially with a family history"]]}},{"ul":["Symptoms or imaging alone are not enough. Repeat spirometry if the result is borderline. [1]","A large bronchodilator response (over 15% and over 400 mL) suggests asthma, alone or with COPD. [1]","Asthma with COPD needs an inhaled corticosteroid with a long-acting bronchodilator, never a LABA alone. [1]"]},{"key":"No spirometry, no diagnosis. The number is a post-bronchodilator ratio under 0.7."}]},{"id":"severity","h":"Severity","body":[{"p":"Grade severity when the person is stable, at least 4 weeks after any exacerbation. [2]"},{"tb":{"h":["Grade","FEV1 (% predicted)","Typical picture"],"r":[["Mild","60 to 80","Breathless on moderate exertion; few symptoms"],["Moderate","40 to 59","Breathless walking on the flat; exacerbations needing corticosteroids or antibiotics"],["Severe","Under 40","Breathless on minimal exertion; frequent, worsening exacerbations"]]}},{"ul":["The spirometry bands are a guide. Symptoms and exacerbation history count as much. [2]","Track symptoms with the COPD Assessment Test (CAT) and the mMRC breathlessness scale. [1,2]"]}]},{"id":"nondrug","h":"Measures for everyone","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Measure","Detail"],"r":[["Stop smoking","The single most important step. Counselling with nicotine replacement, varenicline or bupropion"],["Pulmonary rehabilitation","For anyone with symptoms. Improves exercise capacity and quality of life and reduces exacerbations"],["Physical activity","At least 150 minutes a week of moderate activity"],["Vaccination","Influenza every year, pneumococcal, and COVID-19"],["Nutrition","Review weight and diet"],["Breathlessness techniques","Leaning forward, pursed-lip breathing, a handheld fan"]]}},{"ul":["High nicotine dependence: a patch combined with a fast-acting form. [1]","Few symptoms and a CAT score under 10: these measures alone may be enough at first. [2]"]},{"key":"Smoking cessation is the single most important step. It limits lung damage and reduces deaths."}]},{"id":"steps","h":"Inhalers, step by step","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Step","Treatment"],"r":[["1","Short-acting bronchodilator as needed (salbutamol or ipratropium)"],["2","Add one long-acting bronchodilator: a LAMA or a LABA"],["3","Both together: LAMA with LABA"],["4","Add an inhaled corticosteroid (triple therapy)"]]}},{"p":"An inhaled corticosteroid is added only when both conditions are met. [1,2]"},{"tb":{"h":["Condition","Threshold"],"r":[["Exacerbations in the past 12 months","One needing hospital admission, or two treated with oral corticosteroids or antibiotics"],["Symptoms","Still significant on a LAMA with a LABA"]]}},{"ul":["Before each step: check inhaler technique and adherence. Up to 90% of people use their device incorrectly. [2]","Keep the number of device types small. [1]","Judge symptoms at about 6 weeks and exacerbations over about 6 months. [1]","Inhaled corticosteroids raise the risk of pneumonia in COPD. [2]","A response to oral corticosteroids does not predict a response to inhaled ones. [2]","Still symptomatic on triple therapy: refer. [2]"]},{"key":"Bronchodilators first in COPD. The corticosteroid is last, and only for exacerbations."}]},{"id":"avoid","h":"What not to use","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Avoid","Why"],"r":[["Inhaled corticosteroid alone","Not a COPD treatment unless asthma is also present"],["Long-term oral corticosteroids","Harm without benefit: bone loss, muscle weakness, falls"],["Theophylline","Not recommended"],["Ipratropium with a LAMA","The two are not combined"],["Oxygen for breathlessness without low oxygen","No benefit"]]}},{"ul":["Long-term low-dose macrolide: a specialist option for frequent exacerbations. [1,2]","Cardioselective beta blockers are safe in COPD when there is a cardiac indication. [1]"]}]},{"id":"oxygen","h":"Home oxygen","body":[{"ul":["Refer for assessment when resting saturation is 90% or less while stable. [1,2]","Assess at least 4 weeks after an exacerbation. An arterial blood gas on room air is required. [2]","Criteria: PaO2 consistently under 55 mmHg, or up to 59 mmHg with polycythaemia, pulmonary hypertension or right heart failure. [1]","Dose: more than 18 hours a day. It prolongs life in resting hypoxaemia. [1]","Not for current smokers, because of fire and burns. [1]","Persistent high carbon dioxide: refer for assessment for home non-invasive ventilation. [1,2]"]}]},{"id":"refer","h":"Referral and review","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Refer to a respiratory physician for","Notes"],"r":[["Uncertain diagnosis","Including possible asthma overlap"],["COPD before age 40","To test for alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency"],["Rapid fall in FEV1",""],["Frequent exacerbations or symptoms despite full treatment","Including after pulmonary rehabilitation"],["Coughing blood or other unusual symptoms",""],["Saturation 90% or less, or signs of right heart failure","Oxygen assessment"]]}},{"ul":["Review: at least yearly for mild to moderate disease and twice a year for severe disease. [2]","Spirometry every year for everyone with COPD. [2]","Each review: smoking, symptoms, exacerbations, technique, adherence, action plan, mood, weight and vaccination. [2]","Screen for heart disease, osteoporosis, anxiety, depression, lung cancer and sleep apnoea. [1]","Discuss advance care planning early, and involve palliative care for advanced disease. [1,2]"]}]},{"id":"differ","h":"Where the sources differ","body":[{"p":"The two sources agree on diagnosis, severity bands, the order of inhalers and the threshold for adding an inhaled corticosteroid. The Therapeutic Guidelines topic draws on an earlier version of COPD-X."},{"tb":{"h":["Point","COPD-X Handbook 2024","Therapeutic Guidelines 2020"],"r":[["When to judge a change","Symptoms at 6 weeks; exacerbations at 6 months","Review 3 to 6 months after any change"],["Blood eosinophils to guide corticosteroids","Addressed in the full plan; no cut-off in the handbook summary read","Not mentioned in the sections read"]]}}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["A 58-year-old smoker with a morning cough and a normal chest X-ray: the next test.","Reading a post-bronchodilator ratio of 0.64 with an FEV1 of 52%.","Breathless on a LAMA alone, no exacerbations: the next inhaler.","Two prednisolone courses this year on LAMA with LABA.","A 38-year-old non-smoker with emphysema.","Resting saturation of 88% at a routine review.","The intervention with the greatest effect on survival."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-copd-exac","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"COPD exacerbations","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["More breathless, more cough or more sputum than the usual day-to-day variation is an exacerbation.","First: more reliever, up to 8 puffs of salbutamol by spacer. Needing it more than every 3 hours means review.","Next: prednisolone 30 to 50 mg daily for 5 days, with no taper.","Antibiotics only for more purulent sputum together with more sputum or more breathlessness: amoxicillin or doxycycline for 5 days.","Oxygen is titrated to 88 to 92%. Nebulisers are driven by air."],"curriculum":["Recognise and manage a COPD exacerbation in the community.","Use antibiotics and corticosteroids appropriately.","Identify who needs hospital, and arrange care after an exacerbation."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) exacerbations. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Melbourne: Therapeutic Guidelines Limited.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"},{"n":2,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Antibiotic management of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) exacerbations. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic. Published December 2025.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"},{"n":3,"cite":"Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Handbook: summary clinical practice guidelines for the management of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. Version 1. 2024.","url":"https://lungfoundation.com.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"recognise","h":"Recognising an exacerbation","body":[{"ul":["Definition: a sudden worsening of breathlessness, cough or sputum beyond normal day-to-day variation. [1,3]","Other features: less exercise tolerance, fast breathing, fever, new ankle swelling. [1]","Triggers: viral and bacterial infection, air pollution, heart failure and pulmonary embolism. [1]","Viruses are common triggers. Consider a swab for influenza, RSV and COVID-19. [2]"]},{"tb":{"h":["Other diagnoses to consider","Note"],"r":[["Pneumonia","Fast breathing at rest, fast pulse, rigors, crackles that do not clear with coughing: get a chest X-ray"],["Heart failure, ischaemia, arrhythmia","Can cause the same breathlessness, fatigue and chest discomfort"],["Pulmonary embolism, pneumothorax, pleural effusion","On the list to exclude when the picture does not fit"]]}},{"key":"Not every breathless patient with COPD is having an exacerbation. Think heart, clot and pneumonia."}]},{"id":"treat","h":"Treatment at home or in the practice","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Step","Treatment","Notes"],"r":[["1","Salbutamol up to 8 puffs through a spacer, repeated as needed","A spacer is as effective as a nebuliser for most"],["2","Prednisolone 30 to 50 mg daily for 5 days","If the reliever is not enough. Stop without a taper"],["3","Antibiotic for 5 days","Only if the criteria below are met"]]}},{"ul":["Reliever needed more often than every 3 hours, or symptoms uncontrolled: medical review. [1]","Ipratropium (up to 4 puffs) is an alternative reliever, but not for people taking a LAMA. [1]","If a nebuliser is used, drive it with air, not oxygen. [1]","Repeated high doses of salbutamol can lower potassium and affect heart rhythm. [1]","Chest X-ray and sputum culture are not routine in the community. [2,3]","Review at about 5 days. Still unwell: reassess. Worse: hospital. [3]"]},{"key":"Five days of prednisolone, then stop. No taper."}]},{"id":"abx","h":"Antibiotics","body":[{"p":"Antibiotics are not routine. Most community exacerbations can be managed without them. [2]"},{"tb":{"h":["Situation","Antibiotic?"],"r":[["Sputum more purulent, plus more sputum or more breathlessness","Consider"],["Needs intensive care or ventilation, including non-invasive","Give"],["Less severe exacerbation managed in the community","Withhold"],["Low C-reactive protein","Supports withholding"]]}},{"tb":{"h":["Choice","Dose","Duration"],"r":[["Amoxicillin","500 mg every 8 hours, or 1 g every 12 hours","5 days"],["Doxycycline","100 mg daily","5 days"]]}},{"ul":["Not first line: amoxicillin with clavulanate, macrolides and cephalosporins. They work no better and do more harm. [2]","Doses are lower than for pneumonia. The aim is faster recovery, not clearing colonisation. [2]","Oral treatment unless the person cannot swallow or absorb. [2]"]},{"key":"Purulent sputum is the trigger for antibiotics. Breathlessness alone is not."}]},{"id":"slow","h":"Not improving","body":[{"ul":["Symptoms last 7 to 10 days in mild COPD and can last weeks in more severe disease. [2]","Slow recovery rarely needs a broader antibiotic. [2]","First check that bronchodilator and corticosteroid treatment are adequate. [2]","Then look again for a virus, pneumonia or heart failure. [2]","Severe COPD with no response at 48 to 72 hours and likely bacterial cause: amoxicillin with clavulanate 875+125 mg twice daily for 5 days. [2]","Pseudomonas in sputum now or within 3 months: ciprofloxacin 750 mg twice daily for 5 days. [2]"]},{"tb":{"h":["Sputum culture is considered when","Detail"],"r":[["Severe obstruction","FEV1 under 50% predicted"],["Frequent exacerbations","Two or more a year"],["Hospital","Current admission or one in the past 3 months"],["Pseudomonas","Isolated or colonised in the past 3 months"],["Home oxygen",""]]}},{"p":"A positive culture does not prove infection. Many people with COPD carry Haemophilus, Moraxella or pneumococcus all the time. [2]"}]},{"id":"hospital","h":"Who needs hospital","body":[{"p":"Many exacerbations are managed at home. These features point to hospital. [1,3]"},{"tb":{"h":["Domain","Indication"],"r":[["Response","Not improving with treatment at home"],["Function","Cannot walk between rooms, eat or sleep because of breathlessness; cannot cope at home"],["Oxygen","Saturation under 92% if not on home oxygen, or worse than usual; new or worse cyanosis"],["Brain","Confusion or drowsiness, which suggests carbon dioxide retention"],["Heart","New ankle swelling, new arrhythmia, chest pain, unstable blood pressure"],["Other illness","Pneumonia or a worsening comorbidity"]]}},{"p":"If there is an advance care plan, the person's wishes about hospital are taken into account. [1]"}]},{"id":"oxygen","h":"Oxygen and ventilation","body":[{"ul":["Give oxygen when saturation is under 88%, and titrate to 88 to 92%. [1]","Delivery: nasal prongs at 0.5 to 2 L/min. [3]","Too much oxygen raises carbon dioxide and increases deaths. [1]","Non-invasive ventilation: for PaCO2 over 45 mmHg with pH under 7.35 despite optimal treatment. [1,3]","Signs of respiratory failure: confusion, drowsiness, restlessness, cyanosis. [3]"]},{"key":"In COPD the oxygen target is 88 to 92%, not 'as high as possible'."}]},{"id":"after","h":"After the exacerbation","body":[{"ul":["GP review within 7 days of leaving hospital. [1,3]","Check technique and adherence, and reconsider the level of maintenance treatment. [1]","Update the written action plan and make sure it is understood. [1]","Refer to pulmonary rehabilitation. After an admission it can start within 2 to 4 weeks of discharge. [1,3]","Check vaccinations and smoking status. [3]","A hospital admission is a prompt to discuss advance care planning. [1]","Two exacerbations in 12 months needing corticosteroids or antibiotics: review earlier and step up. [3]","Frequent exacerbations despite full treatment: refer to consider a long-term macrolide. [2]"]},{"p":"Some people hold a prednisolone and antibiotic supply at home under their action plan, with instructions to arrange early review. [3]"}]},{"id":"differ","h":"Where the sources differ","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Point","Therapeutic Guidelines","COPD-X Handbook 2024"],"r":[["Antibiotic trigger","More purulent sputum plus more volume or more breathlessness","Fever, or a change in sputum colour or volume"],["Start oxygen","Saturation under 88%","Hospital if under 92% and not on home oxygen; target 88 to 92%"],["Salbutamol at home","Up to 8 puffs, repeated as needed","4 to 8 puffs every 3 to 4 hours"]]}},{"p":"Questions follow the Therapeutic Guidelines antibiotic criteria, which were updated in December 2025."}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["More breathless for two days with white sputum: antibiotics or not.","Green sputum and more of it: which antibiotic, what dose, how long.","How long to give prednisolone and whether to taper.","A drowsy patient on 6 litres of oxygen with a saturation of 99%.","Setting up a nebuliser for a patient with COPD in the treatment room.","Not better after three days of amoxicillin.","Deciding between home and hospital."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-cap","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Community-acquired pneumonia in adults","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Most coughs with fever are not pneumonia. Look for a fast pulse, fast breathing, low saturation and focal chest signs.","Any of these means possible admission: respiratory rate 22 or more, pulse over 100, systolic under 90, new confusion, saturation under 92%.","None of them: treat at home with amoxicillin 1 g three times a day for 5 days.","Penicillin allergy or a likely atypical organism: doxycycline 100 mg twice a day.","Review early. Fever should settle in days; cough and tiredness can last weeks."],"curriculum":["Diagnose pneumonia and distinguish it from acute bronchitis.","Assess severity and decide the place of care.","Prescribe empirical antibiotics for low-severity pneumonia, review response and prevent recurrence."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Community-acquired pneumonia (CAP) in adults. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic. Melbourne: Therapeutic Guidelines Limited; published December 2025.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"features","h":"Is it pneumonia?","body":[{"p":"Cough and fever are common and non-specific. Among people who present to primary care with respiratory symptoms, only 0.4 to 5% have pneumonia. [1]"},{"tb":{"h":["Finding","More likely pneumonia when"],"r":[["Temperature","Over 37.8 degrees"],["Respiratory rate","18 or more at rest"],["Heart rate","Over 100"],["Oxygen saturation","Under 95% on room air"],["Symptoms","Breathlessness, rigors, pleuritic chest pain"],["Chest signs","Dullness, poor air entry, bronchial breathing, crackles that do not clear with coughing"]]}},{"ul":["Older people may present with confusion, diarrhoea or headache and few chest symptoms. [1]","Acute bronchitis is the main alternative. It does not cause severe hypoxaemia and settles without antibiotics. [1]","COPD or bronchiectasis: suspect pneumonia when the person is sicker than in their usual exacerbations. [1]"]},{"key":"Coloured sputum does not make it pneumonia. Abnormal vital signs and focal chest signs do."}]},{"id":"severity","h":"Severity and where to treat","body":[{"p":"Any one of these features means close observation and possible admission. [1]"},{"tb":{"h":["Feature","Threshold"],"r":[["Respiratory rate","22 or more"],["Heart rate","Over 100"],["Systolic blood pressure","Under 90 mmHg"],["Mental state","New confusion"],["Oxygen saturation","Under 92% on room air, or below the person's usual"],["Chest X-ray, if done","More than one lobe involved"],["Lactate, if done","Over 2 mmol/L"]]}},{"tb":{"h":["Severity","Definition","Usual place of care"],"r":[["Low","None of the features above","Home, with oral antibiotics and planned review"],["Moderate","At least one feature, no red flag","Hospital"],["High","A red flag for intensive care, such as respiratory rate 30 or more, saturation under 90%, or sepsis","Intensive care"]]}},{"ul":["These features call for more support. They do not by themselves call for broader antibiotics. [1]","Also weigh: who is at home, age, comorbidities, ability to swallow tablets, and function. [1]","People can worsen over the first 24 to 48 hours. Give clear instructions on when to return. [1]","Check for an advance care plan and the person's goals before arranging admission. [1]"]},{"key":"No admission feature means low severity and treatment at home."}]},{"id":"tests","h":"Investigations","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Severity","Do","Consider"],"r":[["Low","Oxygen saturation on room air","Chest X-ray; viral PCR swab"],["Moderate","Saturation, chest X-ray, blood cultures","Viral PCR; sputum culture"],["High","All of the above plus arterial gas and sputum culture","Urinary antigens for pneumococcus and Legionella"]]}},{"p":"New consolidation on a chest X-ray usually confirms the diagnosis. In low-severity disease, imaging is done when one of these applies. [1]"},{"ul":["The diagnosis is unclear, such as unexplained hypoxia or marked breathlessness. [1]","Underlying lung disease, immune compromise or advanced age. [1]","No improvement on treatment. [1]","A complication is suspected: effusion, empyema or abscess. [1]"]}]},{"id":"treat","h":"Antibiotics for low-severity pneumonia","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Situation","Antibiotic","Duration"],"r":[["Most adults","Amoxicillin 1 g every 8 hours","5 days"],["Atypical organism suspected","Doxycycline 100 mg every 12 hours, or clarithromycin 500 mg every 12 hours","5 days"],["Penicillin hypersensitivity","Doxycycline 100 mg every 12 hours, or clarithromycin 500 mg every 12 hours","5 days"],["Significant comorbidity and follow-up uncertain","Amoxicillin plus doxycycline or clarithromycin","5 days"]]}},{"ul":["Why amoxicillin: it targets pneumococcus, the commonest bacterial cause. Resistance to tetracyclines and macrolides is rising. [1]","One drug is enough for most. Two drugs from the start only for frailty or advanced organ disease when review may not happen. [1]","Combination with non-severe penicillin hypersensitivity: cefuroxime 500 mg every 12 hours replaces amoxicillin. [1]","COPD, bronchiectasis and tropical residence do not change the choice in low-severity disease. [1]","Influenza circulating and high risk of complications: consider adding an antiviral. [1]","The dose is higher than for a COPD exacerbation, where amoxicillin is 500 mg every 8 hours. [1]"]},{"key":"Amoxicillin 1 g three times a day for 5 days. Doxycycline if allergic or atypical."}]},{"id":"review","h":"Review and duration","body":[{"tb":{"h":["When","What to expect or do"],"r":[["First few days","Fever settles and appetite returns"],["Day 3","If stable (normal pulse, breathing rate, saturation and temperature), consider stopping antibiotics"],["Day 5","Usual end of treatment. Not stable by now: higher risk, so reassess and consider admission"],["Weeks","Cough, sputum and breathlessness can linger"],["Months","Tiredness can persist"]]}},{"ul":["Lingering symptoms are not a reason to extend antibiotics. [1]","A virus is confirmed as the cause: stop the antibiotic. [1]","Not improving on one drug: review the diagnosis first. If still pneumonia, consider adding the second drug and reassess for admission. [1]"]},{"key":"Five days is the course. A cough that lasts three weeks is expected, not a treatment failure."}]},{"id":"notbetter","h":"Not improving","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Think of","Examples"],"r":[["A complication","Lung abscess, parapneumonic effusion, empyema: persistent fever or chest discomfort"],["Another diagnosis","Heart failure, COPD exacerbation, pulmonary embolism, cancer, interstitial lung disease"],["An unusual organism","Viruses, Legionella, Mycoplasma, anaerobes (poor dentition, foul sputum), Pseudomonas"],["An immune problem","Test for HIV"]]}},{"ul":["Repeat or arrange a chest X-ray if none was done. [1]","In tropical northern Australia, consider melioidosis (Burkholderia pseudomallei), especially with risk factors. [1]","Seek specialist advice if the cause remains unclear. [1]"]}]},{"id":"prevent","h":"Preventing the next one","body":[{"ul":["Vaccinate: pneumococcal, influenza and COVID-19. [1]","Stop smoking. [1]","Exercise, nutrition and weight management. [1]","Review medicines linked to pneumonia: proton pump inhibitors, benzodiazepines and other sedatives. [1]","Assess and reduce aspiration risk. [1]"]}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["Cough and green sputum with normal observations and a clear chest: antibiotics or not.","Choosing home or hospital from a set of observations.","The antibiotic, dose and duration for a well 45-year-old with lobar consolidation.","The same patient with a penicillin allergy.","Still coughing at three weeks but otherwise well.","Fever persisting on day 5 of amoxicillin.","What to review after recovery, including a long-term proton pump inhibitor."]}]}]}],"cards":[{"id":"rs1-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Asthma diagnosis needs variable symptoms, no better explanation, and {{c1::objective evidence}}.","e":"Variable expiratory airflow limitation on testing, or a raised FeNO. Symptoms alone are not enough.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#criteria","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"The two symptoms most reliably linked to asthma are wheeze and {{c1::breathlessness}}.","e":"Cough and chest tightness count, but are less specific.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#criteria","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Does a normal chest examination exclude asthma? {{c1::No}}","e":"The examination is usually normal between episodes.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#criteria","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"A positive bronchodilator response is an FEV1 rise of at least {{c1::12%}} and at least 200 mL.","e":"Both parts are required. 9% with 250 mL is negative.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#spirometry","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"A positive bronchodilator response is an FEV1 rise of at least 12% and at least {{c1::200 mL}}.","e":"A rise over 400 mL strongly supports asthma.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#spirometry","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Post-bronchodilator FEV1 is measured {{c1::10 to 15 minutes}} after salbutamol.","e":"Salbutamol 200 to 400 micrograms; at least three blows before and after.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#spirometry","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"On spirometry, expiratory airflow limitation is shown by a reduced {{c1::FEV1/FVC ratio}}.","e":"A low FEV1 alone is non-specific.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#spirometry","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Spirometry is normal while the person is well. Is asthma excluded? {{c1::No}}","e":"Repeat when symptomatic or in the early morning.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#spirometry","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Spirometry is normal while the person is breathless and wheezy. This points to {{c1::another diagnosis}}.","e":"Contrast: normal spirometry on a well day excludes nothing.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#spirometry","src":"eTG Respiratory: Asthma diagnosis, 6 years and older (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs1-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Ideally, spirometry is done {{c1::before::before or after}} a regular preventer is started.","e":"Treatment makes variable airflow harder to show. If symptoms are significant, treat now and test as soon as possible.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#spirometry","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"In adults, a FeNO of {{c1::40 ppb}} or more supports asthma.","e":"Only when the clinical picture fits. A normal FeNO does not exclude asthma.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#next","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"FeNO is a measure of {{c1::airway inflammation}}.","e":"Fractional exhaled nitric oxide. Added to the diagnostic work-up in the 2025 Handbook.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#next","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"A peak flow diary supports asthma when average daily variation exceeds {{c1::10%}}.","e":"Morning and about 4 pm, best of three, for 2 to 4 weeks. Variation = (highest - lowest) / mean.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#next","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"A diagnostic peak flow diary runs for {{c1::2 to 4 weeks}}.","e":"Used when spirometry cannot be done in a reasonable time. Same meter throughout.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#next","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Bronchial provocation testing is contraindicated when FEV1 is under {{c1::60%}} of predicted.","e":"The other contraindication is pregnancy.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#next","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Suspected asthma in pregnancy with non-diagnostic spirometry: the test to avoid is {{c1::bronchial provocation}}.","e":"Options are repeat spirometry, laboratory spirometry with FeNO, or referral.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#next","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Before bronchial provocation, salbutamol is withheld for at least {{c1::4 hours}}.","e":"Formoterol and salmeterol: 24 hours. Laboratories set their own rules.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#next","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"A treatment trial with inhaled corticosteroid is judged after at least {{c1::4 weeks}}.","e":"Clear improvement supports asthma. No improvement: look for another diagnosis.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#trial","src":"eTG Respiratory: Asthma diagnosis, 6 years and older (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs1-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Strong suspicion of asthma, normal spirometry, repeat testing not possible: the fallback is a {{c1::treatment trial}}.","e":"Also used when a person cannot perform spirometry.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#trial","src":"eTG Respiratory: Asthma diagnosis, 6 years and older (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs1-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Is a chest X-ray needed to diagnose asthma? {{c1::No}}","e":"Reserve imaging for unusual features or a suspected alternative diagnosis.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#nottests","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Does allergy testing confirm or exclude asthma? {{c1::No}}","e":"It only guides management of triggers.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#nottests","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Pack-years = years smoked x cigarettes per day, divided by {{c1::20}}.","e":"More than 20 pack-years lowers the probability of asthma and raises that of COPD.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#history","src":"eTG Respiratory: Asthma diagnosis, 6 years and older (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs1-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Throat tightness, voice change and symptoms worse with talking suggest {{c1::inducible laryngeal obstruction::diagnosis}}.","e":"Also called vocal cord dysfunction. Spirometry is normal during symptoms.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#mimics","src":"eTG Respiratory: Asthma diagnosis, 6 years and older (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs1-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Breathlessness with dizziness and tingling fingers suggests {{c1::dysfunctional breathing::diagnosis}}.","e":"Hyperventilation. These features lower the probability of asthma.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#mimics","src":"eTG Respiratory: Asthma diagnosis, 6 years and older (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs1-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"New asthma symptoms that are related to work: the next step is {{c1::immediate specialist referral}}.","e":"Occupational asthma can cause rapid, irreversible loss of lung function.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#mimics","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-26","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Wheeze after aspirin or NSAIDs suggests {{c1::aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease::diagnosis}}.","e":"Refer. An aspirin challenge is done only in a specialist setting.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#mimics","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-27","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Asthma with COPD features must be treated with an {{c1::inhaled corticosteroid::drug class}}.","e":"Long-acting bronchodilators alone carry higher risk when asthma is present.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#mimics","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-28","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Unilateral wheeze in a child suggests {{c1::inhaled foreign body::diagnosis}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#mimics","src":"eTG Respiratory: Asthma diagnosis, 6 years and older (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs1-29","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Suspected asthma in an Aboriginal adult from a remote community: the coexisting diagnosis to consider is {{c1::bronchiectasis::diagnosis}}.","e":"Needs high-resolution CT.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#mimics","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs2-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"FEV1 is the volume breathed out in the {{c1::first second}} of a forced breath.","e":"","nt":"rs-lungfn#measures","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"Spirometry reference values are matched for age, sex, ethnicity and {{c1::height}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-lungfn#measures","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"A low FEV1/FVC ratio indicates an {{c1::obstructive::obstructive or restrictive}} defect.","e":"Asthma, COPD, or both. The expiratory loop is scooped.","nt":"rs-lungfn#patterns","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"A low FVC with a normal or raised FEV1/FVC ratio indicates a {{c1::restrictive::obstructive or restrictive}} defect.","e":"Interstitial lung disease, muscle weakness, kyphoscoliosis, obesity.","nt":"rs-lungfn#patterns","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"A concave (scooped) expiratory flow-volume loop indicates {{c1::airflow obstruction}}.","e":"A normal-shaped but small loop suggests restriction.","nt":"rs-lungfn#patterns","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"A low ratio with a low FVC suggests mixed disease or {{c1::gas trapping}}.","e":"Static lung volumes separate the two.","nt":"rs-lungfn#patterns","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"Suspected restriction on spirometry is assessed further with {{c1::static lung volumes::test}}.","e":"Laboratory test. Also shows hyperinflation.","nt":"rs-lungfn#patterns","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"After a heart attack, spirometry is deferred for {{c1::1 week}}.","e":"A relative contraindication: weigh risk and benefit.","nt":"rs-lungfn#contra","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"After eye, sinus or middle ear surgery, spirometry is deferred for {{c1::1 week}}.","e":"A middle ear infection in the previous week is also a reason to defer.","nt":"rs-lungfn#contra","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"After chest or abdominal surgery, spirometry is deferred for {{c1::4 weeks}}.","e":"Contrast: 1 week for eye, sinus and ear surgery.","nt":"rs-lungfn#contra","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"Is current haemoptysis a reason to defer spirometry? {{c1::Yes}}","e":"As are a current or recent pneumothorax and concussion with ongoing symptoms.","nt":"rs-lungfn#contra","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"A patient who must have spirometry despite a relative contraindication is tested in a {{c1::respiratory laboratory}}.","e":"Experienced operator with emergency care available. Name the concern on the referral.","nt":"rs-lungfn#contra","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"Is a peak flow meter useful for monitoring COPD? {{c1::No}}","e":"The obstruction in COPD does not vary.","nt":"rs-lungfn#pef","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"Can a peak flow meter replace spirometry for diagnosing asthma? {{c1::No}}","e":"It cannot show reversibility or a reduced FEV1/FVC ratio.","nt":"rs-lungfn#pef","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"Serial peak flow readings must use the same {{c1::meter}}.","e":"Readings vary widely between devices.","nt":"rs-lungfn#pef","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"In carbon monoxide poisoning, pulse oximetry reads falsely {{c1::high::high or low}}.","e":"Carboxyhaemoglobin is read as oxyhaemoglobin. Heavy smoking has a smaller effect of the same kind.","nt":"rs-lungfn#oximetry","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"A pulse oximeter gives no information about {{c1::carbon dioxide}}.","e":"It measures saturation, not ventilation. Hypercapnia needs an arterial gas.","nt":"rs-lungfn#oximetry","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"Dapsone and benzocaine make oximetry unreliable by causing {{c1::methaemoglobinaemia}}.","e":"Readings may be falsely high or low. Lidocaine, nitrates and some antimalarials do the same.","nt":"rs-lungfn#oximetry","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"Nail polish is affecting an oximeter reading. The fix is to {{c1::rotate the probe}}.","e":"Side to side on the finger.","nt":"rs-lungfn#oximetry","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"In severe anaemia the oximeter reading is accurate but {{c1::falsely reassuring}}.","e":"Saturation is normal while oxygen delivery is poor. Cyanide poisoning is similar.","nt":"rs-lungfn#oximetry","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"Normal arterial PaCO2 is {{c1::35 to 45}} mmHg.","e":"","nt":"rs-lungfn#gases","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"Normal arterial pH is {{c1::7.35 to 7.45}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-lungfn#gases","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"Normal arterial PaO2 is {{c1::80 to 100}} mmHg.","e":"Varies with age and altitude.","nt":"rs-lungfn#gases","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"Normal arterial bicarbonate is {{c1::22 to 26}} mmol/L.","e":"","nt":"rs-lungfn#gases","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"A venous blood gas is abnormal in a patient with suspected respiratory failure. The next test is an {{c1::arterial blood gas::test}}.","e":"Venous values do not track arterial values reliably.","nt":"rs-lungfn#gases","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-26","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"The lung function test that measures gas exchange is {{c1::DLCO::test}}.","e":"Diffusing capacity for carbon monoxide. A baseline is useful before amiodarone, methotrexate or leflunomide.","nt":"rs-lungfn#lab","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-27","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"The laboratory test used to assess fitness to fly is the {{c1::hypoxic challenge test::test}}.","e":"Also called the high-altitude simulation test.","nt":"rs-lungfn#lab","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-28","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"Bronchial provocation testing detects {{c1::airway hyperresponsiveness}}.","e":"Beta agonists, antimuscarinics and antihistamines interfere: ask the laboratory what to withhold.","nt":"rs-lungfn#lab","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs3-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Every treatment level for asthma in people aged 12 and over contains an {{c1::inhaled corticosteroid::drug class}}.","e":"The 2025 Handbook change. Applies even when symptoms are infrequent.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#principles","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Is salbutamol alone acceptable treatment for an adult with mild, infrequent asthma? {{c1::No}}","e":"Reliever-only treatment carries a higher risk of severe flare-ups than as-needed budesonide-formoterol.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#principles","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"AIR stands for {{c1::anti-inflammatory reliever}}.","e":"A corticosteroid-formoterol inhaler taken only when symptoms occur.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#principles","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"MART stands for {{c1::maintenance and reliever therapy}}.","e":"One corticosteroid-formoterol inhaler used daily and for relief.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#principles","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Salbutamol use of {{c1::3 or more}} canisters a year signals a higher risk of severe flare-ups.","e":"12 or more a year is linked to a higher risk of asthma death.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#principles","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Salbutamol use of {{c1::12 or more}} canisters a year is linked to a higher risk of asthma death.","e":"3 or more already signals higher flare-up risk.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#principles","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"A long-acting beta agonist is never used in asthma without an {{c1::inhaled corticosteroid::drug class}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#principles","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Recommended level 1 treatment: low-dose {{c1::budesonide-formoterol::drug}} as needed.","e":"The only combination approved for as-needed use without maintenance.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#levels","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Recommended level 2 treatment is {{c1::low-dose MART}}.","e":"Alternative: daily low-dose corticosteroid-LABA plus salbutamol as needed.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#levels","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Recommended level 3 treatment is {{c1::medium-dose MART}}.","e":"More maintenance puffs of the low-dose inhaler. The reliever dose stays low.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#levels","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Besides budesonide-formoterol, the combination approved for MART is {{c1::beclometasone-formoterol::drug}}.","e":"Adults only, and not approved for as-needed use without maintenance. Fluticasone-salmeterol cannot be used as MART.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#levels","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Newly diagnosed asthma with a flare-up needing oral corticosteroids in the past year: start with {{c1::MART}}.","e":"Also for frequent or severe symptoms, or any past life-threatening episode. Everyone else starts with as-needed budesonide-formoterol.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#start","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Asthma is first diagnosed during a severe acute episode. Is maintenance treatment started straight away? {{c1::Yes}}","e":"MART, even if an oral corticosteroid course is also given.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#start","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"For predictable exercise symptoms, the usual reliever is taken {{c1::15 minutes}} before exercise.","e":"A person on budesonide-formoterol uses that. A separate salbutamol inhaler is not added.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#start","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"After starting asthma treatment, the first review is within {{c1::2 to 3 months}}.","e":"Reassess control, adherence and technique.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#start","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"For routine asthma treatment, a pressurised inhaler is used with a {{c1::spacer}} rather than a nebuliser.","e":"As effective, and faster.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#start","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 dry powder, as-needed dose: {{c1::1 inhalation}}.","e":"Up to 6 on one occasion.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#doses","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 dry powder: usual maximum of {{c1::8}} inhalations a day.","e":"Up to 12 in a day for a short period. Maintenance and reliever doses both count.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#doses","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Budesonide-formoterol 100/3 pressurised inhaler, as-needed dose: {{c1::2 inhalations}}.","e":"Usual maximum 16 a day, 24 briefly. Twice the puffs of the 200/6 dry powder inhaler.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#doses","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Low-dose MART with budesonide-formoterol 200/6: maintenance of {{c1::2 inhalations}} a day.","e":"In one dose or two. Medium-dose MART is 2 twice a day.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#doses","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"A patient on as-needed budesonide-formoterol worsens over {{c1::3 days}} despite extra doses: arrange medical review.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#doses","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"A low daily dose of budesonide is {{c1::200 to 400}} micrograms.","e":"Medium 500 to 800; high over 800.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#doses","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"A low daily dose of fluticasone propionate is {{c1::100 to 200}} micrograms.","e":"Medium 250 to 500; high over 500. Half the budesonide figures.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#doses","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Before stepping up asthma treatment, check adherence, triggers, comorbidities and {{c1::inhaler technique}}.","e":"Watch the person use the device.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#stepup","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Poor control on fixed-dose corticosteroid-LABA plus salbutamol: before raising the dose, switch to {{c1::MART}}.","e":"At the same corticosteroid dose, MART gives fewer flare-ups.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#stepup","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-26","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"On as-needed budesonide-formoterol and using it {{c1::3 or more}} times a week: add daily maintenance.","e":"That is low-dose MART, using the same inhaler.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#stepup","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-27","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Uncontrolled on medium-dose MART despite good adherence and technique: order {{c1::blood eosinophils::test}} and refer.","e":"Do not simply raise the corticosteroid dose.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#refer","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-28","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"On medium-dose inhaled corticosteroid, blood eosinophils of {{c1::150}} per microlitre or more suggest persistent type 2 inflammation.","e":"These patients may benefit from a monoclonal antibody.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#refer","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-29","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Blood eosinophils of {{c1::1,500}} per microlitre or more in asthma need urgent specialist assessment.","e":"Looks for other disease, such as allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#refer","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-30","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Asthma treatment can be stepped down after good control for {{c1::2 to 3 months}}.","e":"And low flare-up risk. Then review in 4 to 8 weeks.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#stepdown","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-31","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"After stepping asthma treatment down, review in {{c1::4 to 8 weeks}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#stepdown","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-32","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"The lowest acceptable asthma treatment for an adult is {{c1::as-needed budesonide-formoterol}}.","e":"The inhaled corticosteroid is never withdrawn completely.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#stepdown","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-33","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Is pregnancy a time to step asthma treatment down? {{c1::No}}","e":"Nor during a respiratory infection, allergen season or travel.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#stepdown","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Asthma control is assessed over the previous {{c1::4 weeks}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-review#control","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Good asthma control: daytime symptoms on no more than {{c1::2 days}} a week.","e":"The same threshold applies to reliever use, not counting doses before exercise.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#control","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"One night waking with asthma in the past 4 weeks. Is control good? {{c1::No}}","e":"Any night symptom or any activity limitation means poor control.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#control","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"The strongest predictor of a future asthma flare-up is {{c1::poor symptom control}}.","e":"Next: a severe flare-up in the past year.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#risk","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"A confirmed {{c1::food allergy}} is a risk factor for asthma death, although it rarely triggers asthma.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-review#risk","src":"eTG Respiratory: Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs4-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Minimum frequency of asthma review: {{c1::once a year}}.","e":"And 2 to 3 months after any new treatment.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#review","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"In well-controlled asthma, spirometry is repeated about every {{c1::2 years}}.","e":"Also at diagnosis and whenever control is poor.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#review","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Are bronchodilators withheld before spirometry done to monitor known asthma? {{c1::No}}","e":"They are withheld for diagnostic spirometry.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#review","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"An asthma action plan is reviewed at least {{c1::yearly}} and whenever treatment changes.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-review#plan","src":"eTG Respiratory: Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs4-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Peak-flow action plans are set against the person's {{c1::personal best}}, not the predicted value.","e":"For poor perceivers, over-perceivers and people with sudden severe attacks. Most people use a symptom-based plan.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#plan","src":"eTG Respiratory: Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs4-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Worsening asthma on inhaled corticosteroid alone: the plan {{c1::quadruples}} the dose for 1 to 2 weeks.","e":"Doubling is not enough. If no response, start prednisolone.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#plan","src":"eTG Respiratory: Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs4-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Worsening asthma on a fixed corticosteroid-LABA inhaler: the practical step-up in the plan is {{c1::oral prednisolone::drug}}.","e":"Raising the inhaled dose would need a second, corticosteroid-only inhaler.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#plan","src":"eTG Respiratory: Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs4-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"An action plan states the maximum reliever dose per occasion and per {{c1::24 hours}}.","e":"Specific to that person's inhaler.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#plan","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Oral corticosteroids are started when symptoms return within {{c1::4 hours}} of the reliever.","e":"Or do not settle promptly.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#ocs","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Adult asthma flare-up: prednisolone {{c1::37.5 to 50 mg}} each morning.","e":"For 5 to 10 days, without a taper.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#ocs","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Adult asthma flare-up: prednisolone is given for {{c1::5 to 10 days}}.","e":"37.5 to 50 mg each morning.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#ocs","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Does a 7-day prednisolone course for asthma need a taper? {{c1::No}}","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-review#ocs","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Adolescent asthma flare-up: prednisolone 1 mg/kg (maximum 50 mg) daily for {{c1::3 days}}.","e":"Adults: 5 to 10 days.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#ocs","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Prednisolone for asthma flare-ups is prescribed with {{c1::no repeats::how many repeats}}.","e":"Exact quantity only, so that each course is counted.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#ocs","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"During pregnancy, about {{c1::40%}} of women with asthma have worsening symptoms.","e":"At least 20% have a flare-up needing medical care.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#pregnancy","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"A pregnant woman asks whether to stop her inhaled corticosteroid. {{c1::Continue it}}","e":"Flare-ups are linked to low birth weight and preterm birth. Step up if needed; do not step down.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#pregnancy","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Asthma flare-ups in pregnancy are linked to preterm birth and {{c1::low birth weight}}.","e":"Also small-for-dates babies.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#pregnancy","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"In an obese person with asthma, weight loss of {{c1::5 to 10%}} can improve control.","e":"Confirm asthma with lung function before escalating: obesity itself causes breathlessness.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#general","src":"eTG Respiratory: Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs4-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"A person with asthma now needs a beta blocker after a heart attack. Start it at a {{c1::low dose}} under supervision.","e":"An indication for a beta blocker is not overridden by asthma.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#general","src":"eTG Respiratory: Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs4-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Breathlessness that worsens in the minutes after stopping exercise suggests {{c1::asthma::asthma or unfitness}}.","e":"Breathlessness from poor fitness eases as soon as exercise stops.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#general","src":"eTG Respiratory: Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs4-26","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"The adverse effects to warn about with montelukast are {{c1::neuropsychiatric}}.","e":"Behaviour change, depression, suicidal thoughts. Stop the drug if they occur.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#general","src":"eTG Respiratory: Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs4-27","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"After a maintenance dose of inhaled corticosteroid, the person should {{c1::rinse and spit}}.","e":"Reduces thrush and hoarseness. Use a spacer with pressurised inhalers.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#general","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-28","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Does treating reflux reliably improve asthma control? {{c1::No}}","e":"Reflux is common in asthma, but treating it does not necessarily help the asthma.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#general","src":"eTG Respiratory: Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs5-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Asthma first aid: salbutamol {{c1::4 puffs}} through a spacer, repeated after 4 minutes.","e":"One puff at a time, 4 breaths after each. Still no better: call an ambulance and keep going.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#firstaid","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs5-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"In the 4 x 4 x 4 plan, the wait between sets of salbutamol puffs is {{c1::4 minutes}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#firstaid","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs5-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"After an asthma attack settles, reliever needed more often than every {{c1::3 to 4 hours}} means same-day medical review.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#firstaid","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs5-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Acute asthma, speaking whole sentences, saturation 96%: severity is {{c1::mild to moderate}}.","e":"All of: can walk, whole sentences in one breath, saturation over 94%.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#severity","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"In acute asthma, a room-air saturation of {{c1::94%}} or less makes the attack at least severe.","e":"Under 92% is life-threatening.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#severity","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"In acute asthma, a room-air saturation under {{c1::92%}} is a life-threatening feature.","e":"It is also the threshold for starting oxygen.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#severity","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Acute asthma with speech limited to a few words per breath: severity is {{c1::severe}}.","e":"Cannot speak at all is life-threatening.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#severity","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"In acute asthma, a heart rate of {{c1::110}} or more is a severe feature.","e":"Respiratory rate threshold: 25 or more.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#severity","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"In acute asthma, a respiratory rate of {{c1::25}} or more is a severe feature.","e":"A slow rate in a tiring patient signals exhaustion.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#severity","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"A silent chest in acute asthma means the attack is {{c1::life-threatening}}.","e":"So do drowsiness, cyanosis, poor effort and bradycardia.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#severity","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Bradycardia in a patient with acute asthma warns of imminent {{c1::respiratory arrest}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#severity","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Mild to moderate acute asthma in an adult: salbutamol {{c1::4 to 12}} puffs through a spacer.","e":"Repeat every 20 to 30 minutes in the first hour if needed.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#treat","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Severe acute asthma in an adult: salbutamol {{c1::12}} puffs through a spacer.","e":"At least every 20 minutes for the first hour, and call an ambulance.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#treat","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"In severe acute asthma, salbutamol is repeated every {{c1::20 minutes}} for the first hour.","e":"Three doses in the hour.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#treat","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Severe acute asthma and the patient cannot use a spacer: nebulised salbutamol {{c1::5 mg}}.","e":"Life-threatening: 2 x 5 mg nebules continuously, driven by oxygen.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#treat","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"The bronchodilator added to salbutamol in severe acute asthma is {{c1::ipratropium::drug}}.","e":"Adults: 8 puffs of 21 micrograms through a spacer every 20 minutes for the first hour.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#treat","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs5-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Severe asthma not responding to bronchodilators in hospital: the intravenous drug added is {{c1::magnesium sulfate::drug}}.","e":"10 mmol over 20 minutes in adults.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#treat","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs5-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Acute asthma not relieved within {{c1::5 minutes}} of the first dose: repeat it and arrange transfer.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#treat","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"In acute asthma, oxygen is started when saturation is under {{c1::92%}}.","e":"Target 92 to 96%.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#oxygen","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Oxygen saturation target in acute asthma: {{c1::92 to 96%}}.","e":"88 to 92% if at risk of carbon dioxide retention. Do not over-oxygenate.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#oxygen","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"An adult's asthma attack settles fully after one salbutamol dose in the practice. Is prednisolone still given? {{c1::Yes}}","e":"The 2025 Handbook gives systemic corticosteroids to all who present, regardless of initial severity.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#steroids","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"In acute asthma, corticosteroids are started within {{c1::1 hour}} of presentation.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#steroids","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs5-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Alternative to prednisolone in adult acute asthma: dexamethasone {{c1::16 mg}}, repeated once the next day.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#steroids","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs5-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Sudden breathlessness with hives or facial swelling is {{c1::anaphylaxis::diagnosis}}.","e":"Give intramuscular adrenaline first.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#anaphylaxis","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs5-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Wheeze where anaphylaxis cannot be excluded: the first drug is {{c1::intramuscular adrenaline::drug}}.","e":"Suspect it with skin, gut or circulation features alongside sudden breathlessness.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#anaphylaxis","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs5-26","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"After an asthma attack settles in the practice, the patient is observed for at least {{c1::4 hours}}.","e":"In the practice or with a responsible adult who can give salbutamol and call an ambulance.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#after","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-27","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"A patient is not sent home after acute asthma while reliever is needed more often than every {{c1::4 hours}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#after","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs5-28","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"First follow-up after an acute asthma episode is within {{c1::3 to 5 days}}.","e":"Then a full asthma review about 2 weeks later.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#after","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-29","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"After an acute attack, the maintenance inhaled corticosteroid dose should be at least {{c1::medium}}.","e":"As a corticosteroid-LABA, with the reliever used as needed, not by the clock.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#after","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-30","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Near-fatal asthma needs specialist follow-up for {{c1::life}}.","e":"After any severe attack: at least a year.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#after","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs5-31","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"The marker of thunderstorm asthma risk in a person without asthma is {{c1::springtime hay fever}}.","e":"Ryegrass pollen allergy.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#storm","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-32","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"The treatment that protects against severe thunderstorm asthma is an {{c1::inhaled corticosteroid::drug class}}.","e":"Highest risk: asthma, grass pollen allergy, and no preventer.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#storm","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-33","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"The grass most often implicated in thunderstorm asthma is {{c1::ryegrass}}.","e":"Storms fragment the pollen into particles small enough to reach the small airways.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#storm","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-34","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Thunderstorm asthma exposure is greatest in the {{c1::wind gusts}} just before the rain.","e":"Stay indoors with windows shut; turn off evaporative coolers.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#storm","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs6-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Asthma is not diagnosed in children younger than {{c1::12 months}}.","e":"Wheeze at this age is usually bronchiolitis. Persistent wheeze is referred.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#ages","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Persistent wheeze in a 9-month-old: the next step is {{c1::specialist referral::next step}}.","e":"No asthma diagnosis and no asthma treatment under 12 months.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#ages","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"In a child aged 1 to 5, the evidence for asthma is signs that clear within minutes of {{c1::salbutamol}}.","e":"Lung function testing is not possible at this age.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#ages","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"In children aged 6 to 11, a FeNO of {{c1::25 ppb}} or more supports asthma.","e":"Adults: 40 ppb.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#ages","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Is peak flow used to diagnose asthma in a child aged 6 to 11? {{c1::No}}","e":"Spirometry with bronchodilator, with FeNO if done in a laboratory.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#ages","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"A 7-year-old with suspected asthma and normal spirometry and FeNO: the next step is {{c1::specialist referral::next step}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#ages","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"In a preschooler, wheeze only with colds makes asthma {{c1::less likely::more or less}}.","e":"It does not rule asthma out. Viral infection is the commonest trigger of severe attacks at every age.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#history","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"A child's chronic cough is moist. This makes asthma {{c1::less likely::more or less}}.","e":"Think protracted bacterial bronchitis.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#history","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Sudden-onset cough with wheeze on one side in a toddler suggests {{c1::inhaled foreign body::diagnosis}}.","e":"Urgent referral.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#redflags","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"A child with a persistent wet cough and no wheeze most likely has {{c1::protracted bacterial bronchitis::diagnosis}}.","e":"Also consider bronchiectasis and cystic fibrosis.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#redflags","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"A child's dry cough occurs in the daytime only and stops in sleep. This suggests {{c1::habit cough::diagnosis}}.","e":"Also called somatic cough.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#redflags","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"A wheezy child with nasal polyps and poor growth should be tested for {{c1::cystic fibrosis::diagnosis}}.","e":"Clubbing points the same way.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#redflags","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Wheeze in a child that never varies suggests a {{c1::structural airway problem}}.","e":"Asthma wheeze comes and goes.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#redflags","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Usual reliever dose for a child aged 1 to 5: salbutamol {{c1::2 puffs}} by spacer.","e":"With a mask if needed. Two more if not settled in a few minutes.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#young","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"In a child under 12, daytime asthma symptoms {{c1::twice a week}} or more are an indication for a preventer.","e":"Or night symptoms twice a month or more, or activity or sleep restricted.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#young","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"In a child under 12, night-time asthma symptoms {{c1::twice a month}} or more are an indication for a preventer.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#young","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Low-dose inhaled corticosteroid for a child aged 1 to 5: fluticasone propionate {{c1::50 micrograms}} twice daily.","e":"Higher doses only under a specialist.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#young","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"A preventer trial in a child aged 1 to 5 is reviewed at about {{c1::3 months}}.","e":"Ages 6 to 11: 8 to 12 weeks.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#young","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"A 4-year-old has done well on a 3-month inhaled corticosteroid trial. Consider {{c1::pausing treatment}} to see if symptoms return.","e":"Remission occurs in some children.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#young","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Level 1 asthma treatment for a child under 12 is {{c1::salbutamol as needed}}.","e":"Budesonide-formoterol as needed is for ages 12 and over.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#levels","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Level 2 asthma treatment for a child under 12 is a low-dose {{c1::inhaled corticosteroid::drug class}}.","e":"Plus salbutamol as needed.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#levels","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Level 3 for a child aged 6 to 11: low-dose {{c1::corticosteroid-LABA}}.","e":"Alternative: medium-dose corticosteroid alone. At ages 1 to 5, level 3 is medium-dose corticosteroid.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#levels","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"A LABA combination can be used in asthma from age {{c1::4 years}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#levels","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Montelukast at level 2 in children is reserved for when parents {{c1::decline corticosteroids}}.","e":"Warn about mood and behaviour change. Switch to an inhaled corticosteroid when possible.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#levels","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Reliever for a child aged 6 to 11 who is working hard to breathe: salbutamol {{c1::6 to 12}} puffs.","e":"For cough or wheeze without increased effort: 2 to 4 puffs.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#levels","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-26","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"A copy of a child's asthma action plan goes to the {{c1::school or day care}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#levels","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-27","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Should a home nebuliser be prescribed for a child with asthma? {{c1::No}}","e":"Use a spacer, with a mask for small children.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#never","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-28","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Should oral salbutamol be prescribed for a child with asthma? {{c1::No}}","e":"Nor theophylline.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#never","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs7-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"In a child with acute asthma, a saturation of {{c1::94%}} or less makes the attack severe.","e":"Same threshold as adults.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#severity","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"In a child with acute asthma, a saturation under {{c1::90%}} is life-threatening.","e":"Adults: under 92%.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#severity","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Subcostal recession in a child with acute asthma makes the attack {{c1::severe}}.","e":"So does tracheal tug or an unfinished sentence.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#severity","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Mild to moderate acute asthma, age 1 to 5: salbutamol {{c1::2 to 6}} puffs.","e":"By spacer, with a mask if needed.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#dose","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Severe acute asthma, age 1 to 5: salbutamol {{c1::6}} puffs.","e":"At least every 20 minutes for the first hour, and call an ambulance.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#dose","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Mild to moderate acute asthma, age 6 to 11: salbutamol {{c1::4 to 12}} puffs.","e":"The adult doses apply from age 6.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#dose","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Severe acute asthma, age 6 to 11: salbutamol {{c1::12}} puffs.","e":"Under 6 years: 6 puffs.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#dose","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Nebulised salbutamol dose for a child aged 1 to 5 who cannot use a spacer: {{c1::2.5 mg}}.","e":"From age 6: 5 mg.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#dose","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Is oral salbutamol used for acute asthma in children? {{c1::No}}","e":"Nor intravenous salbutamol in primary care.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#dose","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Repeated high doses of salbutamol can cause low serum {{c1::potassium}}.","e":"Also tachycardia, fast breathing and metabolic acidosis.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#dose","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Ipratropium dose in severe acute asthma, age 1 to 5: {{c1::4 puffs}} of 21 micrograms.","e":"From age 6: 8 puffs.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#dose","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs7-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"In a child with acute asthma, oxygen is started when saturation is under {{c1::92%}}.","e":"Target 92 to 96%.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#oxygen","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Oxygen by simple mask for a child with acute asthma runs at {{c1::5 L/min}}.","e":"Nasal prongs: 3 L/min.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#oxygen","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Acute asthma, age 1 to 5: oral corticosteroids are given only if the child needs {{c1::hospital admission}}.","e":"In mild to moderate preschool wheeze they may give no benefit.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#steroids","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Acute asthma, age 6 to 11: oral corticosteroids are given unless the attack is graded {{c1::mild}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#steroids","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Prednisolone dose for acute asthma in a child: {{c1::1 mg/kg}} each morning.","e":"Maximum 50 mg, for 3 days.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#steroids","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Prednisolone course length for acute asthma in a child: {{c1::3 days}}.","e":"Adults: 5 to 10 days.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#steroids","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Alternative to prednisolone in a child with acute asthma: dexamethasone {{c1::0.6 mg/kg}}.","e":"Maximum 16 mg, repeated the next day if needed.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#steroids","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs7-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Intravenous magnesium sulfate for acute asthma is not used under age {{c1::2 years}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#transfer","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs7-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"A child's acute asthma is not relieved within {{c1::5 minutes}} of the first dose: repeat and transfer.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#transfer","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"After a child's asthma attack settles, observe for at least {{c1::4 hours}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#after","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Follow-up after a child's asthma attack is within {{c1::3 to 5 days}}.","e":"Then a full review about 2 weeks later.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#after","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs8-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"COPD is confirmed by a post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC ratio under {{c1::0.7}}.","e":"Symptoms or a chest X-ray alone cannot make the diagnosis.","nt":"rs-copd#dx","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"The spirometry ratio that diagnoses COPD is measured {{c1::after::before or after}} a bronchodilator.","e":"","nt":"rs-copd#dx","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Case-finding spirometry for COPD starts at age {{c1::35}} in people with a smoking history and symptoms.","e":"","nt":"rs-copd#who","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Is a CT scan needed to diagnose COPD? {{c1::No}}","e":"It shows emphysema and bronchiectasis, and is ordered for red flags.","nt":"rs-copd#dx","src":"eTG Respiratory: Stable COPD (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs8-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"COPD diagnosed before age 40 suggests {{c1::alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency::diagnosis}}.","e":"Especially with a family history. Refer.","nt":"rs-copd#dx","src":"eTG Respiratory: Stable COPD (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs8-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"In COPD, an FEV1 rise over 15% and over 400 mL after a bronchodilator suggests coexisting {{c1::asthma}}.","e":"Overlap needs an inhaled corticosteroid with a long-acting bronchodilator.","nt":"rs-copd#dx","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Mild COPD: FEV1 of {{c1::60 to 80%}} predicted.","e":"Moderate 40 to 59%; severe under 40%.","nt":"rs-copd#severity","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Severe COPD: FEV1 under {{c1::40%}} predicted.","e":"Breathless on minimal exertion.","nt":"rs-copd#severity","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"COPD severity is graded at least {{c1::4 weeks}} after an exacerbation.","e":"The same wait applies before assessing for home oxygen.","nt":"rs-copd#severity","src":"eTG Respiratory: Stable COPD (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs8-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"The questionnaire used to track COPD symptoms is the {{c1::COPD Assessment Test}}.","e":"CAT. Breathlessness is graded with the mMRC scale.","nt":"rs-copd#severity","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"The single most important intervention in COPD is {{c1::smoking cessation}}.","e":"Limits lung damage and reduces deaths.","nt":"rs-copd#nondrug","src":"eTG Respiratory: Stable COPD (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs8-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"The non-drug program recommended for anyone with symptomatic COPD is {{c1::pulmonary rehabilitation}}.","e":"Improves exercise capacity and quality of life and reduces exacerbations.","nt":"rs-copd#nondrug","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Vaccines recommended in COPD: influenza, COVID-19 and {{c1::pneumococcal}}.","e":"Influenza every year.","nt":"rs-copd#nondrug","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Physical activity target in COPD: at least {{c1::150 minutes}} a week.","e":"Moderate intensity. Any activity is better than none.","nt":"rs-copd#nondrug","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Smoker with high nicotine dependence: combine a patch with a {{c1::fast-acting nicotine}} product.","e":"Gum, lozenge, spray or inhalator, with counselling.","nt":"rs-copd#nondrug","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"COPD, breathless despite a reliever alone: add a {{c1::long-acting bronchodilator::drug class}}.","e":"A LAMA or a LABA.","nt":"rs-copd#steps","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"COPD, still breathless on a LAMA alone, no exacerbations: the next step is {{c1::LAMA plus LABA}}.","e":"Not an inhaled corticosteroid.","nt":"rs-copd#steps","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"In COPD an inhaled corticosteroid is added after {{c1::2}} moderate exacerbations in 12 months.","e":"Or one needing hospital admission, and only with ongoing symptoms on LAMA plus LABA.","nt":"rs-copd#steps","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"In COPD an inhaled corticosteroid is added after {{c1::1}} exacerbation needing hospital admission.","e":"Or two moderate ones in 12 months, with ongoing symptoms on dual bronchodilators.","nt":"rs-copd#steps","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"The infection made more likely by inhaled corticosteroids in COPD is {{c1::pneumonia::diagnosis}}.","e":"Also hoarseness and oral thrush.","nt":"rs-copd#steps","src":"eTG Respiratory: Stable COPD (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs8-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Does a good response to oral prednisolone predict benefit from an inhaled corticosteroid in COPD? {{c1::No}}","e":"","nt":"rs-copd#steps","src":"eTG Respiratory: Stable COPD (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs8-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Proportion of people with COPD who use their inhaler incorrectly: up to {{c1::90%}}.","e":"Check technique at every visit and before any step up.","nt":"rs-copd#steps","src":"eTG Respiratory: Stable COPD (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs8-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Is an inhaled corticosteroid alone a treatment for COPD? {{c1::No}}","e":"Unless asthma is also present.","nt":"rs-copd#avoid","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Is theophylline recommended for COPD? {{c1::No}}","e":"","nt":"rs-copd#avoid","src":"eTG Respiratory: Stable COPD (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs8-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"A person with COPD has a heart attack. Can a cardioselective beta blocker be used? {{c1::Yes}}","e":"Safe in COPD when there is a cardiac indication.","nt":"rs-copd#avoid","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-26","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Refer for home oxygen assessment when resting saturation is {{c1::90%}} or less.","e":"When stable, at least 4 weeks after an exacerbation. An arterial gas on room air is needed.","nt":"rs-copd#oxygen","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-27","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Long-term oxygen is indicated when PaO2 is consistently under {{c1::55 mmHg}}.","e":"Or up to 59 mmHg with polycythaemia, pulmonary hypertension or right heart failure.","nt":"rs-copd#oxygen","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-28","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Long-term oxygen is used for more than {{c1::18 hours}} a day.","e":"","nt":"rs-copd#oxygen","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-29","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Home oxygen is contraindicated in people who {{c1::still smoke}}.","e":"Fire and burns.","nt":"rs-copd#oxygen","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-30","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Spirometry in established COPD is repeated every {{c1::year}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-copd#refer","src":"eTG Respiratory: Stable COPD (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs8-31","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Severe COPD is reviewed at least {{c1::twice a year}}.","e":"Mild to moderate: at least yearly.","nt":"rs-copd#refer","src":"eTG Respiratory: Stable COPD (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs9-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"COPD exacerbation, first step: salbutamol up to {{c1::8 puffs}} through a spacer.","e":"Repeated as needed.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#treat","src":"eTG Respiratory: COPD exacerbations"},{"id":"rs9-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"In a COPD exacerbation, reliever needed more often than every {{c1::3 hours}} means medical review.","e":"","nt":"rs-copd-exac#treat","src":"eTG Respiratory: COPD exacerbations"},{"id":"rs9-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"COPD exacerbation: prednisolone {{c1::30 to 50 mg}} daily.","e":"For 5 days, then stop.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#treat","src":"eTG Respiratory: COPD exacerbations"},{"id":"rs9-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"COPD exacerbation: prednisolone is given for {{c1::5 days}}.","e":"No taper. Asthma in adults: 5 to 10 days.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#treat","src":"eTG Respiratory: COPD exacerbations"},{"id":"rs9-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"Ipratropium should not be given to a person already taking a {{c1::LAMA::drug class}}.","e":"Long-acting muscarinic antagonist, such as tiotropium.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#treat","src":"eTG Respiratory: COPD exacerbations"},{"id":"rs9-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"In COPD, a nebuliser is driven by {{c1::air::air or oxygen}}.","e":"Oxygen-driven nebulisers risk dangerous over-oxygenation.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#treat","src":"eTG Respiratory: COPD exacerbations"},{"id":"rs9-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"Is sputum culture routine in a COPD exacerbation? {{c1::No}}","e":"Many patients are colonised, so a positive result does not prove infection.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#slow","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"The sputum change that prompts antibiotics in a COPD exacerbation is increased {{c1::purulence}}.","e":"Together with more sputum volume or more breathlessness.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#abx","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"COPD exacerbation with more breathlessness but unchanged white sputum: antibiotics? {{c1::No}}","e":"Bronchodilator, and prednisolone if needed.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#abx","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"First-line antibiotics for a COPD exacerbation: amoxicillin or {{c1::doxycycline::drug}}.","e":"Not amoxicillin with clavulanate, macrolides or cephalosporins.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#abx","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"Amoxicillin dose for a COPD exacerbation: {{c1::500 mg}} every 8 hours.","e":"Or 1 g every 12 hours. Five days.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#abx","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"Doxycycline dose for a COPD exacerbation: {{c1::100 mg daily}}.","e":"For 5 days.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#abx","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"Antibiotic course length for a COPD exacerbation: {{c1::5 days}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-copd-exac#abx","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"A COPD exacerbation needing non-invasive ventilation: are antibiotics given? {{c1::Yes}}","e":"They reduce treatment failure and deaths in this group.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#abx","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"In a COPD exacerbation, a low {{c1::C-reactive protein}} supports withholding antibiotics.","e":"","nt":"rs-copd-exac#abx","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"In mild COPD, exacerbation symptoms usually last {{c1::7 to 10 days}}.","e":"Weeks in moderate to severe disease. Slow recovery rarely needs a broader antibiotic.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#slow","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"Severe COPD, no response to amoxicillin at 48 to 72 hours, bacterial cause still likely: switch to {{c1::amoxicillin with clavulanate::drug}}.","e":"875+125 mg twice daily for 5 days.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#slow","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"COPD exacerbation not responding, with Pseudomonas in recent sputum: use {{c1::ciprofloxacin::drug}}.","e":"750 mg twice daily for 5 days.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#slow","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"In a COPD exacerbation, sputum culture is considered when FEV1 is under {{c1::50%}} predicted.","e":"Also with two or more exacerbations a year, recent hospital stay, Pseudomonas, or home oxygen.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#slow","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"Confusion or drowsiness during a COPD exacerbation suggests {{c1::carbon dioxide retention}}.","e":"An indication for hospital.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#hospital","src":"eTG Respiratory: COPD exacerbations"},{"id":"rs9-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"Oxygen target in a COPD exacerbation: {{c1::88 to 92%}}.","e":"Start when saturation is under 88%.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#oxygen","src":"eTG Respiratory: COPD exacerbations"},{"id":"rs9-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"Non-invasive ventilation in a COPD exacerbation: PaCO2 over 45 mmHg with pH under {{c1::7.35}}.","e":"Despite optimal treatment, including controlled oxygen.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#oxygen","src":"eTG Respiratory: COPD exacerbations"},{"id":"rs9-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"After hospital discharge for a COPD exacerbation, GP review is within {{c1::7 days}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-copd-exac#after","src":"eTG Respiratory: COPD exacerbations"},{"id":"rs9-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"After an admission for COPD, pulmonary rehabilitation can start within {{c1::2 to 4 weeks}} of discharge.","e":"","nt":"rs-copd-exac#after","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs9-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"Frequent COPD exacerbations despite full treatment: the specialist option is a long-term low-dose {{c1::macrolide::drug class}}.","e":"After excluding non-tuberculous mycobacteria. Watch hearing and the QT interval.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#after","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-26","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"A hospital admission for COPD should prompt discussion of {{c1::advance care planning}}.","e":"A sentinel event.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#after","src":"eTG Respiratory: COPD exacerbations"},{"id":"rs10-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"First-line antibiotic for low-severity pneumonia in an adult: {{c1::amoxicillin::drug}}.","e":"1 g every 8 hours for 5 days. Targets pneumococcus.","nt":"rs-cap#treat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Amoxicillin dose for low-severity pneumonia in an adult: {{c1::1 g}} every 8 hours.","e":"Twice the dose used for a COPD exacerbation.","nt":"rs-cap#treat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Usual antibiotic duration for low-severity pneumonia: {{c1::5 days}}.","e":"Consider stopping at day 3 if clinically stable.","nt":"rs-cap#review","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Low-severity pneumonia with penicillin hypersensitivity: {{c1::doxycycline::drug}} 100 mg every 12 hours.","e":"Or clarithromycin 500 mg every 12 hours. Five days.","nt":"rs-cap#treat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Doxycycline dose for pneumonia: 100 mg every {{c1::12 hours}}.","e":"For a COPD exacerbation it is once daily.","nt":"rs-cap#treat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Low-severity pneumonia where Mycoplasma is suspected: use doxycycline or {{c1::clarithromycin::drug}}.","e":"Atypical organisms are intrinsically resistant to beta-lactams.","nt":"rs-cap#treat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"The commonest bacterial cause of community-acquired pneumonia is {{c1::Streptococcus pneumoniae}}.","e":"Resistance to tetracyclines and macrolides is rising, hence amoxicillin.","nt":"rs-cap#treat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Two antibiotics from the start in low-severity pneumonia: only with significant comorbidity and uncertain {{c1::follow-up}}.","e":"Amoxicillin plus doxycycline or clarithromycin.","nt":"rs-cap#treat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"In combination treatment for pneumonia, the oral cephalosporin for non-severe penicillin hypersensitivity is {{c1::cefuroxime::drug}}.","e":"500 mg every 12 hours. Better pneumococcal activity than cefalexin.","nt":"rs-cap#treat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"In pneumonia, a respiratory rate of {{c1::22}} or more is a feature that may need admission.","e":"30 or more is a red flag for intensive care.","nt":"rs-cap#severity","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"In pneumonia, a heart rate over {{c1::100}} is a feature that may need admission.","e":"","nt":"rs-cap#severity","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"In pneumonia, a systolic blood pressure under {{c1::90 mmHg}} is a feature that may need admission.","e":"","nt":"rs-cap#severity","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"In pneumonia, a room-air saturation under {{c1::92%}} is a feature that may need admission.","e":"Or below the person's usual. Under 90% is a red flag for intensive care.","nt":"rs-cap#severity","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"New confusion in an adult with pneumonia makes the severity at least {{c1::moderate}}.","e":"One admission feature is enough.","nt":"rs-cap#severity","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Pneumonia with none of the admission features is {{c1::low severity}}.","e":"Treat at home with oral antibiotics and planned review.","nt":"rs-cap#severity","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Pneumonia can worsen over the first {{c1::24 to 48 hours}}.","e":"Plan a review and give clear return advice.","nt":"rs-cap#severity","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"The acute chest infection that settles without antibiotics and is the main alternative to pneumonia is {{c1::acute bronchitis::diagnosis}}.","e":"No severe hypoxaemia, no focal signs.","nt":"rs-cap#features","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"A resting respiratory rate of {{c1::18}} or more raises the likelihood of pneumonia.","e":"Along with temperature over 37.8, pulse over 100 and saturation under 95%.","nt":"rs-cap#features","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Crackles that {{c1::do not clear::clear or not}} with coughing suggest pneumonia.","e":"","nt":"rs-cap#features","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"In low-severity pneumonia, the one routine measurement is {{c1::oxygen saturation}}.","e":"Chest X-ray and viral PCR are considered, not mandatory.","nt":"rs-cap#tests","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"The finding that usually confirms pneumonia is new {{c1::consolidation}} on chest X-ray.","e":"PA and lateral views if possible.","nt":"rs-cap#tests","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Blood cultures are taken in pneumonia from {{c1::moderate}} severity upward.","e":"","nt":"rs-cap#tests","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"A patient is well on day 5 of treatment for pneumonia but still coughing. Extend the antibiotic? {{c1::No}}","e":"Cough can last weeks and tiredness months.","nt":"rs-cap#review","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"A viral cause is confirmed in a patient started on amoxicillin for pneumonia. The antibiotic is {{c1::stopped}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-cap#review","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Pneumonia not clinically stable by day {{c1::5}} carries a higher risk of death.","e":"Reassess severity and consider admission.","nt":"rs-cap#review","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-26","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Persistent fever and chest discomfort on treatment for pneumonia suggest {{c1::empyema::diagnosis}}.","e":"Or a parapneumonic effusion or lung abscess.","nt":"rs-cap#notbetter","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-27","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Pneumonia with poor dentition and foul-smelling sputum suggests {{c1::anaerobes}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-cap#notbetter","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-28","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Pneumonia not improving in tropical northern Australia: the organism to consider is {{c1::Burkholderia pseudomallei}}.","e":"Melioidosis.","nt":"rs-cap#notbetter","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-29","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"The acid-suppressing drug class linked to a higher incidence of pneumonia is {{c1::proton pump inhibitors::drug class}}.","e":"Also benzodiazepines and other sedatives. Review them after an episode.","nt":"rs-cap#prevent","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-30","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Vaccines reviewed after pneumonia: influenza, COVID-19 and {{c1::pneumococcal}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-cap#prevent","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"}],"questions":[{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#spirometry","s":"Callum Reid, aged 24 years, is an apprentice electrician. Over the past four months he has had episodes of wheeze and chest tightness, mostly in the early morning and after indoor soccer. Each episode settles within an hour. He had eczema as a child and his sister has asthma. He has never smoked and does not vape. Today he feels well. Spirometry was performed this morning before and after salbutamol.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Cetirizine 10 mg daily in spring","No inhalers"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Respiratory rate 14/min, SpO2 99% on room air","Chest clear, no wheeze"]}],"tb":{"h":["Spirometry","Before salbutamol","After salbutamol","Predicted"],"r":[["FEV1","4.10 L","4.22 L","4.20 L"],["FVC","5.05 L","5.10 L","5.00 L"],["FEV1/FVC","0.81","0.83",">0.75"]]},"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange a chest X-ray to look for another cause","Reassure him that normal spirometry has excluded asthma","Repeat spirometry with bronchodilator on a day when he has symptoms","Request skin prick testing to confirm allergic asthma","Start a daily preventer and review him in twelve months without further testing"],"a":2,"x":["Imaging is not part of diagnosing asthma. It is used for unusual features or when another diagnosis is suspected, and nothing here suggests one.","Normal spirometry on a well day does not exclude asthma. Airflow limitation in asthma varies, and he was tested between episodes.","Correct. His history is typical, so the test is repeated when airflow limitation is most likely to be present: during symptoms or in the early morning.","Allergy tests neither confirm nor exclude asthma. They guide trigger management once the diagnosis is made.","Treating without objective evidence makes the diagnosis harder to confirm later. This would be reasonable only as a structured trial with review at about four weeks."],"e":"Problem: a young man with a typical history, a clear chest and normal spirometry while well. Task: decide how to get objective evidence. The normal result is the distractor. What if spirometry were normal while he was wheezy and breathless? Then another diagnosis should be considered.","k":"Normal spirometry on a well day does not exclude asthma: repeat it when symptomatic.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Diagnosing asthma in adults and adolescents","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Asthma diagnosis"],"c":["rs1-04","rs1-05","rs1-06","rs1-07","rs1-08"],"id":"rs-a01","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#spirometry","s":"Priya Nair, aged 37 years, is a primary school teacher with three months of cough and occasional wheeze at night. She has allergic rhinitis and has never smoked. Her general practitioner arranged spirometry at the practice, performed by the practice nurse with three acceptable blows before and 15 minutes after four puffs of salbutamol through a spacer.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Mometasone nasal spray daily","Combined oral contraceptive pill","Ibuprofen 400 mg occasionally for headaches"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 98% on room air","Chest clear"]}],"tb":{"h":["Spirometry","Before salbutamol","After salbutamol"],"r":[["FEV1","2.60 L","2.84 L"],["FVC","3.60 L","3.66 L"],["FEV1/FVC","0.72","0.78"]]},"q":"What is the MOST appropriate interpretation of the bronchodilator response?","o":["Negative, because the rise in FEV1 is less than 12%","Negative, because the rise in FEV1 is less than 400 mL","Positive, because FEV1 rose by at least 200 mL and at least 12%","Positive, because FEV1 rose by more than 200 mL","Uninterpretable, because the FEV1/FVC ratio changed"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. FEV1 rose by 240 mL, which is 9.2% of 2.60 L. Both parts are required, and the percentage falls short. Asthma is not excluded.","A rise over 400 mL strongly supports asthma, but it is not the threshold for a positive test.","The rise is 240 mL but only 9.2%, so the second condition is not met.","The volume criterion is met, but the percentage criterion is not. One without the other is a negative test.","The ratio is expected to rise when FEV1 improves more than FVC. It does not invalidate the test."],"e":"Problem: a pre and post bronchodilator result to read. Task: apply both criteria. 240 mL passes the volume test and tempts a positive call. 240 divided by 2,600 is 9.2%, which fails. What if her baseline FEV1 were 1.90 L? The same 240 mL would be 12.6% and the test would be positive.","k":"A positive bronchodilator response needs both: at least 12% and at least 200 mL.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Diagnosing asthma in adults and adolescents"],"c":["rs1-04","rs1-05","rs1-06","rs1-07","rs1-08"],"id":"rs-a02","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#mimics","s":"Dung Pham, aged 41 years, has worked in a commercial bakery for six years. For the past five months he has had wheeze, cough and a tight chest that start a few hours into each shift and ease on his days off. His symptoms cleared during two weeks of leave and returned in his first week back. He had no asthma as a child. He stopped smoking ten years ago after an eight pack-year history.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Loratadine 10 mg daily","Fluticasone nasal spray daily","Pantoprazole 20 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 98% on room air","Scattered expiratory wheeze","Nasal mucosa pale and swollen"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Advise a P2 mask at work and review in three months","Arrange a chest X-ray and full blood count","Prescribe a salbutamol inhaler to use before each shift","Refer him now to a respiratory or occupational physician","Start a four-week trial of inhaled corticosteroid and review"],"a":3,"x":["A mask does not reliably prevent sensitiser exposure, and three months of continued exposure risks permanent loss of lung function.","Neither test confirms occupational asthma, and waiting for them delays the step that matters.","Pre-treating so that he can keep working in the exposure masks the problem. Reliever-only treatment is also no longer recommended for adults.","Correct. Symptoms that follow the pattern of work and improve away from it suggest occupational asthma, which needs prompt specialist confirmation while he is still exposed.","A treatment trial is a fallback when testing is not possible. It would not identify the cause, and exposure would continue."],"e":"Problem: adult-onset wheeze in a baker that tracks his roster. Task: recognise work-related asthma and act on it. His rhinitis and the response to leave are the deciding facts. What if his symptoms were unrelated to work? Spirometry with bronchodilator in the practice would be the next step.","k":"Work-related asthma symptoms: refer immediately.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Diagnosing asthma in adults and adolescents"],"c":["rs1-23","rs1-24","rs1-25","rs1-26","rs1-27"],"id":"rs-a03","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#mimics","s":"Sophie Laurent, aged 19 years, is a university student and competitive netballer. For six months she has had sudden episodes of noisy breathing and throat tightness during hard matches, which settle within minutes of stopping. Her voice goes hoarse during episodes. A salbutamol inhaler before games and eight weeks of a regular inhaled corticosteroid have made no difference. Spirometry performed at courtside during an episode was normal.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide 200 micrograms twice daily","Salbutamol two puffs before sport","Etonogestrel implant"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["At rest: chest clear, SpO2 99% on room air","Video from her coach: noise is loudest on breathing in"]}],"q":"What is the MOST likely diagnosis?","o":["Dysfunctional breathing with hyperventilation","Exercise-induced bronchoconstriction","Inducible laryngeal obstruction","Poorly controlled asthma needing a higher corticosteroid dose","Tracheobronchomalacia"],"a":2,"x":["This usually brings dizziness, tingling and sighing rather than a noise on breathing in with voice change.","This typically peaks after exercise stops, responds to salbutamol, and shows airflow limitation on spirometry during symptoms.","Correct. Throat tightness, voice change, noise on inspiration, normal spirometry during symptoms and no response to asthma treatment point to the larynx.","No response to eight weeks of inhaled corticosteroid, with normal spirometry while symptomatic, argues against asthma. More steroid is not the answer.","A rare structural cause of wheeze. It would not explain the voice change or the rapid recovery."],"e":"Problem: exertional breathing difficulty labelled as asthma that has not responded to treatment. Task: name the mimic. The deciding facts are the inspiratory noise, hoarse voice and normal spirometry during an episode. What if spirometry during an episode showed a reduced FEV1/FVC that reversed with salbutamol? Exercise-induced bronchoconstriction.","k":"Throat tightness, voice change and normal spirometry during symptoms: inducible laryngeal obstruction.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Asthma diagnosis","National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Diagnosing asthma in adults and adolescents"],"c":["rs1-23","rs1-24","rs1-25","rs1-26","rs1-27"],"id":"rs-a04","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#spirometry","s":"Marlene Kowalski, aged 52 years, runs a sheep property four hours from the nearest town with a respiratory laboratory. For two months she has woken three or four nights a week with wheeze and cough, and she is breathless carrying feed. She had asthma as a child. She has never smoked. The visiting spirometry service is next in the district in five weeks. Her practice has no spirometer.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Perindopril 5 mg daily","Atorvastatin 20 mg daily","Salbutamol inhaler borrowed from her daughter, used most days"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Respiratory rate 16/min, SpO2 97% on room air","Scattered expiratory wheeze","No ankle oedema, heart sounds normal"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange a chest CT before deciding on treatment","Continue salbutamol alone until spirometry is done in five weeks","Give a peak flow meter and withhold all treatment until four weeks of twice-daily readings are complete","Start an inhaled corticosteroid-containing treatment now and book the first available spirometry","Stop perindopril and review her in four weeks"],"a":3,"x":["Imaging does not diagnose asthma and would add delay.","Reliever-only treatment is not recommended for adults, and frequent night waking should not go untreated for five weeks.","A peak flow diary is a reasonable substitute when spirometry is delayed, but treatment that is clinically needed should not be withheld to complete it.","Correct. Significant symptoms are treated now. Testing follows as soon as it can be arranged, accepting that treatment may blunt the result.","ACE inhibitor cough is dry and without wheeze. Her wheeze and night waking are not explained by perindopril."],"e":"Problem: frequent night symptoms and no access to spirometry for five weeks. Task: balance confirming the diagnosis against the need to treat. The ideal order is test, then treat, but not at the cost of leaving significant symptoms untreated. What if her symptoms were mild and occasional? Waiting for spirometry before a preventer would be reasonable.","k":"Test before treating where possible, but do not delay treatment that is needed now.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Diagnosing asthma in adults and adolescents","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Asthma diagnosis"],"c":["rs1-04","rs1-05","rs1-06","rs1-07","rs1-08"],"id":"rs-a05","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#next","s":"Amira Haddad, aged 30 years, is 16 weeks pregnant with her second child. Since early pregnancy she has had bouts of chest tightness and cough at night, twice a week. She had a wheezy chest with colds as a teenager. Spirometry with bronchodilator at the practice was normal three weeks ago and again last week, when she had mild symptoms. She asks what other tests can be done. A respiratory laboratory is 20 minutes away.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Pregnancy multivitamin with folic acid and iodine","Doxylamine 12.5 mg at night for nausea"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 99% on room air","Chest clear","Fundal height consistent with dates"]}],"q":"Which investigation is MOST appropriate to arrange next?","o":["Chest X-ray with abdominal shielding","Exercise challenge test","Laboratory spirometry with exhaled nitric oxide","Mannitol bronchial provocation test","Serum total IgE and skin prick tests to common aeroallergens"],"a":2,"x":["Imaging does not diagnose asthma and is not indicated by her presentation.","An exercise challenge is a form of bronchial provocation, which is not arranged in pregnancy.","Correct. When practice spirometry is non-diagnostic and suspicion remains, repeat testing at an accredited laboratory with FeNO is the next step, and it is safe in pregnancy.","Bronchial provocation is contraindicated in pregnancy.","Allergy tests show sensitisation. They do not confirm or exclude asthma."],"e":"Problem: suspected asthma, two normal practice spirometry tests, and a pregnancy. Task: choose the next objective test. Provocation testing is the usual step after non-diagnostic spirometry and FeNO, which is why two forms of it are offered. Pregnancy rules them out. What if she were not pregnant and laboratory spirometry and FeNO were also normal? Bronchial provocation could then be requested.","k":"Bronchial provocation is contraindicated in pregnancy and when FEV1 is under 60% predicted.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Diagnosing asthma in adults and adolescents"],"c":["rs1-11","rs1-12","rs1-13","rs1-14","rs1-15"],"id":"rs-a06","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#history","n":3,"s":"Jordan Blake, aged 28 years, is a barista who presents with four months of intermittent cough and breathlessness. You are taking a history to judge how likely asthma is before arranging tests. Jordan uses no regular medicines and takes ibuprofen for occasional back pain.","q":"Which THREE of the following findings on history would MOST increase the probability of asthma? Select three.","o":["Breathlessness with tingling in the fingers and light-headedness","Cough that is productive every day, with no wheeze","Hay fever every spring since childhood","Symptoms that are worse in the early morning","Symptoms that worsen with talking and laughing","Throat tightness with a change in voice","Wheeze within an hour or two of taking ibuprofen"],"sc":[0,0,1,1,0,0,1],"x":["Scores 0. These suggest hyperventilation or dysfunctional breathing and lower the probability of asthma.","Scores 0. A chronic productive cough without wheeze or breathlessness points towards bronchiectasis or chronic bronchitis.","Scores 1. A personal history of atopy raises the probability.","Scores 1. Night and early-morning symptoms are typical of asthma.","Scores 0. This pattern suggests a laryngeal or functional cause.","Scores 0. These point to inducible laryngeal obstruction.","Scores 1. Symptoms after aspirin or NSAIDs raise the probability, and suggest aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease."],"e":"Three features raise the odds: atopy, a night and early-morning pattern, and symptoms triggered by NSAIDs. The other four each point to a mimic. History shapes the probability but never completes the diagnosis: spirometry follows.","k":"Atopy, early-morning symptoms and NSAID-triggered wheeze raise the probability of asthma.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Asthma diagnosis, Table 9.1"],"c":["rs1-22","rs1-01","rs1-02","rs1-03","rs1-04"],"id":"rs-k01","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Lung function tests","nt":"rs-lungfn#patterns","s":"Graham Whitlock, aged 66 years, is a retired fitter who has become breathless walking up the hill to his letterbox over the past two years. He coughs up a little clear sputum most mornings. He smoked 25 cigarettes a day from age 18 until he stopped at 60. He has no chest pain or ankle swelling. Spirometry was performed after salbutamol.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Amlodipine 5 mg daily","Rosuvastatin 10 mg daily","Paracetamol 1 g as needed for knee pain"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 95% on room air","Quiet breath sounds, prolonged expiration","BMI 23 kg/m2"]}],"tb":{"h":["Post-bronchodilator spirometry","Result","Percent predicted"],"r":[["FEV1","1.62 L","56%"],["FVC","3.40 L","92%"],["FEV1/FVC","0.48",""]]},"q":"What pattern does the spirometry show?","o":["Mixed obstructive and restrictive defect","Normal for his age","Obstructive ventilatory defect","Poor effort, uninterpretable","Restrictive ventilatory defect"],"a":2,"x":["A mixed pattern or gas trapping is suggested when the ratio and the FVC are both low. His FVC is preserved.","A ratio of 0.48 with an FEV1 of 56% predicted is well outside normal at any age.","Correct. The FEV1/FVC ratio is low and the FVC is normal. That is airflow obstruction.","Nothing in the result suggests poor effort, and the FVC is close to predicted.","Restriction is a low FVC with a normal or raised ratio. His is the reverse."],"e":"Problem: a spirometry report in a breathless ex-smoker. Task: name the pattern. Read the ratio first: 0.48 is low, so there is obstruction. Then the FVC: 92% is normal, so there is no suggestion of restriction. What if the ratio were 0.85 and the FVC 58% predicted? A restrictive pattern.","k":"Low FEV1/FVC ratio is obstruction. Low FVC with a normal or high ratio suggests restriction.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Pulmonary function testing, Table 9.27"],"c":["rs2-03","rs2-04","rs2-05","rs2-06","rs2-07"],"id":"rs-a07","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Lung function tests","nt":"rs-lungfn#lab","s":"Helen Papadakis, aged 71 years, has had atrial fibrillation for four years and has taken amiodarone for the past 18 months. She reports a dry cough and breathlessness on the stairs for three months. She has never smoked. Her echocardiogram last month showed normal left ventricular function. Practice spirometry shows an FEV1 of 62% predicted, an FVC of 60% predicted and an FEV1/FVC ratio of 0.84.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Amiodarone 200 mg daily","Apixaban 5 mg twice daily","Perindopril 2.5 mg daily","Levothyroxine 75 micrograms daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 94% on room air","Fine crackles at both bases","No ankle oedema, JVP not raised"]}],"q":"Which additional lung function test BEST assesses the gas-exchanging capacity of her lungs?","o":["Bronchial provocation test","Diffusing capacity for carbon monoxide","Hypoxic challenge test","Peak expiratory flow diary","Repeat spirometry after a bronchodilator"],"a":1,"x":["This looks for airway hyperresponsiveness in suspected asthma. Her spirometry is not obstructive.","Correct. DLCO measures transfer of gas from the air sacs to the blood. It is the laboratory test for suspected lung toxicity from drugs such as amiodarone.","This simulates altitude to assess fitness to fly.","Peak flow tracks variable obstruction in asthma. It adds nothing to a restrictive pattern.","Reversibility testing is for obstruction. Her ratio is normal and her FVC is low."],"e":"Problem: breathlessness, basal crackles and a restrictive spirometry pattern in a woman on amiodarone. Task: pick the test of gas exchange. The normal echocardiogram and absence of oedema steer away from heart failure. What if the question were how to assess the restriction itself? Static lung volumes.","k":"DLCO measures gas exchange. Static lung volumes assess restriction and hyperinflation.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Pulmonary function testing"],"c":["rs2-26","rs2-27","rs2-28","rs2-01","rs2-02"],"id":"rs-a08","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Lung function tests","nt":"rs-lungfn#contra","s":"Robert Ainsley, aged 74 years, attends for his annual COPD review. The practice nurse has him booked for spirometry today. He mentions that he had cataract surgery on his right eye five days ago and is using eye drops. His breathing is unchanged from last year and he has had no exacerbations. He is keen to have the test today because his daughter has driven him 40 minutes to the appointment.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Tiotropium 18 micrograms inhaled daily","Salbutamol as needed","Prednisolone-phenylephrine eye drops four times daily","Metformin 1 g twice daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 95% on room air","Chest: reduced breath sounds, no wheeze"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Defer spirometry until at least four weeks after the surgery","Defer spirometry until at least one week after the surgery","Perform spirometry today with a nose clip and gentle effort","Perform spirometry today, as cataract surgery is not relevant","Replace spirometry with a peak flow reading"],"a":1,"x":["Four weeks applies to chest and abdominal surgery. For eye, sinus and middle ear surgery the period is one week.","Correct. Eye surgery in the previous week is a relative contraindication. His review is routine, so a short delay costs nothing.","Spirometry requires maximal forced effort. A gentle blow produces an unusable result.","Forced expiration raises pressure in the eye. Recent eye surgery is a recognised reason to defer.","Peak flow is not useful in COPD, where the obstruction does not vary."],"e":"Problem: routine spirometry booked five days after eye surgery. Task: apply the deferral periods. The long drive adds pressure to proceed, but nothing about a stable annual review is urgent. What if he had had a laparoscopic hernia repair two weeks ago? Defer until four weeks have passed.","k":"Defer spirometry 1 week after eye, sinus or ear surgery and 4 weeks after chest or abdominal surgery.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Pulmonary function testing"],"c":["rs2-08","rs2-09","rs2-10","rs2-11","rs2-12"],"id":"rs-a09","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Lung function tests","nt":"rs-lungfn#oximetry","s":"Tevita Fifita, aged 45 years, is brought to a rural practice by a neighbour after being found drowsy in his shed, where he had been running a petrol generator with the door closed during a power cut. He has a throbbing headache, nausea and feels unsteady. He takes no regular medicines and does not smoke. A fingertip pulse oximeter is applied.","b":[{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Alert but slow to answer","Heart rate 108/min, blood pressure 128/78 mmHg, respiratory rate 20/min","SpO2 99% on room air","Chest clear, no burns, lips pink"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate interpretation of the oxygen saturation reading?","o":["It confirms adequate oxygen delivery to his tissues, so a viral illness is the more likely cause","It is falsely high because the oximeter reads carboxyhaemoglobin as oxyhaemoglobin","It is falsely low because of poor peripheral perfusion","It is unreliable because methaemoglobin absorbs light unpredictably","It rules out carbon dioxide retention"],"a":1,"x":["The exposure and symptoms fit carbon monoxide poisoning. A normal reading does not show that oxygen is reaching the tissues.","Correct. Carboxyhaemoglobin is counted as oxygenated haemoglobin by a standard pulse oximeter, so the reading is falsely reassuring.","Poor perfusion makes readings unreliable, but he is warm and normotensive, and the error with carbon monoxide runs in the other direction.","Methaemoglobinaemia follows drugs such as dapsone or benzocaine, not exhaust fumes.","Pulse oximetry never gives information about carbon dioxide, with or without carbon monoxide exposure."],"e":"Problem: headache and drowsiness after generator exhaust in a closed shed, with a saturation of 99%. Task: know what the oximeter cannot see. The normal reading and pink lips are the trap. What if he had been using benzocaine throat spray heavily and looked slate-grey? Methaemoglobinaemia, where the reading can be wrong in either direction.","k":"Pulse oximetry reads falsely high in carbon monoxide poisoning.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Pulmonary function testing"],"c":["rs2-16","rs2-17","rs2-18","rs2-19","rs2-20"],"id":"rs-a10","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Lung function tests","nt":"rs-lungfn#pef","s":"Lorraine Simmons, aged 69 years, has COPD confirmed on spirometry three years ago, with a post-bronchodilator FEV1 of 54% predicted. She smoked for 40 years and stopped at diagnosis. Her neighbour has asthma and measures her peak flow every morning. Lorraine asks for a peak flow meter so that she can monitor her own lungs at home and know when a flare-up is coming.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Umeclidinium-vilanterol inhaler daily","Salbutamol as needed","Alendronate 70 mg weekly","Colecalciferol 1000 units daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 94% on room air","Reduced breath sounds throughout"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate response to her request?","o":["Agree, and ask her to record the best of three readings each morning and each evening in a diary","Agree, but only for use during chest infections","Explain that peak flow is not useful in COPD because the obstruction does not vary","Explain that peak flow is unreliable in people over 65","Suggest a home spirometer instead"],"a":2,"x":["Twice-daily readings help in asthma, where airflow varies from day to day. In COPD they add nothing.","Peak flow does not reliably detect a COPD exacerbation. Symptoms do: more breathlessness, more sputum, or a change in its colour.","Correct. Airflow obstruction in COPD is largely fixed, so serial peak flow readings do not track the disease.","Peak flow is unreliable in children. Age over 65 is not the reason here.","Home spirometry is not part of routine COPD self-management."],"e":"Problem: a request to copy an asthma monitoring habit in COPD. Task: know where peak flow helps. It tracks variable obstruction. COPD obstruction is not variable. What if she had asthma? A meter, used with the same device each time, would be reasonable for monitoring.","k":"Peak flow monitoring is for asthma. It has no role in COPD.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Pulmonary function testing"],"c":["rs2-13","rs2-14","rs2-15","rs2-01","rs2-02"],"id":"rs-a11","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Lung function tests","nt":"rs-lungfn#gases","s":"Neville Carter, aged 72 years, has severe COPD and is brought to a small rural hospital where you are the on-call doctor. He has had two days of worsening breathlessness and green sputum. Ambulance officers gave oxygen by mask during the 50-minute trip. He is now drowsy but rouses to voice. A venous blood gas is taken with his routine bloods.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Tiotropium-olodaterol inhaler daily","Fluticasone-salmeterol inhaler twice daily","Salbutamol as needed","Furosemide 40 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Respiratory rate 24/min, heart rate 104/min, temperature 37.9 degrees","SpO2 97% on oxygen 6 L/min by mask","Widespread wheeze, prolonged expiration"]}],"tb":{"h":["Venous blood gas","Result"],"r":[["pH","7.29"],["PCO2","64 mmHg"],["Bicarbonate","30 mmol/L"]]},"q":"Which investigation is MOST appropriate to perform next?","o":["Arterial blood gas","Chest CT","D-dimer","Repeat venous blood gas in four hours","Spirometry"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. A venous gas alone cannot accurately assess respiratory failure. An abnormal venous result is followed by an arterial sample.","CT does not assess ventilation and would delay management of a drowsy patient.","Pulmonary embolism is not the leading concern, and a D-dimer would not explain his drowsiness.","Venous values do not track arterial values reliably. Repeating the same test does not answer the question.","He is too unwell to perform a forced manoeuvre, and spirometry does not measure gas exchange."],"e":"Problem: a drowsy man with COPD on oxygen, a reassuring saturation and an abnormal venous gas. Task: choose the test that defines his ventilation. The saturation of 97% says nothing about carbon dioxide. What if the venous gas had been normal? Arterial sampling would be less pressing, though a normal venous result does not fully exclude carbon dioxide retention.","k":"An abnormal venous gas in suspected respiratory failure is followed by an arterial gas.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Pulmonary function testing"],"c":["rs2-21","rs2-22","rs2-23","rs2-24","rs2-25"],"id":"rs-a12","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Lung function tests","nt":"rs-lungfn#contra","n":3,"s":"You supervise the spirometry clinic at your practice. The nurse shows you this afternoon's list of seven patients booked for routine spirometry and asks whether any should be rebooked.","q":"For which THREE patients should spirometry be deferred today? Select three.","o":["A 34-year-old who had a laparoscopic appendicectomy 16 days ago","A 45-year-old with well-controlled hypertension on perindopril","A 52-year-old who had a cold that cleared three weeks ago","A 58-year-old who had endoscopic sinus surgery four days ago","A 63-year-old who had a heart attack and a stent five days ago","A 70-year-old who had cataract surgery three months ago","A 77-year-old with stable angina and no recent change in symptoms"],"sc":[1,0,0,1,1,0,0],"x":["Scores 1. Abdominal surgery in the previous four weeks is a relative contraindication.","Scores 0. Controlled hypertension is not a reason to defer.","Scores 0. The infection has resolved. A current infection would call for infection-control measures.","Scores 1. Sinus surgery in the previous week is a relative contraindication.","Scores 1. A heart attack in the previous week is a relative contraindication.","Scores 0. The one-week period after eye surgery has long passed.","Scores 0. Stable angina is not on the list of reasons to defer."],"e":"Two timeframes cover most of the list: one week for a heart attack and for eye, sinus or middle ear surgery, and four weeks for chest or abdominal surgery. These are relative contraindications. If a result is needed urgently, a respiratory laboratory can test with support on hand.","k":"Defer spirometry: 1 week after MI or eye, sinus or ear surgery; 4 weeks after chest or abdominal surgery.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Pulmonary function testing"],"c":["rs2-08","rs2-09","rs2-10","rs2-11","rs2-12"],"id":"rs-k02","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#start","s":"Ethan Marsh, aged 26 years, is a graphic designer whose asthma was confirmed last week on spirometry with a positive bronchodilator response. He gets wheeze and chest tightness about twice a month, usually when visiting a friend who has cats, and has never woken at night with symptoms. He has had no flare-ups needing oral corticosteroids and has never been to hospital with his breathing. He does not smoke or vape.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Loratadine 10 mg as needed","No inhalers"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 99% on room air","Chest clear","Demonstrates good technique with a dry powder inhaler"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate initial treatment?","o":["Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms, one inhalation as needed","Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms, two inhalations twice daily, plus one as needed","Fluticasone propionate-salmeterol 250/25 micrograms, two inhalations twice daily","Montelukast 10 mg daily","Salbutamol 100 micrograms, two inhalations as needed"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. Most adults start on low-dose budesonide-formoterol taken only when symptoms occur. It treats inflammation every time the reliever is used.","This is medium-dose maintenance and reliever therapy. It is for frequent or severe symptoms or a serious past flare-up, none of which he has.","A fixed-dose combination at this strength is more treatment than infrequent symptoms need, and it cannot be used as a reliever.","Montelukast is less effective than inhaled corticosteroid-based treatment and is an alternative for people who cannot or will not use one.","A reliever alone is no longer recommended for anyone aged 12 or over, however infrequent the symptoms."],"e":"Problem: newly confirmed asthma with symptoms twice a month and no risk factors. Task: choose the first treatment. The mildness of his asthma is what makes salbutamol alone tempting. The 2025 Handbook removes that option. What if he had needed prednisolone for a flare-up eight months ago? Start with maintenance and reliever therapy.","k":"First treatment for most people aged 12 and over: low-dose budesonide-formoterol as needed.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Management: adults and adolescents: Initial asthma treatment after diagnosis"],"c":["rs3-12","rs3-13","rs3-14","rs3-15","rs3-16"],"id":"rs-a13","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#principles","s":"Deborah Lang, aged 48 years, is a hairdresser who has had asthma since her teens. She asks for another salbutamol prescription and says her asthma is 'fine'. She uses salbutamol three or four times a week and has no other inhaler. Pharmacy records show five salbutamol inhalers dispensed in the past 12 months. She had a course of prednisolone from an after-hours clinic last winter. She has never smoked.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Salbutamol 100 micrograms, two puffs as needed","Sertraline 50 mg daily","Levonorgestrel intrauterine device"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 98% on room air","Chest clear today","Uses her inhaler without a spacer; technique otherwise adequate"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add montelukast 10 mg daily and continue salbutamol as needed","Change her treatment to a regimen that contains an inhaled corticosteroid","Prescribe salbutamol with a spacer and review in 12 months","Refer her to a respiratory physician for assessment of difficult-to-treat asthma","Supply a home nebuliser for use when her symptoms are worse"],"a":1,"x":["Montelukast is less effective than an inhaled corticosteroid and is not the first choice when she has never tried one.","Correct. Reliever-only treatment is not recommended. Five canisters a year, use on more than two days a week and a recent oral corticosteroid course all mark her as at risk.","A spacer improves delivery, but leaves her on a reliever alone with poor control and a recent flare-up.","Referral is for asthma that stays uncontrolled on an optimised medium-dose regimen. She has not yet had any preventer.","Nebulisers are not used for routine treatment. A pressurised inhaler with a spacer is as effective."],"e":"Problem: an adult who feels fine on salbutamol alone but uses it most days. Task: recognise uncontrolled, under-treated asthma. Her own assessment is the distractor. The dispensing record and the prednisolone course are the deciding facts. What if she were already on medium-dose maintenance and reliever therapy with good technique? Then eosinophils and referral.","k":"Salbutamol alone is not acceptable asthma treatment in anyone aged 12 or over.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Management: adults and adolescents: Initial asthma treatment after diagnosis; Treatment levels"],"c":["rs3-01","rs3-02","rs3-03","rs3-04","rs3-05"],"id":"rs-a14","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#stepup","s":"Mohammed Aziz, aged 39 years, drives a delivery van and has had asthma for 15 years. Over the past two months he has woken with wheeze once or twice a week and uses salbutamol on four days a week. He takes his preventer every morning and evening, which his dispensing history confirms. You watch him use his inhaler with a spacer and his technique is correct. He has no nasal symptoms and has never smoked. His weight is stable.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Fluticasone propionate-salmeterol 50/25 micrograms, two puffs twice daily","Salbutamol 100 micrograms, two puffs as needed","Esomeprazole 20 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 98% on room air","Chest clear","BMI 26 kg/m2"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add tiotropium by soft mist inhaler","Continue the same treatment and add montelukast","Increase to fluticasone propionate-salmeterol 250/25 micrograms, two puffs twice daily","Refer to a respiratory physician","Switch to low-dose maintenance and reliever therapy with budesonide-formoterol"],"a":4,"x":["A long-acting muscarinic antagonist is an add-on at higher treatment levels, guided by spirometry.","Montelukast is not the recommended next step from a low-dose combination.","Raising the corticosteroid dose is not the first move. Changing the reliever to corticosteroid-formoterol comes before a dose increase.","Referral follows failure of an optimised medium-dose regimen. He is on a low-dose regimen.","Correct. For anyone on a fixed-dose combination with a salbutamol reliever, a switch to maintenance and reliever therapy is considered before increasing the dose."],"e":"Problem: poor control on a low-dose fixed combination with salbutamol, with adherence and technique confirmed. Task: choose the step up. The stem removes the usual reasons to hold off. The Handbook's preferred move is to change the kind of regimen, not the dose. What if he had not been taking the preventer? Address adherence first and leave the regimen alone.","k":"Uncontrolled on fixed corticosteroid-LABA plus salbutamol: switch to MART before raising the dose.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Management: adults and adolescents: Adjusting treatment"],"c":["rs3-24","rs3-25","rs3-26","rs3-01","rs3-02"],"id":"rs-a15","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#refer","s":"Natalie Brooks, aged 44 years, is an accountant with asthma since childhood. Despite four months on her current treatment she wakes with symptoms twice a week and has needed two courses of prednisolone this year. She takes every dose, and the practice nurse confirmed correct technique last week. Her allergic rhinitis is well controlled, she has never smoked, and there are no pets or mould at home. Spirometry shows an FEV1 of 71% predicted.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms, two inhalations twice daily and one as needed","Mometasone nasal spray daily","Cetirizine 10 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 97% on room air","Scattered expiratory wheeze","BMI 24 kg/m2"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add a second inhaled corticosteroid inhaler to reach a high daily dose","Arrange a blood eosinophil count and refer to a respiratory physician","Change to a different brand of budesonide-formoterol inhaler","Prescribe prednisolone 10 mg daily long term","Step down to as-needed budesonide-formoterol to reduce her exposure"],"a":1,"x":["Most of the benefit of an inhaled corticosteroid comes at low dose, and long-term high doses add harm. This is not the recommended response.","Correct. Asthma uncontrolled on medium-dose maintenance and reliever therapy, with adherence, technique, triggers and comorbidities addressed, needs specialist assessment. Eosinophils are checked while waiting.","Her technique is correct and the drug would be the same. A brand change does not address the problem.","Maintenance oral corticosteroids carry serious long-term harm and are not started in primary care for this.","Stepping down requires good control for two to three months. Hers is poor."],"e":"Problem: persistent poor control and repeated oral corticosteroid courses on a medium-dose regimen, taken properly. Task: know when general practice has reached its limit. Every modifiable factor has been ruled out in the stem. What if her eosinophil count came back at 1,600 per microlitre? The referral becomes urgent.","k":"Uncontrolled on an optimised medium-dose regimen: blood eosinophils and specialist referral.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Management: adults and adolescents: Adjusting treatment"],"c":["rs3-27","rs3-28","rs3-29","rs3-01","rs3-02"],"id":"rs-a16","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#doses","s":"Lucy Tran, aged 31 years, is a nurse who started as-needed budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms by dry powder inhaler three months ago for mild asthma. She has a cold and has been using more of the inhaler. She phones the practice to ask how many inhalations she can safely take, because she used five yesterday and is worried she is overdoing it. She is speaking in full sentences and says the inhaler is working each time.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms dry powder, one inhalation as needed","Combined oral contraceptive pill","Paracetamol for the cold"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate advice about the usual maximum number of inhalations?","o":["Up to 12 inhalations on one occasion and 16 in a day","Up to 12 inhalations on one occasion and 24 in a day","Up to 2 inhalations on one occasion and 4 a day","Up to 4 inhalations on one occasion and 6 in a day","Up to 6 inhalations on one occasion and 8 in a day"],"a":4,"x":["These are the limits for the 100/3 pressurised inhaler, which delivers half the dose per puff.","Twenty-four is the short-term daily ceiling for the 100/3 pressurised inhaler, not for hers.","This is lower than the approved limits and would leave her undertreated during a cold.","This is lower than the approved limits for the dry powder inhaler.","Correct. For the 200/6 dry powder inhaler the limits are 6 on one occasion and 8 in a day. Up to 12 in a day is allowed briefly."],"e":"Problem: more reliever use during a cold, and a question about the ceiling. Task: recall the limits for her device. Two sets of numbers exist because the pressurised inhaler has half the strength per puff. What if she kept needing extra doses for three days without improving? She needs to be seen.","k":"Budesonide-formoterol 200/6: up to 6 at a time and 8 a day (12 briefly). The 100/3 inhaler doubles each number.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), table of budesonide-formoterol combinations approved as anti-inflammatory relievers"],"c":["rs3-17","rs3-18","rs3-19","rs3-20","rs3-21"],"id":"rs-a17","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#stepdown","s":"George Petrou, aged 57 years, is a high school teacher with asthma diagnosed at 30. For the past four months he has had no daytime symptoms, no night waking and has not needed extra reliever doses. His last flare-up was three years ago. He walks 5 km most days. He asks whether he can stop his inhaler altogether, because he feels cured and dislikes taking medicines. It is late summer and he has no hay fever.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms, one inhalation twice daily and one as needed","Rosuvastatin 5 mg daily","Telmisartan 40 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 98% on room air","Chest clear","Spirometry: FEV1 94% predicted"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Change to salbutamol as needed and stop budesonide-formoterol","Continue the current regimen indefinitely, as asthma is a lifelong inflammatory condition","Reduce to budesonide-formoterol as needed only, and review in 4 to 8 weeks","Stop all inhalers and review if symptoms return","Switch to montelukast 10 mg daily"],"a":2,"x":["This removes the inhaled corticosteroid, which is never withdrawn completely, and returns him to reliever-only treatment.","He meets the criteria to step down: good control for more than two to three months and low risk. Staying on more treatment than needed is not required.","Correct. Low-dose maintenance and reliever therapy steps down to the same inhaler used as needed. Review follows in four to eight weeks.","Stopping everything leaves him with no anti-inflammatory treatment and no reliever.","Montelukast is less effective and is not a step-down option from a well-tolerated inhaler."],"e":"Problem: four months of good control on low-dose maintenance and reliever therapy, and a wish to stop. Task: step down safely. He can reduce, but there is a floor. What if he were asking in the first week of a chest infection? Stepping down waits until he is well.","k":"Step down after 2 to 3 months of good control, one level at a time, and never below as-needed budesonide-formoterol.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Management: adults and adolescents: Adjusting treatment"],"c":["rs3-30","rs3-31","rs3-32","rs3-33","rs3-01"],"id":"rs-a18","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#stepup","n":4,"s":"Karen Whitfield, aged 35 years, has asthma treated with low-dose maintenance and reliever therapy. Over six weeks her control has slipped: she now has symptoms on four days a week and woke twice last week. She asks for 'something stronger'.","q":"Before stepping up her treatment, which FOUR of the following should you assess? Select four.","o":["Adherence to her maintenance doses","Bone mineral density","Exposure to triggers at home and work","Her inhaler technique, by watching her use the device","Serum total IgE","Symptoms of rhinitis and other comorbidities","Thyroid function"],"sc":[1,0,1,1,0,1,0],"x":["Scores 1. Poor adherence to maintenance doses is common and is checked before any step up.","Scores 0. Bone density is relevant to long-term oral corticosteroid use, not to this decision.","Scores 1. Smoke, allergens, infections and work exposures can explain a loss of control.","Scores 1. Technique is often poor and can only be judged by watching.","Scores 0. Total IgE is a specialist test for selecting monoclonal antibody treatment.","Scores 1. Rhinitis, obesity, reflux and laryngeal obstruction can each worsen control or mimic it.","Scores 0. Thyroid function has no routine role here."],"e":"Four checks come before any step up: is she taking it, is she taking it correctly, is something setting her off, and is something else going on. Only when all four are satisfactory does the regimen move up a level.","k":"Before stepping up: adherence, technique, triggers, comorbidities.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Management: adults and adolescents: Adjusting treatment"],"c":["rs3-24","rs3-25","rs3-26","rs3-01","rs3-02"],"id":"rs-k03","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","nt":"rs-asthma-review#control","s":"Aroha Ngata, aged 33 years, is a childcare worker attending for her yearly asthma review. In the past four weeks she has had daytime chest tightness on one day a week, relieved by a single dose of her inhaler. She used her reliever before netball twice a week as well. She woke once with wheeze after a late shift. She has missed no work and plays a full game without stopping. She does not smoke.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms, one inhalation as needed","Etonogestrel implant","Ferrous sulfate 325 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 99% on room air","Chest clear","Technique with her dry powder inhaler is correct"]}],"q":"How is her asthma symptom control BEST classified?","o":["Good, because daytime symptoms occur on fewer than three days a week","Good, because she has no limitation of activity","Poor, because she has had a night waking with asthma","Poor, because she uses her reliever before exercise","Poor, because she uses her reliever on three days a week in total"],"a":2,"x":["Daytime symptoms are within the limit, but good control requires every criterion to be met.","Activity is unrestricted, but one criterion being met does not make control good.","Correct. Any night or waking symptom in the previous four weeks places her outside good control.","Doses taken before exercise are not counted when judging reliever use.","Pre-exercise doses are excluded. Her symptom-driven use is one day a week, which is within the limit."],"e":"Problem: a four-week history to classify. Task: apply all four criteria. Three are met: daytime symptoms and reliever use on two days a week or fewer, and no activity limit. The single night waking is the one that fails. What if she had not woken at night? Good control.","k":"Good control needs all four: symptoms and reliever on 2 days a week or fewer, no night waking, no activity limit.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Management: adults and adolescents: Assessing and reviewing asthma"],"c":["rs4-01","rs4-02","rs4-03","rs4-04","rs4-05"],"id":"rs-a19","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","nt":"rs-asthma-review#ocs","s":"Stuart Mackie, aged 46 years, is a chef with asthma treated with maintenance and reliever therapy. He has had a head cold for four days. Since yesterday his wheeze has returned within two to three hours of each reliever dose, and he took nine inhalations in the past 24 hours. He is speaking in sentences. He follows a written action plan but has run out of the tablets it mentions.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms, two inhalations twice daily and one as needed","Pantoprazole 40 mg daily","Ibuprofen avoided since a reaction in his twenties"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Respiratory rate 20/min, heart rate 96/min","SpO2 95% on room air","Widespread expiratory wheeze, no accessory muscle use"]}],"q":"Which prescription is MOST appropriate?","o":["Amoxicillin 500 mg three times daily for 5 days","Prednisolone 25 mg each morning for 3 days","Prednisolone 50 mg each morning for 5 days, then reduce by 10 mg every second day","Prednisolone 50 mg each morning for 5 days, with no taper","Prednisolone 50 mg each morning for 5 days, with two repeats to keep at home"],"a":3,"x":["A viral cold is the trigger. Antibiotics are not part of treating an asthma flare-up.","This is below the adult dose of 37.5 to 50 mg and shorter than the 5 to 10 days recommended for adults.","A taper is not needed after a short course and only prolongs exposure.","Correct. Symptoms returning within four hours of the reliever call for oral corticosteroids: 37.5 to 50 mg each morning for 5 to 10 days.","Repeats are not provided. Each course should be prescribed and counted, because frequent courses signal a need to step up or refer."],"e":"Problem: a flare-up that extra reliever doses are no longer holding. Task: prescribe the oral corticosteroid course correctly. Three of the options are variations on the right drug with one wrong detail each: dose, taper or repeats. What if he were 14 years old? 1 mg/kg, to a maximum of 50 mg, for 3 days.","k":"Adult flare-up: prednisolone 37.5 to 50 mg each morning for 5 to 10 days, no taper, no repeats.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Management: adults and adolescents: Adjusting treatment","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents"],"c":["rs4-14","rs4-15","rs4-16","rs4-17","rs4-18"],"id":"rs-a20","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","nt":"rs-asthma-review#pregnancy","s":"Hannah Schultz, aged 29 years, is 10 weeks pregnant with her first child. She has had asthma since childhood, well controlled for two years. She stopped her preventer when she found out she was pregnant because she was worried about steroids harming the baby. For the past two weeks she has had a tight chest most mornings and has woken twice at night.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide 400 micrograms twice daily (stopped four weeks ago)","Salbutamol two puffs, now used daily","Folic acid 0.5 mg and iodine 150 micrograms daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 98% on room air","Scattered wheeze on forced expiration","Blood pressure 108/64 mmHg"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate advice?","o":["Avoid all inhaled corticosteroids until after the first trimester","Rely on salbutamol alone until delivery","Restart an inhaled corticosteroid-containing regimen now","Take a course of oral prednisolone in place of the inhaler","Use montelukast in place of the inhaled corticosteroid"],"a":2,"x":["There is no reason to wait. Poorly controlled asthma is the risk in pregnancy, and it has already returned.","Reliever-only treatment is not recommended for any adult, and daily use shows that her asthma is uncontrolled.","Correct. Asthma in pregnancy is managed as in any adult. The preventer is not stopped, and treatment is stepped up as needed.","Oral corticosteroids are for flare-ups that do not settle. They do not replace an inhaled preventer.","Montelukast is less effective than an inhaled corticosteroid, and there is no reason to switch."],"e":"Problem: a pregnant woman who has stopped her preventer out of concern for the baby. Task: correct the belief and restore control. Flare-ups are linked to low birth weight and preterm birth. What if she were well controlled and asking to reduce her dose? Stepping down is deferred until after the pregnancy.","k":"Asthma in pregnancy: continue the inhaled corticosteroid, step up if needed, do not step down.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Management: adults and adolescents: Adjusting treatment; Assessing and reviewing asthma"],"c":["rs4-20","rs4-21","rs4-22","rs4-01","rs4-02"],"id":"rs-a21","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","nt":"rs-asthma-review#plan","s":"Raymond Cheng, aged 62 years, is a retired engineer with asthma managed for a decade on a single inhaler. He travels to a remote fishing camp for three weeks each winter and wants his action plan updated before he goes. He perceives his symptoms reliably. He asks what the plan should tell him to do first if his chest tightens for several days and he needs his reliever most days.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Ciclesonide 160 micrograms once daily by pressurised inhaler with spacer","Salbutamol two puffs as needed","Tamsulosin 400 micrograms daily","Atorvastatin 20 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 98% on room air","Chest clear","Technique correct"]}],"q":"For a persistent, non-urgent increase in symptoms, which instruction is MOST appropriate for his written plan?","o":["Double the ciclesonide dose for 3 days","Quadruple the ciclesonide dose for 1 to 2 weeks","Start amoxicillin and continue usual inhalers","Stop ciclesonide and start prednisolone 50 mg daily","Use salbutamol every 2 hours until symptoms settle"],"a":1,"x":["Doubling the inhaled dose is not enough to prevent progression.","Correct. For a person on inhaled corticosteroid alone, quadrupling the dose for one to two weeks is the recommended first response to persistent worsening.","Antibiotics have no routine role in an asthma flare-up.","Prednisolone is added if the higher inhaled dose does not work. The inhaled corticosteroid is not stopped.","More reliever treats symptoms but does nothing for the inflammation driving them."],"e":"Problem: writing the middle section of an action plan for someone on inhaled corticosteroid alone. Task: know the size of the increase. What if he were on a fixed corticosteroid-LABA inhaler? A prednisolone course is usually more practical, because raising the corticosteroid would need a second inhaler.","k":"On inhaled corticosteroid alone, the plan quadruples the dose for 1 to 2 weeks. Doubling is not enough.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents"],"v":"This instruction comes from the 2020 Therapeutic Guidelines. The 2025 Australian Asthma Handbook pages read for this unit did not cover it, and the Handbook now prefers regimens where the reliever itself carries the corticosteroid.","c":["rs4-09","rs4-10","rs4-11","rs4-12","rs4-13"],"id":"rs-a22","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","nt":"rs-asthma-review#general","s":"Vincent Russo, aged 58 years, has had asthma since his twenties, well controlled on maintenance and reliever therapy with no flare-ups in five years. He was discharged from hospital three days ago after a heart attack treated with a stent. The discharge summary notes that a beta blocker was withheld 'because of asthma' and asks the general practitioner to review. His echocardiogram showed mildly reduced left ventricular function.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms, one inhalation twice daily and one as needed","Aspirin 100 mg daily","Ticagrelor 90 mg twice daily","Atorvastatin 80 mg daily","Ramipril 2.5 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Heart rate 84/min, blood pressure 124/76 mmHg","SpO2 98% on room air","Chest clear"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate approach to beta-blocker treatment?","o":["Avoid beta blockers permanently and use diltiazem","Start a beta blocker at a low dose under supervision","Start a beta blocker at the full target dose, as his asthma is controlled","Start a beta blocker only after bronchial provocation testing","Start a beta blocker only if his asthma treatment is first stepped up"],"a":1,"x":["Asthma does not remove the benefit of a beta blocker after a heart attack. Avoidance is not required when an indication exists.","Correct. When a person with asthma develops an indication for a beta blocker, it is started at a low dose under supervision.","Starting at full dose ignores the risk of bronchoconstriction. The start is cautious.","Provocation testing is a diagnostic test for uncertain asthma, not a safety screen for beta blockers.","His asthma is well controlled. There is no reason to increase asthma treatment pre-emptively."],"e":"Problem: a clear cardiac indication for a beta blocker in a man with well-controlled asthma. Task: weigh the two conditions. Beta blockers are a trigger to minimise, not an absolute ban. What if he had no cardiac indication and was offered a beta blocker for tremor? Another drug would be chosen.","k":"Asthma with a new indication for a beta blocker: start low, under supervision.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents, Table 9.7"],"c":["rs4-23","rs4-24","rs4-25","rs4-26","rs4-27"],"id":"rs-a23","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","nt":"rs-asthma-review#review","s":"Eleni Markou, aged 41 years, attends for a cervical screening test. Her record shows asthma, and her last asthma-specific visit was three years ago. She uses budesonide-formoterol as needed about once a fortnight, has had no night symptoms or flare-ups, and plays tennis weekly. Her last spirometry was at diagnosis six years ago. She smokes socially, about five cigarettes a week. She has no written action plan.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms, one inhalation as needed","No other regular medicines"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 99% on room air","Chest clear"]}],"q":"Which is the MOST appropriate plan for her asthma?","o":["Add a daily maintenance dose, because she smokes","Arrange a chest X-ray, because she smokes","Arrange a review with spirometry, an action plan and smoking cessation support","No action is needed today, because her symptoms are well controlled and she rarely needs her inhaler","Refer to a respiratory physician, because spirometry is overdue"],"a":2,"x":["Her control is good. Smoking is addressed directly, not by adding inhaled corticosteroid.","Imaging is not part of routine asthma review.","Correct. Asthma is reviewed at least yearly, spirometry is repeated about every two years, everyone has a written plan, and smoking is addressed at every opportunity.","Good symptom control does not remove the need for review. She is overdue on three counts and smokes.","Overdue spirometry is arranged in primary care. Referral is for uncertain diagnosis or uncontrolled disease."],"e":"Problem: well-controlled asthma noticed during an unrelated visit, with no review for three years. Task: list what a review must cover. Feeling well is the distractor. What if she had frequent symptoms with normal spirometry? The diagnosis would need reconsidering.","k":"Asthma review at least yearly; spirometry about every 2 years; a written plan for everyone; address smoking.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Management: adults and adolescents: Assessing and reviewing asthma"],"c":["rs4-06","rs4-07","rs4-08","rs4-01","rs4-02"],"id":"rs-a24","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","nt":"rs-asthma-review#plan","n":4,"s":"You are writing an asthma action plan for Michelle Okafor, aged 27 years, who has just started low-dose maintenance and reliever therapy with budesonide-formoterol.","q":"Which FOUR of the following must her written action plan include? Select four.","o":["Her predicted peak flow from a height chart","Her usual maintenance and reliever doses","The date of her next influenza vaccination","The maximum number of reliever inhalations per occasion and per day","The results of her allergy tests","When to seek medical care and when to call an ambulance","When to start a course of oral corticosteroids"],"sc":[0,1,0,1,0,1,1],"x":["Scores 0. Peak-flow plans are for selected patients and use personal best, not a predicted value.","Scores 1. The plan starts with usual treatment.","Scores 0. Useful to arrange, but not part of an action plan.","Scores 1. Limits are specific to the inhaler and must be stated.","Scores 0. Allergy results do not guide what to do in a flare-up.","Scores 1. Every person with asthma must know when to get help.","Scores 1. The plan states the trigger for oral corticosteroids."],"e":"A plan answers four questions: what do I take when well, how much extra can I take, when do I add prednisolone, and when do I get help. It is matched to the regimen and reviewed yearly and whenever treatment changes.","k":"An action plan lists usual doses, reliever limits, when to start oral corticosteroids, and when to seek help.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Management: adults and adolescents: Adjusting treatment","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents"],"c":["rs4-09","rs4-10","rs4-11","rs4-12","rs4-13"],"id":"rs-k04","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#treat","s":"Bianca Rossi, aged 34 years, walks into your suburban practice at 10 am with a tight chest and wheeze that began overnight during a cold. She has asthma and ran out of her preventer a month ago. She can say only four or five words between breaths and is sitting forward on the chair. The nearest emergency department is 25 minutes away by road.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Salbutamol inhaler, about 20 puffs since midnight","Fluticasone-salmeterol inhaler (not taken for four weeks)","Sertraline 100 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Alert, anxious","Respiratory rate 28/min, heart rate 118/min","SpO2 93% on room air","Widespread wheeze, using neck muscles to breathe"]}],"q":"In addition to calling an ambulance, what is the MOST appropriate immediate treatment?","o":["Intramuscular adrenaline 0.5 mg","Nebulised salbutamol 2.5 mg, driven by air","Oral prednisolone 50 mg, then reassess in one hour before giving a bronchodilator","Salbutamol 12 puffs through a spacer, repeated every 20 minutes","Salbutamol 4 puffs through a spacer, repeated every 4 hours"],"a":3,"x":["Adrenaline is first-line for anaphylaxis, or for a peri-arrest patient who cannot inhale. She has neither.","A nebuliser is used only when a spacer cannot be. She can use one, and 2.5 mg is not the adult dose.","Prednisolone is given early, but never in place of a bronchodilator and never with a delay to treatment.","Correct. Speech in short phrases, accessory muscle use, a pulse of 118 and saturation of 93% make this severe. The dose is 12 puffs through a spacer, at least every 20 minutes for the first hour.","Four puffs is the first aid dose. A four-hourly interval is far too long for a severe attack."],"e":"Problem: an acute attack in the treatment room. Task: grade it and dose it. Any one severe feature is enough: here there are four. Severe means 12 puffs, every 20 minutes, with an ambulance on the way. What if she were speaking full sentences with a saturation of 97%? Mild to moderate: 4 to 12 puffs, managed in the practice.","k":"Severe acute asthma: salbutamol 12 puffs via spacer every 20 minutes for the first hour, and an ambulance.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Managing acute asthma in adults and adolescents in primary care"],"c":["rs5-12","rs5-13","rs5-14","rs5-15","rs5-16"],"id":"rs-a25","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#steroids","s":"Darren Holt, aged 42 years, is a landscaper who presents with wheeze and breathlessness that came on while mowing a client's lawn this morning. He has asthma treated with as-needed budesonide-formoterol and had taken four inhalations before arriving. On arrival he could speak in full sentences with a saturation of 96%. He was given eight puffs of salbutamol through a spacer. Twenty minutes later he feels back to normal.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms as needed","Cetirizine 10 mg daily in spring","No other regular medicines"]},{"h":"Examination findings (after treatment)","i":["Respiratory rate 16/min, heart rate 84/min","SpO2 98% on room air","Chest clear"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange a chest X-ray before he leaves","Discharge now with advice to use his inhaler as needed","Give a further 12 puffs of salbutamol to prevent a relapse","Give oral prednisolone and observe him before discharge","Start oral amoxicillin for five days"],"a":3,"x":["Imaging is not part of managing an uncomplicated asthma attack that has responded to treatment.","He has responded, but the episode was significant enough to bring him in. Leaving without a corticosteroid or a period of observation risks relapse.","He is symptom-free. More bronchodilator adds side effects without benefit.","Correct. Systemic corticosteroids are started for every adult who presents with acute asthma, whatever the first severity grading, and the patient is observed after settling.","Antibiotics have no role. The trigger was pollen and exertion."],"e":"Problem: a mild to moderate attack that cleared with one dose. Task: decide what else he needs. The rapid recovery makes sending him straight home attractive. The 2025 Handbook gives prednisolone to all who present and asks for at least four hours of observation. What if this were a brief wheeze that settled at home with his own inhaler? No presentation, no prednisolone: his action plan covers it.","k":"Every adult who presents with acute asthma gets a systemic corticosteroid, even after a good response.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Managing acute asthma in adults and adolescents in primary care"],"v":"The 2020 Therapeutic Guidelines allow corticosteroids to be omitted in the mildest cases that respond quickly and completely. This question follows the 2025 Australian Asthma Handbook.","c":["rs5-21","rs5-22","rs5-23","rs5-01","rs5-02"],"id":"rs-a26","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#severity","s":"Kylie Brennan, aged 27 years, has been in your rural practice treatment room for 40 minutes with a severe asthma attack. She has had three doses of 12 puffs of salbutamol through a spacer and oral prednisolone. The ambulance is 15 minutes away. The nurse calls you back because Kylie has 'calmed down': she has stopped talking, her eyes are closing, and the wheeze that was loud on arrival can no longer be heard.","b":[{"h":"Observations on arrival","i":["Respiratory rate 32/min, heart rate 126/min","SpO2 91% on room air","Loud wheeze, speaking in single words"]},{"h":"Observations now","i":["Respiratory rate 12/min, heart rate 58/min","SpO2 86% on oxygen 6 L/min","Breath sounds very soft on both sides"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate interpretation of the change in her condition?","o":["A panic attack has resolved, unmasking mild asthma","She has developed a pneumothorax on one side","She is exhausted and close to respiratory arrest","She is over-sedated by salbutamol","The bronchodilator has taken effect and she is recovering"],"a":2,"x":["Falling saturation and a slowing pulse do not occur when anxiety settles.","A pneumothorax is a recognised complication, but it gives one-sided signs. Her breath sounds are soft on both sides.","Correct. Drowsiness, a silent chest, a slow respiratory rate and bradycardia are life-threatening features. Bradycardia may come just before respiratory arrest.","Salbutamol causes tremor and tachycardia. It does not sedate or slow the pulse.","Recovery brings easier speech and better saturation. Hers is falling as the chest goes quiet."],"e":"Problem: a patient who looks calmer after 40 minutes of treatment. Task: tell recovery from exhaustion. Every number has moved the wrong way for the wrong reason: the breathing has slowed because she is tiring, and the wheeze has gone because too little air is moving. What if her saturation had risen to 96% and she were chatting? That is recovery.","k":"Silent chest, drowsiness, slow breathing and bradycardia in acute asthma mean impending arrest.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Secondary severity assessment in adults and adolescents in primary care"],"c":["rs5-04","rs5-05","rs5-06","rs5-07","rs5-08"],"id":"rs-a27","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#anaphylaxis","s":"Jasmine Wu, aged 19 years, is carried into the practice by friends from the food court next door, ten minutes after starting a satay lunch. She has asthma and a peanut allergy diagnosed in childhood. She is wheezing loudly and says her throat feels tight. Her friends gave six puffs of her salbutamol inhaler in the car park without improvement. She did not have her adrenaline autoinjector with her.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms as needed","Salbutamol inhaler as needed","Adrenaline autoinjector (at home)"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Respiratory rate 30/min, heart rate 124/min, blood pressure 92/58 mmHg","SpO2 93% on room air","Widespread wheeze","Raised red wheals on the neck and chest; lips swollen"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate immediate treatment?","o":["Intramuscular adrenaline into the outer thigh","Intravenous hydrocortisone 100 mg","Nebulised salbutamol 5 mg with ipratropium 500 micrograms","Oral cetirizine 10 mg and prednisolone 50 mg","Salbutamol 12 puffs through a spacer"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. Sudden breathlessness with hives and lip swelling after a known allergen is anaphylaxis. Intramuscular adrenaline is first-line and is given without delay.","Corticosteroids act too slowly to treat anaphylaxis and are never the first drug.","Bronchodilators can follow, but they do not treat the airway swelling or the falling blood pressure.","Antihistamines relieve itch and hives only. They do not treat airway or circulatory compromise.","This is the right dose for severe asthma, but treating this as asthma alone misses the diagnosis."],"e":"Problem: wheeze in a known asthmatic that has not responded to salbutamol. Task: recognise anaphylaxis. The skin signs, lip swelling, low blood pressure and timing after food are the deciding facts. What if there were no skin signs but she had vomiting and dizziness with the wheeze? Anaphylaxis is still suspected and adrenaline is still given.","k":"Wheeze with skin, gut or circulation signs is anaphylaxis: intramuscular adrenaline first.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Acute asthma","National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Managing acute asthma in adults and adolescents in primary care"],"c":["rs5-24","rs5-25","rs5-01","rs5-02","rs5-03"],"id":"rs-a28","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#after","s":"Walter Henare, aged 61 years, presents to your rural practice at 4 pm with an asthma attack graded mild to moderate. He has had three doses of salbutamol through a spacer and oral prednisolone. Two hours later he can speak in sentences, but wheeze has returned about 90 minutes after each dose. He lives alone on a property 45 minutes away with patchy phone reception. He was admitted to intensive care with asthma four years ago.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms, two inhalations twice daily and as needed","Metformin 1 g twice daily","Ramipril 5 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings (two hours after arrival)","i":["Respiratory rate 20/min, heart rate 98/min","SpO2 95% on room air","Scattered wheeze"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange transfer to hospital for further observation and treatment","Discharge home with a nebuliser on loan from the practice","Discharge home with instructions to take salbutamol every two hours overnight","Discharge home with prednisolone and review in the morning","Keep him in the practice until closing, then discharge"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. He still needs reliever more often than every four hours, lives alone far from help, and has a past intensive care admission. Each of these weighs against going home.","A nebuliser at home does not make an unstable patient safe, and encourages delay in seeking help.","Needing reliever every two hours is itself the reason not to discharge.","Review tomorrow assumes a safe night. His reliever need and isolation make that unsafe.","A few more hours do not change his overnight risk if he is still needing frequent reliever."],"e":"Problem: partial response to treatment in a man with risk factors and no one at home. Task: decide where he spends the night. The reasonable saturation is the distractor. The deciding fact is that wheeze returns well inside four hours. What if he had needed no reliever for four hours and lived in town with his partner? Home, with written instructions and review in 3 to 5 days.","k":"No discharge while reliever is needed more often than every 4 hours. Weigh distance, isolation and past severe attacks.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Acute asthma","National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Managing acute asthma in adults and adolescents in primary care"],"c":["rs5-26","rs5-27","rs5-28","rs5-29","rs5-30"],"id":"rs-a29","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#storm","s":"Lachlan Burke, aged 23 years, is an agronomy student in the Riverina. He attends in late August for a medical certificate. He mentions sneezing and itchy eyes every spring, and that for the past two Octobers he has had a few days of chest tightness and wheeze, which he treated with a salbutamol inhaler bought from the pharmacy. He is well between seasons and has never been told he has asthma. Spirometry today is normal.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Salbutamol inhaler, used in October only","Fexofenadine 180 mg as needed","No preventer inhaler"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 99% on room air","Chest clear","Pale, swollen nasal turbinates"]}],"q":"Which advice is MOST appropriate to reduce his risk during the coming pollen season?","o":["Avoid outdoor exercise in winter","Carry salbutamol and take two puffs before going outdoors on each day of the pollen season","Start a nasal corticosteroid spray as his only preventive treatment","Take prednisolone 25 mg on days when storms are forecast","Use an inhaled corticosteroid-containing treatment through the pollen season"],"a":4,"x":["His risk period is spring and early summer, when grass pollen counts are high.","Reliever-only self-management with grass pollen allergy is the highest-risk pattern for severe thunderstorm asthma.","A nasal spray will help his rhinitis, but it is not known whether it protects against thunderstorm asthma. He has lower airway symptoms each spring.","Pre-emptive oral corticosteroids are not recommended and expose him to avoidable harm.","Correct. Springtime rhinitis with seasonal wheeze marks him as at risk. Inhaled corticosteroid treatment is protective, and should be in place before and during the season."],"e":"Problem: seasonal wheeze self-treated with pharmacy salbutamol in a high-pollen region. Task: prevent a severe attack. Normal spirometry in August is expected out of season and does not clear him. What if he had rhinitis alone and had never wheezed? He is still at risk, and needs advice about storm days and what to do if symptoms start.","k":"Springtime hay fever with seasonal wheeze: inhaled corticosteroid through the pollen season, not salbutamol alone.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Thunderstorm asthma"],"c":["rs5-31","rs5-32","rs5-33","rs5-34","rs5-01"],"id":"rs-a30","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#oxygen","s":"Fiona Gallagher, aged 55 years, is brought to your practice by her husband with a severe asthma attack. An ambulance has been called and the first dose of salbutamol is being given through a spacer. She has had asthma since childhood, has never smoked and has no other lung disease. The practice has an oxygen cylinder with a range of masks and nasal prongs.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms, two inhalations twice daily and as needed","Candesartan 8 mg daily","Estradiol 50 microgram patch twice weekly"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Alert, speaking in short phrases","Respiratory rate 28/min, heart rate 116/min","SpO2 90% on room air"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate use of oxygen?","o":["Give high-flow oxygen by non-rebreather mask to keep saturation at 100%","Give oxygen only if she becomes drowsy","Start oxygen and titrate to a saturation of 88 to 92%","Start oxygen and titrate to a saturation of 92 to 96%","Withhold oxygen, because it suppresses respiratory drive in asthma"],"a":3,"x":["Over-oxygenation in adults raises the risk of carbon dioxide retention. The aim is a range, not a maximum.","Oxygen is started on the saturation reading, without waiting for drowsiness, which is a late sign.","The lower range of 88 to 92% is for people at risk of carbon dioxide retention, such as those with COPD. She has no such risk.","Correct. Oxygen is started when saturation is under 92% and titrated to 92 to 96%.","A saturation of 90% is treated. Controlled oxygen titrated to a target is safe."],"e":"Problem: saturation of 90% in a severe attack. Task: start and target oxygen correctly. Two targets exist, and the lower one is the distractor. What if she also had COPD with previous carbon dioxide retention? Target 88 to 92%.","k":"Acute asthma: oxygen if saturation is under 92%, titrated to 92 to 96% (88 to 92% if at risk of CO2 retention).","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Managing acute asthma in adults and adolescents in primary care","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Acute asthma"],"c":["rs5-19","rs5-20","rs5-01","rs5-02","rs5-03"],"id":"rs-a31","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#after","n":4,"s":"Tamara Singh, aged 30 years, has recovered in your practice from a moderate asthma attack triggered by a cold. She has been observed for four hours, needs no further reliever and is ready to go home with her partner. Before the attack she used salbutamol alone.","q":"Which FOUR of the following should be part of her discharge plan? Select four.","o":["A course of amoxicillin for the cold","A maintenance inhaled corticosteroid-containing preventer","A review appointment within 3 to 5 days","A written interim action plan, including when to call an ambulance","Instructions to take salbutamol every four hours by the clock for a week","Peak flow readings four times a day for a month","Prednisolone each morning to complete a 5 to 10 day course"],"sc":[0,1,1,1,0,0,1],"x":["Scores 0. A viral trigger does not call for antibiotics.","Scores 1. After an attack she starts maintenance treatment with at least a medium corticosteroid dose. Salbutamol alone is not acceptable.","Scores 1. First follow-up is within 3 to 5 days, with a full review about two weeks later.","Scores 1. Written instructions cover what to do if symptoms return.","Scores 0. Reliever is used as needed, not on a schedule.","Scores 0. Not part of routine post-acute care.","Scores 1. The corticosteroid course is completed, with written timing."],"e":"Four things leave with her: a preventer, the rest of the prednisolone course, a written plan, and an appointment. The attack is also the moment to end reliever-only treatment.","k":"After an attack: preventer, prednisolone course, written plan, review in 3 to 5 days.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Managing acute asthma in adults and adolescents in primary care"],"c":["rs5-26","rs5-27","rs5-28","rs5-29","rs5-30"],"id":"rs-k05","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma in children","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#ages","s":"Oliver Grant, aged 9 months, is brought in by his parents with a third episode of noisy, wheezy breathing in three months. Each has come with a runny nose and lasted about a week. Between episodes he feeds well, is active and is growing along the 50th centile. He was born at term. His father has asthma and asks whether Oliver can have 'a puffer like mine' to keep at home.","b":[{"h":"History","i":["No eczema or food reactions","Attends childcare three days a week","No smokers at home"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Alert, smiling","Respiratory rate 36/min, SpO2 98% on room air","Scattered wheeze, mild subcostal recession","No murmur, no clubbing"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate response to the father's request?","o":["Diagnose asthma and start a daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid","Diagnose asthma and supply salbutamol with a spacer and mask","Explain that asthma is not diagnosed or treated before 12 months","Prescribe montelukast granules as a trial","Supply a home nebuliser with salbutamol for future episodes"],"a":2,"x":["Asthma is not diagnosed under 12 months, and asthma treatment is not prescribed at this age.","A reliever is not prescribed for asthma under 12 months, whatever the family history.","Correct. Wheeze in infants is most often viral bronchiolitis. Asthma is not diagnosed or treated before 12 months, and persistent wheeze is referred.","Montelukast is an alternative preventer for older children whose parents decline corticosteroids. It is not for infants.","Nebulised medicines are not prescribed for home use in children."],"e":"Problem: recurrent viral wheeze in a thriving infant with an asthmatic father. Task: resist the label. The family history makes asthma feel likely, but the age rules it out for now. What if he were 3 years old with the same story and settled within minutes of salbutamol? A reliever, and a preventer trial if episodes are frequent.","k":"No asthma diagnosis and no asthma treatment under 12 months.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), children 1 to 5 years and 6 to 11 years: Diagnosing asthma in children 1 to 5 years; Initial asthma treatment"],"c":["rs6-01","rs6-02","rs6-03","rs6-04","rs6-05"],"id":"rs-a32","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma in children","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#young","s":"Mia Patel, aged 3 years, has had wheeze and cough since she turned two. Her mother reports a night cough that wakes Mia two or three times a week, and wheeze when she runs at the park on most days, even when she has no cold. Salbutamol through a spacer and mask clears the wheeze within minutes. She has had one emergency department visit and has never had oral corticosteroids. She has eczema.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Salbutamol 100 micrograms, two puffs by spacer and mask as needed","Emollient cream daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Growing along the 25th centile","Chest clear today, SpO2 99% on room air","Dry eczema in elbow creases"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add montelukast 4 mg daily","Continue salbutamol alone and review in six months","Refer for spirometry with bronchodilator","Start fluticasone propionate 50 micrograms twice daily by spacer and mask","Start fluticasone propionate-salmeterol 50/25 micrograms twice daily by spacer and mask"],"a":3,"x":["Montelukast is an alternative only if parents decline an inhaled corticosteroid after discussion.","Daytime symptoms more than twice a week and night symptoms more than twice a month both call for a preventer.","Children under 6 cannot perform spirometry reliably. The response to salbutamol is the evidence at this age.","Correct. Frequent symptoms that respond to salbutamol are an indication for a trial of daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid, reviewed at about three months.","A LABA combination is a level 3 option from age 4, not a first preventer for a 3-year-old."],"e":"Problem: a preschooler with frequent, salbutamol-responsive symptoms between colds. Task: decide whether she needs a preventer and which. Her thresholds are met twice over. What if she wheezed only with colds, three times a year, and was well in between? Salbutamol as needed, without a preventer.","k":"Ages 1 to 5: start low-dose inhaled corticosteroid for daytime symptoms twice a week or night symptoms twice a month.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), children 1 to 5 years and 6 to 11 years: Initial asthma treatment for children 1 to 5 years"],"c":["rs6-14","rs6-15","rs6-16","rs6-17","rs6-18"],"id":"rs-a33","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma in children","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#young","s":"Noah Williams, aged 4 years, started a daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid three months ago for frequent wheeze. His parents give every dose through a spacer and their technique is good. Since starting he has had no wheeze, no night cough and no reliever use, including through two colds and the start of the pollen season. He is growing along his centile. His parents ask how long he must stay on the inhaler.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Fluticasone propionate 50 micrograms twice daily by spacer","Salbutamol as needed (not used for 10 weeks)"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Chest clear","Height and weight on the 50th centile"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Consider pausing the inhaled corticosteroid and watching for a return of symptoms","Continue the same dose every day until he is old enough to perform spirometry reliably","Double the dose over winter to prevent flare-ups","Replace the inhaled corticosteroid with daily montelukast","Switch to budesonide-formoterol as needed"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. After a good response through his usual triggers, treatment can be paused to see whether symptoms return. Some preschool children go into remission.","There is no need to continue for two more years without testing whether he still needs it.","He is fully controlled. More corticosteroid adds risk with no gain.","Montelukast is less effective and is not a step-down option.","Budesonide-formoterol as needed is for people aged 12 and over."],"e":"Problem: a preschooler fully controlled after a three-month trial that included colds. Task: decide what the trial has shown. A good response through typical triggers is the cue to test whether treatment is still needed. What if symptoms had continued despite good technique? Check triggers at home, then question the diagnosis.","k":"Preschool preventer trial: review at 3 months; if well through usual triggers, consider a pause.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), children 1 to 5 years and 6 to 11 years: Initial asthma treatment for children 1 to 5 years"],"c":["rs6-14","rs6-15","rs6-16","rs6-17","rs6-18"],"id":"rs-a34","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma in children","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#redflags","s":"Zara Ahmed, aged 7 years, has had a cough every day for seven weeks since a cold. Her mother describes it as 'chesty and rattly', worst in the morning, and Zara sometimes swallows what she coughs up. There is no wheeze, no breathlessness with sport and no night waking. A salbutamol inhaler from an after-hours clinic made no difference. She is growing well and has had no previous chest problems.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Salbutamol two puffs as needed for two weeks, now stopped"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Afebrile, SpO2 99% on room air","Coarse crackles that clear with coughing","No clubbing, no chest deformity","Weight on the 50th centile"]}],"q":"What is the MOST likely diagnosis?","o":["Cough-variant asthma needing a preventer","Habit cough","Inhaled foreign body","Pertussis","Protracted bacterial bronchitis"],"a":4,"x":["An isolated moist cough with no wheeze or breathlessness, and no response to salbutamol, makes asthma less likely.","A habit cough is dry, present only in the daytime, and stops in sleep.","This usually starts abruptly and gives one-sided signs.","Pertussis gives bouts of dry coughing, often ending in a whoop or vomiting, not a daily wet cough.","Correct. A persistent wet cough in an otherwise well child, with no features of asthma, points to protracted bacterial bronchitis."],"e":"Problem: seven weeks of daily wet cough in a well child who has been given an asthma reliever. Task: recognise that cough alone is not asthma. The moist quality is the deciding fact. What if she also had clubbing and poor growth? Cystic fibrosis and bronchiectasis would need excluding.","k":"A child's persistent wet cough without wheeze is protracted bacterial bronchitis until shown otherwise, not asthma.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Signs and symptoms that suggest an alternative diagnosis in children"],"c":["rs6-09","rs6-10","rs6-11","rs6-12","rs6-13"],"id":"rs-a35","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma in children","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#levels","s":"Jack Morrison, aged 8 years, had asthma confirmed on spirometry six months ago and uses salbutamol as needed. Over the past two months he has coughed at night about once a week and needs his reliever at school sport three times a week. He had one course of prednisolone for a flare-up in winter. His technique with a spacer is good. His older brother, aged 14, uses budesonide-formoterol as needed, and his mother asks whether Jack can have the same.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Salbutamol 100 micrograms, two to four puffs by spacer as needed","Intranasal mometasone in spring"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Chest clear, SpO2 99% on room air","Height on the 60th centile"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate change to his treatment?","o":["Add a daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid, with salbutamol as needed","Add daily montelukast, with salbutamol as needed","Change to budesonide-formoterol as needed, like his brother","Change to fluticasone propionate-salmeterol 50/25 micrograms, two puffs twice daily","Continue salbutamol alone and add a peak flow diary"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. At 6 to 11 years, frequent symptoms or a flare-up needing oral corticosteroids in the past year call for a daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid, reviewed in 8 to 12 weeks.","Montelukast is an alternative only when parents decline an inhaled corticosteroid.","As-needed budesonide-formoterol is recommended from age 12. Under 12 the reliever remains salbutamol.","A LABA combination is level 3. He has not yet tried level 2.","His symptoms and flare-up already meet the threshold for a preventer."],"e":"Problem: an 8-year-old with weekly night cough, reliever use three times a week and a recent prednisolone course. Task: step up correctly for his age. His brother's regimen is the distractor. What if Jack were 12? As-needed budesonide-formoterol, or maintenance and reliever therapy, would be the choice.","k":"Under 12: level 2 is daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid plus salbutamol. AIR and MART start at 12.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), children 1 to 5 years and 6 to 11 years: Treatment levels and Initial asthma treatment for children 6 to 11 years"],"c":["rs6-20","rs6-21","rs6-22","rs6-23","rs6-24"],"id":"rs-a36","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma in children","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#never","s":"Chloe Bennett, aged 5 years, has asthma treated with a daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid and salbutamol by spacer. Her grandmother, who minds her after kindergarten, finds the spacer fiddly. She asks for salbutamol syrup, which her own children had, or a nebuliser machine to keep at her house. Chloe's asthma is well controlled, with reliever use about once a fortnight.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Fluticasone propionate 50 micrograms twice daily by spacer","Salbutamol two puffs by spacer as needed"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Chest clear","Chloe shows good technique with her spacer and mouthpiece"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate response?","o":["Prescribe a home nebuliser with salbutamol nebules","Prescribe salbutamol syrup for use at her grandmother's house after kindergarten each day","Replace both inhalers with daily montelukast","Stop the preventer so that only one inhaler is needed","Teach her grandmother to use the spacer, and give her a copy of the action plan"],"a":4,"x":["Nebulised medicines are not prescribed for home use in children.","Oral salbutamol is not recommended for asthma.","Montelukast is less effective and is reserved for families who decline corticosteroids.","She is well controlled because of the preventer. Removing it for convenience invites flare-ups.","Correct. A pressurised inhaler and spacer remains the device. Everyone who cares for the child is taught to use it and holds a copy of the plan."],"e":"Problem: a carer who wants an older, easier-seeming treatment. Task: keep the right device and fix the real barrier, which is training. What if Chloe herself could not manage the mouthpiece? Add a mask to the spacer.","k":"Children with asthma: spacer, not nebuliser or syrup. Teach every carer and share the action plan.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), children 1 to 5 years and 6 to 11 years: Initial asthma treatment"],"c":["rs6-27","rs6-28","rs6-01","rs6-02","rs6-03"],"id":"rs-a37","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Asthma in children","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#redflags","n":3,"s":"Leo Fischer, aged 6 years, is referred to you by a locum as 'asthma not responding to treatment' after three months on an inhaled corticosteroid.","q":"Which THREE of the following findings would MOST strongly suggest a diagnosis other than asthma? Select three.","o":["Eczema in the elbow creases","Finger clubbing","His mother has hay fever","Night cough that is worse in the early morning","Symptoms present since birth","Wheeze after running that eases with salbutamol","Wheeze heard only over the right lower chest"],"sc":[0,1,0,0,1,0,1],"x":["Scores 0. Atopy raises the probability of asthma.","Scores 1. Clubbing points to cystic fibrosis, bronchiectasis, primary ciliary dyskinesia or immunodeficiency.","Scores 0. A family history of allergy raises the probability of asthma.","Scores 0. This pattern is typical of asthma.","Scores 1. Symptoms from birth suggest cystic fibrosis, a structural abnormality or ciliary disease.","Scores 0. Exercise wheeze that responds to a reliever supports asthma.","Scores 1. Focal or one-sided wheeze suggests an inhaled foreign body or a structural lesion and needs urgent attention."],"e":"When a child's 'asthma' does not respond, the first question is whether it is asthma. Clubbing, onset at birth and focal wheeze each point away from it, and each needs investigation or referral.","k":"Clubbing, symptoms from birth and focal wheeze point away from asthma in a child.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Signs and symptoms that suggest an alternative diagnosis in children"],"c":["rs6-09","rs6-10","rs6-11","rs6-12","rs6-13"],"id":"rs-k06","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Acute asthma in children","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#dose","s":"Ruby Chen, aged 3 years, is carried into your practice by her father with wheeze and fast breathing that began this morning during a cold. She has had two previous wheezy episodes that settled with salbutamol. She is clinging to her father and crying in short bursts, managing two or three words at a time. She weighs 15 kg.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Salbutamol two puffs by spacer and mask, given twice at home today"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Alert, distressed","Respiratory rate 48/min, heart rate 150/min","SpO2 93% on room air","Widespread wheeze with subcostal recession and tracheal tug"]}],"q":"In addition to calling an ambulance, what is the MOST appropriate immediate treatment?","o":["Nebulised salbutamol 5 mg, driven by air","Oral prednisolone 15 mg, then reassess in 30 minutes","Salbutamol 12 puffs through a spacer and mask, repeated every 20 minutes","Salbutamol 2 puffs through a spacer and mask, repeated every 4 hours","Salbutamol 6 puffs through a spacer and mask, repeated every 20 minutes"],"a":4,"x":["A nebuliser is for a child who cannot breathe through a spacer and mask, and the dose under 6 years is 2.5 mg.","A corticosteroid may follow if she needs admission, but the bronchodilator comes first and is not delayed.","Twelve puffs is the severe dose from age 6. Under 6 the dose is 6 puffs.","Two puffs is her usual home reliever dose, and a four-hourly interval is far too long for a severe attack.","Correct. Recession, tracheal tug, broken speech and a saturation of 93% make this severe. Under 6 years the dose is 6 puffs, at least every 20 minutes for the first hour."],"e":"Problem: a 3-year-old with a severe attack. Task: pick the age-correct dose and interval. Two of the options are right for an older child. What if she were 7 years old? Twelve puffs every 20 minutes.","k":"Severe acute asthma: 6 puffs under 6 years, 12 puffs from 6 years, every 20 minutes for the first hour.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Managing acute asthma in children in primary care: children 1 to 5 years, immediate treatment table"],"c":["rs7-04","rs7-05","rs7-06","rs7-07","rs7-08"],"id":"rs-a38","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Acute asthma in children","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#steroids","s":"Archie Dunn, aged 4 years, presents with wheeze on the second day of a cold. He has had three similar episodes, each settling with salbutamol. On arrival he was running around the waiting room, talking in sentences, with a saturation of 97% and mild wheeze. He was given four puffs of salbutamol through a spacer. Thirty minutes later his chest is clear. His mother asks whether he should have 'the steroid syrup' he was given in hospital last year.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Salbutamol two puffs by spacer as needed"]},{"h":"Examination findings (after treatment)","i":["Playing, talking freely","Respiratory rate 26/min, SpO2 98% on room air","Chest clear, no recession"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate advice about oral corticosteroids?","o":["Give a single dose of dexamethasone","Give prednisolone 1 mg/kg daily for 3 days","Give prednisolone 1 mg/kg daily for 5 to 10 days","Give prednisolone only if he wheezes again tonight","They are not needed for this episode"],"a":4,"x":["Dexamethasone is an alternative when a corticosteroid is indicated. Here none is.","This is the right dose and duration when a corticosteroid is indicated. In a preschooler that means an attack needing hospital admission.","Five to ten days is the adult course. Children have three days, when a course is needed at all.","Leaving a parent to start corticosteroids for a recurrence overnight is not the advice. Returning symptoms need reassessment.","Correct. In children aged 1 to 5, systemic corticosteroids are started only if hospital admission is required. A mild episode that clears with salbutamol does not need them."],"e":"Problem: a mild preschool wheeze that cleared with one dose, and a parent expecting steroids. Task: know who benefits. In preschoolers with mild to moderate wheeze, corticosteroids may add nothing. What if he were 8 years old with a moderate attack? Prednisolone 1 mg/kg for 3 days.","k":"Ages 1 to 5: oral corticosteroids only if the child needs hospital admission.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Managing acute asthma in children in primary care: children 1 to 5 years","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Acute asthma"],"c":["rs7-14","rs7-15","rs7-16","rs7-17","rs7-18"],"id":"rs-a39","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Acute asthma in children","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#steroids","s":"Ella Robinson, aged 9 years, has asthma treated with a daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid. She presents after school with wheeze that began at lunchtime during cross-country training. On arrival she spoke in full sentences but was breathing fast, with a saturation of 95% and widespread wheeze. Her attack was graded moderate. She has had two doses of eight puffs of salbutamol through a spacer and is improving. She weighs 30 kg.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Fluticasone propionate 50 micrograms twice daily by spacer","Salbutamol by spacer as needed"]},{"h":"Examination findings (after two doses)","i":["Respiratory rate 22/min, heart rate 104/min","SpO2 97% on room air","Scattered wheeze, no recession"]}],"q":"Which corticosteroid prescription is MOST appropriate?","o":["Dexamethasone 8 mg orally each morning for 5 days","No corticosteroid, as she is improving","Prednisolone 30 mg each morning for 3 days","Prednisolone 30 mg each morning for 7 days, then taper","Prednisolone 50 mg each morning for 5 days"],"a":2,"x":["Dexamethasone is an accepted alternative, but as 0.6 mg/kg (maximum 16 mg) in a single dose, repeated once the next day if needed. It is not given as a five-day course.","At 6 to 11 years, corticosteroids are omitted only when the attack is graded mild at first assessment. Hers was moderate.","Correct. Prednisolone 1 mg/kg, to a maximum of 50 mg, each morning for 3 days: 30 mg for a 30 kg child.","Seven days with a taper is longer than a child needs. The course is 3 days with no taper.","Fifty milligrams is the adult dose and the paediatric ceiling, not the dose for a 30 kg child."],"e":"Problem: a moderate attack in a school-age child who is responding. Task: prescribe the corticosteroid by weight and for the right time. What if her attack had been mild at first assessment? No corticosteroid would be needed.","k":"Child's acute asthma: prednisolone 1 mg/kg (maximum 50 mg) each morning for 3 days.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Managing acute asthma in children in primary care: children 6 to 11 years","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Acute asthma"],"c":["rs7-14","rs7-15","rs7-16","rs7-17","rs7-18"],"id":"rs-a40","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Acute asthma in children","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#severity","s":"Samuel Okoye, aged 5 years, has been in your treatment room for 25 minutes with a severe asthma attack. He has had two doses of salbutamol through a spacer and mask, and oxygen by mask. An ambulance is ten minutes away. His mother says he 'seems to be settling', because he has stopped crying and is lying still on the bed. You re-examine him.","b":[{"h":"Observations on arrival","i":["Crying, speaking in single words","Respiratory rate 52/min, heart rate 158/min","SpO2 91% on room air","Loud wheeze with marked recession"]},{"h":"Observations now","i":["Eyes closed, rouses briefly to voice","Respiratory rate 18/min, heart rate 70/min","SpO2 87% on oxygen 5 L/min","Very quiet breath sounds, lips dusky"]}],"q":"How should his current condition be classified?","o":["Improving, as his respiratory rate and heart rate have fallen","Life-threatening","Mild","Severe, unchanged from arrival","Sleeping normally after exertion"],"a":1,"x":["The falls in breathing and heart rate reflect exhaustion, not recovery. His saturation has dropped despite oxygen.","Correct. Reduced consciousness, a silent chest, cyanosis, slow breathing, bradycardia and a saturation under 90% are each life-threatening features.","He meets none of the criteria: he is not alert, not vocalising, and his saturation is far below 94%.","He has moved from severe to life-threatening. New drowsiness and a quiet chest mark the change.","A child with a saturation of 87% and dusky lips is not simply asleep."],"e":"Problem: a child who has gone quiet during treatment. Task: read the quiet correctly. Every feature that looks like calm is a feature of exhaustion. Bradycardia may come just before respiratory arrest. What if he were alert, pink and chatting with a saturation of 96%? That would be improvement.","k":"A drowsy child with a quiet chest, slow pulse and falling saturation has life-threatening asthma.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Secondary severity assessment of acute asthma in children 1 to 5 in primary care"],"c":["rs7-01","rs7-02","rs7-03","rs7-04","rs7-05"],"id":"rs-a41","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Acute asthma in children","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#oxygen","s":"Isla McKenzie, aged 7 years, is brought to a rural practice with an asthma attack. The first dose of salbutamol is being given through a spacer and an ambulance has been called. She is alert, speaking a few words at a time, with recession. Her saturation on room air is 90%. The practice has oxygen, simple paediatric masks and nasal prongs. She has no other medical conditions.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Salbutamol by spacer as needed","No preventer"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Respiratory rate 38/min, heart rate 136/min","SpO2 90% on room air","Widespread wheeze"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate use of oxygen?","o":["Give oxygen by simple mask at 15 L/min, with the aim of reaching a saturation of 100%","Give oxygen by simple mask at 5 L/min, titrated to a saturation of 92 to 96%","Give oxygen only if her saturation falls below 85%","Titrate oxygen to a saturation of 88 to 92%","Withhold oxygen until salbutamol has had 20 minutes to work"],"a":1,"x":["The aim is a target range, not a maximum, and a simple mask is run at 5 L/min.","Correct. Oxygen starts when saturation is under 92% on room air, by simple mask at 5 L/min or nasal prongs at 3 L/min, aiming for 92 to 96%.","The threshold for starting is under 92%, not 85%.","The 88 to 92% range is for adults at risk of carbon dioxide retention. It is not the target for a child with asthma.","Oxygen is given alongside the bronchodilator, not after it."],"e":"Problem: a saturation of 90% in a child with a severe attack. Task: start, deliver and target oxygen. What if her saturation were 93%? The attack is still severe, but oxygen is not yet required.","k":"Children with acute asthma: oxygen if under 92%, simple mask 5 L/min or prongs 3 L/min, target 92 to 96%.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Managing acute asthma in children in primary care: children 6 to 11 years"],"c":["rs7-12","rs7-13","rs7-01","rs7-02","rs7-03"],"id":"rs-a42","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Acute asthma in children","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#after","n":3,"s":"Harper Lewis, aged 6 years, has been treated in your practice for a moderate asthma attack. Two hours after her last dose of salbutamol she is playing, speaking normally, and has a saturation of 98% with a clear chest. Her parents are keen to take her home.","q":"Which THREE of the following should happen before or as she leaves? Select three.","o":["Arrange a review appointment within 3 to 5 days","Give written instructions on reliever, prednisolone and when to call an ambulance","Keep her under observation until 4 hours have passed since she settled","Lend the family a nebuliser for the next week","Order a chest X-ray to exclude pneumonia","Prescribe amoxicillin in case of bacterial infection","Tell the parents to give salbutamol every 3 hours by the clock for 3 days"],"sc":[1,1,1,0,0,0,0],"x":["Scores 1. First follow-up is within 3 to 5 days, with a full review about two weeks later.","Scores 1. An interim written plan goes home with every child.","Scores 1. Observation continues for at least 4 hours after the breathing has settled. She is at 2 hours.","Scores 0. Nebulisers are not prescribed or recommended for home use.","Scores 0. Imaging is not routine after an attack that has responded.","Scores 0. Antibiotics have no routine role in acute asthma.","Scores 0. Reliever is given as needed, not on a schedule."],"e":"Three things stand between a recovered child and the car park: enough observation time, a written plan, and a booked review.","k":"After a child's attack: observe 4 hours, give a written plan, review in 3 to 5 days.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Managing acute asthma in children in primary care"],"c":["rs7-21","rs7-22","rs7-01","rs7-02","rs7-03"],"id":"rs-k07","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"COPD: long-term management","nt":"rs-copd#dx","s":"Kevin Doyle, aged 58 years, is a truck driver who attends for a licence medical. On questioning he admits to a cough with clear sputum most mornings for three years and says he now stops for breath on the stairs to his flat. He has smoked 20 cigarettes a day since he was 17. He has had two courses of antibiotics for 'bronchitis' in the past year. A chest X-ray six months ago was reported as normal.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Perindopril 5 mg daily","Atorvastatin 40 mg daily","Pantoprazole 40 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 96% on room air","Chest: quiet breath sounds, no crackles","BMI 29 kg/m2, no ankle oedema"]}],"q":"Which investigation is MOST appropriate to make the diagnosis?","o":["CT scan of the chest","Peak expiratory flow diary for two weeks","Repeat chest X-ray with a lateral view, to look for hyperinflation and flattened diaphragms","Serum alpha-1 antitrypsin level","Spirometry before and after a bronchodilator"],"a":4,"x":["CT can show emphysema but is not required for diagnosis, and cannot confirm airflow obstruction.","Peak flow does not diagnose COPD and is not useful for monitoring it.","A chest X-ray helps exclude other causes. A normal film does not exclude COPD, and no film can diagnose it.","Alpha-1 antitrypsin is tested when COPD is diagnosed before 40 or runs in the family. The diagnosis itself comes first.","Correct. COPD cannot be diagnosed without spirometry. A post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC under 0.7 confirms it."],"e":"Problem: a 58-year-old smoker with cough, sputum, breathlessness and recurrent chest infections. Task: choose the test that makes the diagnosis. The normal X-ray is the distractor. What if he were 37 with the same picture? Spirometry first, then alpha-1 antitrypsin testing and referral.","k":"COPD is diagnosed on spirometry: post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC under 0.7.","src":["Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Handbook (2024)","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Stable chronic obstructive pulmonary disease"],"c":["rs8-01","rs8-02","rs8-04","rs8-05","rs8-06"],"id":"rs-a43","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"COPD: long-term management","nt":"rs-copd#steps","s":"Margaret Liu, aged 67 years, has moderate COPD diagnosed two years ago, with an FEV1 of 55% predicted. She stopped smoking at diagnosis and completed pulmonary rehabilitation last year. She has had no exacerbations, but in recent months she has to stop halfway to the shops to catch her breath. She takes her inhaler every morning, and you confirm her technique is correct. Her vaccinations are up to date.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Tiotropium 18 micrograms inhaled once daily","Salbutamol two puffs as needed, used most days","Amlodipine 5 mg daily","Calcium and vitamin D daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 95% on room air","Reduced breath sounds, no wheeze or crackles","No ankle oedema"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add a long-acting beta agonist to her long-acting muscarinic antagonist","Add an inhaled corticosteroid to her long-acting muscarinic antagonist","Add regular ipratropium four times daily to her current inhalers","Add theophylline 200 mg twice daily and check a serum level after one week of treatment","Start prednisolone 5 mg daily"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. Persisting breathlessness on one long-acting bronchodilator is treated by adding the other. LAMA with LABA comes before any corticosteroid.","An inhaled corticosteroid is added for exacerbations with ongoing symptoms on dual bronchodilators. She has had none, and it raises pneumonia risk.","Ipratropium is not combined with a long-acting muscarinic antagonist.","Theophylline is not recommended for COPD.","Long-term oral corticosteroids are not used for maintenance. They cause bone loss and muscle weakness."],"e":"Problem: breathlessness on a single long-acting bronchodilator, with adherence, technique and non-drug measures all in place. Task: take the next step in order. The absence of exacerbations is what rules out the corticosteroid. What if she had been admitted with an exacerbation this year and was already on both bronchodilators? Triple therapy.","k":"COPD steps: reliever, then LAMA or LABA, then both, then add inhaled corticosteroid for exacerbations.","src":["Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Handbook (2024)","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Stable chronic obstructive pulmonary disease"],"c":["rs8-16","rs8-17","rs8-18","rs8-19","rs8-20"],"id":"rs-a44","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"COPD: long-term management","nt":"rs-copd#steps","s":"Brian Foster, aged 71 years, has COPD with an FEV1 of 44% predicted. In the past 12 months he has had two exacerbations treated at home with prednisolone and antibiotics. Between them he remains breathless walking to his letterbox. He stopped smoking five years ago and attends a maintenance exercise class. His inhaler technique is good and pharmacy records show regular dispensing. He has not had pneumonia.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Umeclidinium-vilanterol 62.5/25 micrograms, one inhalation daily","Salbutamol as needed","Metoprolol 50 mg twice daily","Rivaroxaban 20 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 93% on room air","Hyperinflated chest, quiet breath sounds","No peripheral oedema"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add an inhaled corticosteroid, as a single triple-therapy inhaler","Add oral prednisolone 10 mg daily for three months, then reassess his exacerbation rate","Change to an inhaled corticosteroid alone","Continue current inhalers and review in 12 months","Stop metoprolol, as beta blockers worsen COPD"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. Two moderate exacerbations in 12 months with significant symptoms on LAMA with LABA meet both conditions for adding an inhaled corticosteroid.","Maintenance oral corticosteroids are not recommended in COPD.","An inhaled corticosteroid alone is not a COPD treatment. The bronchodilators stay.","He meets the threshold for stepping up now. Each exacerbation costs lung function.","Cardioselective beta blockers are safe in COPD when there is a cardiac indication."],"e":"Problem: two exacerbations and ongoing symptoms on dual bronchodilators. Task: recognise the threshold for triple therapy. Both parts of the rule are present. What if he had symptoms but no exacerbations? No corticosteroid: review technique, rehabilitation and other causes of breathlessness.","k":"Add inhaled corticosteroid in COPD for 2 moderate or 1 hospitalised exacerbation in a year, with symptoms on LAMA plus LABA.","src":["Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Handbook (2024)","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Stable chronic obstructive pulmonary disease"],"c":["rs8-16","rs8-17","rs8-18","rs8-19","rs8-20"],"id":"rs-a45","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"COPD: long-term management","nt":"rs-copd#refer","s":"Daniel Kovac, aged 38 years, is a carpenter who presents with two years of breathlessness on exertion and a daily cough. He smoked ten cigarettes a day for eight years and stopped at 30. His father died of 'emphysema' at 52. Spirometry at your practice shows a post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC of 0.58 and an FEV1 of 61% predicted, with an FEV1 rise of 4% after salbutamol.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Salbutamol as needed, supplied by a locum last month","No regular medicines"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 96% on room air","Reduced breath sounds at both bases","No clubbing"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange a sleep study","Reassure him that this degree of obstruction is expected in an ex-smoker and repeat spirometry in a year","Refer to a respiratory physician and test for alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","Start an inhaled corticosteroid for presumed asthma","Start long-term azithromycin"],"a":2,"x":["Nothing in the history suggests a sleep disorder.","An eight pack-year history does not explain this obstruction at 38. COPD before 40 always needs explanation.","Correct. COPD under 40, with a parent who died young of emphysema, suggests alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency. Young patients are referred.","A 4% bronchodilator response and fixed obstruction do not support asthma.","A long-term macrolide is a specialist option for frequent exacerbations. He has had none."],"e":"Problem: fixed airflow obstruction at 38 with a light smoking history and a family history. Task: recognise early-onset COPD. Age is the deciding fact. What if he were 62 with a 40 pack-year history? Ordinary COPD, managed in general practice.","k":"COPD before age 40: think alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency and refer.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Stable chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Handbook (2024)"],"c":["rs8-30","rs8-31","rs8-01","rs8-02","rs8-03"],"id":"rs-a46","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"COPD: long-term management","nt":"rs-copd#oxygen","s":"Shirley Watson, aged 74 years, has severe COPD and attends for a routine review. She has not had an exacerbation for three months. She stopped smoking eight years ago. She is breathless dressing and has noticed her ankles swelling in the evenings. Her inhaler technique is correct and she has completed pulmonary rehabilitation twice.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Fluticasone furoate-umeclidinium-vilanterol, one inhalation daily","Salbutamol as needed","Furosemide 20 mg daily","Alendronate 70 mg weekly"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 88% on room air at rest, repeated after 10 minutes","Pitting oedema to mid-shin","Raised jugular venous pressure","Quiet chest, no crackles"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add theophylline","Increase furosemide and review in three months","Prescribe a portable oxygen cylinder to use at home whenever she feels breathless","Refer for arterial blood gases and assessment for long-term oxygen therapy","Start a course of prednisolone and amoxicillin"],"a":3,"x":["Theophylline is not recommended for COPD.","The oedema may improve, but the cause, which is persistent low oxygen with right heart strain, would be left untreated.","Oxygen 'as needed' for breathlessness is not the indication. Long-term oxygen is prescribed on blood gas criteria and used for more than 18 hours a day.","Correct. A resting saturation of 90% or less while stable calls for assessment. With signs of right heart failure she qualifies at a PaO2 of up to 59 mmHg.","She is stable, with no change in cough, sputum or breathlessness. This is not an exacerbation."],"e":"Problem: resting saturation of 88% and right heart failure in stable severe COPD. Task: recognise the indication for long-term oxygen assessment. Stability matters: testing waits four weeks after an exacerbation, and she is three months clear. What if she still smoked? Home oxygen is contraindicated because of fire risk.","k":"Stable COPD with resting saturation of 90% or less: arterial gases and long-term oxygen assessment.","src":["Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Handbook (2024)","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Stable chronic obstructive pulmonary disease"],"c":["rs8-26","rs8-27","rs8-28","rs8-29","rs8-01"],"id":"rs-a47","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"COPD: long-term management","nt":"rs-copd#nondrug","s":"Gary Simmons, aged 60 years, was diagnosed with mild COPD last month, with an FEV1 of 72% predicted. He smokes 25 cigarettes a day, has his first within ten minutes of waking and has tried to quit 'cold turkey' three times. He gets breathless hurrying but has had no exacerbations. He asks which of the things you have discussed will make the most difference to how long he lives.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Salbutamol as needed, used twice a week","Irbesartan 150 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 97% on room air","Chest clear","BMI 26 kg/m2"]}],"q":"Which intervention has the GREATEST effect on his survival?","o":["Flu vaccine","Long-acting muscarinic antagonist inhaler","Pulmonary rehabilitation","Smoking cessation","Triple inhaled therapy with a corticosteroid and two long-acting bronchodilators"],"a":3,"x":["Vaccination reduces exacerbations and is recommended, but it does not match the effect of stopping smoking.","Bronchodilators relieve symptoms and reduce exacerbations. Their effect on mortality is unclear.","Rehabilitation improves exercise capacity and quality of life. It is recommended for symptomatic patients.","Correct. Smoking cessation is the single most important intervention. It limits lung damage and reduces deaths.","Triple therapy is for exacerbations despite dual bronchodilators. He has mild disease with none."],"e":"Problem: a newly diagnosed heavy smoker asking what matters most. Task: rank the interventions. His dependence is high, so the plan is counselling with a nicotine patch plus a fast-acting form, or varenicline. What if he had already quit and had resting hypoxaemia? Long-term oxygen would be the intervention that improves survival.","k":"Smoking cessation is the most important intervention in COPD.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Stable chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Handbook (2024)"],"c":["rs8-11","rs8-12","rs8-13","rs8-14","rs8-15"],"id":"rs-a48","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"COPD: long-term management","nt":"rs-copd#severity","s":"Patricia Nolan, aged 64 years, has COPD confirmed three years ago. She returns for results of her annual spirometry, performed when she was well, eight weeks after her last chest infection. She becomes breathless walking on level ground at her own pace and had two exacerbations this year, each treated with prednisolone. She stopped smoking two years ago.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Tiotropium-olodaterol, two puffs daily","Salbutamol as needed","Sertraline 50 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 94% on room air","Reduced breath sounds"]}],"tb":{"h":["Post-bronchodilator spirometry","Result","Percent predicted"],"r":[["FEV1","1.05 L","47%"],["FVC","2.60 L","88%"],["FEV1/FVC","0.40",""]]},"q":"How is the severity of her COPD BEST classified?","o":["Mild","Moderate","Severe","Unable to be graded, because the test was done after an exacerbation","Very severe, with a need for home oxygen"],"a":1,"x":["Mild COPD has an FEV1 of 60 to 80% predicted and breathlessness only on moderate exertion.","Correct. An FEV1 of 40 to 59% predicted, breathlessness on the flat and exacerbations needing corticosteroids fit moderate COPD.","Severe COPD has an FEV1 under 40% predicted and breathlessness on minimal exertion.","Grading waits at least four weeks after an exacerbation. She was tested at eight weeks.","Home oxygen is decided by blood gases, not by FEV1. Her saturation is 94%."],"e":"Problem: an annual spirometry result with a symptom and exacerbation history. Task: grade severity. All three strands agree: FEV1 47%, breathless on the flat, exacerbations needing prednisolone. What if her FEV1 were 36% predicted? Severe, with review at least twice a year.","k":"COPD severity by FEV1: mild 60 to 80%, moderate 40 to 59%, severe under 40%.","src":["Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Handbook (2024)","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Stable chronic obstructive pulmonary disease"],"c":["rs8-07","rs8-08","rs8-09","rs8-10","rs8-01"],"id":"rs-a49","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"COPD: long-term management","nt":"rs-copd#refer","n":4,"s":"You are reviewing the care of Ronald Hughes, aged 70 years, who has moderate COPD and attends once a year. He stopped smoking three years ago.","q":"Which FOUR of the following should be part of his annual COPD review? Select four.","o":["Bronchial provocation test","Check of inhaler technique and adherence","CT scan of the chest","Review and update of his written action plan","Serum theophylline level","Spirometry","Vaccination status, including influenza and pneumococcal"],"sc":[0,1,0,1,0,1,1],"x":["Scores 0. This is a test for uncertain asthma.","Scores 1. Most people use their device incorrectly, and this is checked at every visit.","Scores 0. CT is not a routine monitoring test.","Scores 1. Every person with COPD has a written plan, reviewed at least yearly.","Scores 0. Theophylline is not recommended for COPD.","Scores 1. Spirometry is repeated every year in everyone with COPD.","Scores 1. Influenza each year, pneumococcal and COVID-19."],"e":"The yearly review covers smoking, symptoms, exacerbations, technique, adherence, the action plan, vaccination, mood and weight, with spirometry each year. In severe disease the review is twice a year and adds oxygen saturation, signs of right heart failure and advance care planning.","k":"Annual COPD review: spirometry, technique and adherence, action plan, vaccination.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Stable chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Handbook (2024)"],"c":["rs8-30","rs8-31","rs8-01","rs8-02","rs8-03"],"id":"rs-k08","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"COPD: exacerbations","nt":"rs-copd-exac#abx","s":"Dennis Clarke, aged 68 years, has moderate COPD. For two days he has been more breathless than usual and has used his reliever five or six times a day. His cough is unchanged, and he brings up a small amount of white sputum each morning, as he always does. He has had no fever. His granddaughter, who stayed last weekend, had a cold. He is eating, sleeping and walking around the house.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Umeclidinium-vilanterol, one inhalation daily","Salbutamol as needed","Rosuvastatin 10 mg daily","Aspirin 100 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 36.9 degrees, respiratory rate 20/min, heart rate 88/min","SpO2 93% on room air (usually 94%)","Scattered wheeze, no crackles","No ankle oedema"]}],"q":"In addition to regular salbutamol through a spacer, which treatment is MOST appropriate?","o":["Amoxicillin 500 mg every 8 hours for 5 days","Amoxicillin with clavulanate 875+125 mg twice daily for 7 days, to cover resistant organisms","Doxycycline 100 mg daily for 5 days and prednisolone 50 mg daily for 5 days","Prednisolone 30 to 50 mg daily for 5 days","Roxithromycin 300 mg daily for 5 days"],"a":3,"x":["Antibiotics are considered when sputum becomes more purulent together with more volume or more breathlessness. His sputum has not changed.","This is not a first-line antibiotic for an exacerbation, and he does not meet the criteria for any antibiotic.","The prednisolone is right. The antibiotic is not indicated without a change in sputum purulence.","Correct. An exacerbation not settling with bronchodilator alone is treated with a 5-day course of prednisolone. A likely viral trigger with unchanged sputum does not need an antibiotic.","Macrolides are not recommended as initial antibiotic treatment, and no antibiotic is indicated here."],"e":"Problem: more breathlessness and reliever use, with unchanged white sputum after contact with a cold. Task: decide what to add. Purulence is the trigger for antibiotics, and it is absent. What if his sputum had turned green and doubled in volume? Add amoxicillin or doxycycline for 5 days.","k":"COPD exacerbation: prednisolone if bronchodilator is not enough; antibiotics only with more purulent sputum plus more volume or dyspnoea.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), COPD exacerbations"],"c":["rs9-08","rs9-09","rs9-10","rs9-11","rs9-12"],"id":"rs-a50","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"COPD: exacerbations","nt":"rs-copd-exac#abx","s":"Beverley Hart, aged 72 years, has moderate COPD with an FEV1 of 52% predicted. For three days her sputum has changed from clear to thick and green, there is about twice as much of it, and she is more breathless. She has had one exacerbation in the past two years and no hospital admissions. She has no drug allergies. She is managing at home and her husband is with her.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Tiotropium 18 micrograms daily","Salbutamol as needed, now every 3 to 4 hours","Prednisolone 37.5 mg daily, started yesterday under her action plan","Atenolol 25 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 37.6 degrees, respiratory rate 22/min","SpO2 92% on room air","Coarse crackles that clear with coughing, scattered wheeze"]}],"q":"Which antibiotic regimen is MOST appropriate?","o":["Amoxicillin 1 g every 8 hours for 7 days","Amoxicillin 500 mg every 8 hours for 5 days","Amoxicillin with clavulanate 875+125 mg every 12 hours for 10 days","Azithromycin 500 mg daily for 3 days","Cefalexin 500 mg every 6 hours for 5 days"],"a":1,"x":["One gram every 8 hours is the dose for pneumonia. Exacerbation doses are lower, and the course is 5 days.","Correct. More purulent sputum with more volume and more breathlessness meets the criteria. Amoxicillin 500 mg every 8 hours, or doxycycline 100 mg daily, for 5 days.","This is reserved for severe COPD that has not responded at 48 to 72 hours, and then for 5 days.","Macrolides are not recommended for initial treatment of an exacerbation.","Cephalosporins are not recommended for initial treatment. They are no more effective and are broader than needed."],"e":"Problem: an exacerbation with purulent sputum, more of it, and more breathlessness. Task: choose the drug, dose and duration. Crackles that clear with coughing argue against pneumonia. What if the crackles persisted with rigors and a fast pulse? Chest X-ray, and treat as pneumonia if confirmed.","k":"COPD exacerbation antibiotic: amoxicillin 500 mg 8-hourly or doxycycline 100 mg daily, for 5 days.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations"],"c":["rs9-08","rs9-09","rs9-10","rs9-11","rs9-12"],"id":"rs-a51","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"COPD: exacerbations","nt":"rs-copd-exac#oxygen","s":"Leonard Marsh, aged 76 years, has severe COPD and was found breathless at home by his daughter. Paramedics gave oxygen at 8 L/min by mask during the 30-minute trip to your rural hospital. On arrival he is drowsy, rousing to voice, with a flushed face and a bounding pulse. His usual saturation at the practice is 91%. He has not been on home oxygen.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Fluticasone furoate-umeclidinium-vilanterol, one inhalation daily","Salbutamol as needed","Furosemide 40 mg daily","Apixaban 5 mg twice daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Respiratory rate 12/min, heart rate 104/min","SpO2 99% on oxygen 8 L/min by mask","Widespread wheeze, poor air entry"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Continue oxygen at 8 L/min, as his saturation is now satisfactory","Give oxygen-driven nebulised salbutamol","Increase oxygen to 15 L/min by non-rebreather mask until he is more alert","Reduce oxygen and titrate to a saturation of 88 to 92%","Remove oxygen completely and observe on room air for 30 minutes"],"a":3,"x":["A saturation of 99% in a drowsy patient with severe COPD is the problem. Excess oxygen is driving carbon dioxide retention.","Nebulisers in COPD are driven by air. An oxygen-driven nebuliser adds to the over-oxygenation.","More oxygen will worsen carbon dioxide retention. His drowsiness is from carbon dioxide, not low oxygen.","Correct. In a COPD exacerbation oxygen is titrated to 88 to 92%. Excess oxygen raises carbon dioxide and increases deaths.","Stopping oxygen abruptly risks severe hypoxaemia. The dose is reduced to the target range, not removed."],"e":"Problem: drowsiness with a saturation of 99% on high-flow oxygen in severe COPD. Task: recognise oxygen-induced carbon dioxide retention and correct it. The 'good' saturation is the trap. What if his arterial gas then showed a PaCO2 of 68 mmHg and a pH of 7.28? Non-invasive ventilation.","k":"COPD exacerbation: titrate oxygen to 88 to 92%. A high saturation in a drowsy patient means too much oxygen.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), COPD exacerbations"],"c":["rs9-21","rs9-22","rs9-01","rs9-02","rs9-03"],"id":"rs-a52","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"COPD: exacerbations","nt":"rs-copd-exac#slow","s":"Joyce Palmer, aged 69 years, has mild COPD with an FEV1 of 68% predicted. Four days ago she started amoxicillin and prednisolone under her action plan for an exacerbation with green sputum. She returns because she 'still has a cough'. Her sputum is now pale yellow and less in volume, her breathlessness is improving, and she slept through last night. She has had no fever. She asks for 'a stronger antibiotic'.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Amoxicillin 500 mg every 8 hours, day 4","Prednisolone 37.5 mg daily, day 4","Tiotropium 18 micrograms daily","Salbutamol as needed, now twice a day"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 36.8 degrees, respiratory rate 16/min","SpO2 95% on room air","Chest: a few scattered wheezes, no crackles"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add doxycycline to the amoxicillin","Change to amoxicillin with clavulanate for a further 7 days","Change to ciprofloxacin to cover Pseudomonas","Complete the 5-day courses and explain that symptoms can last 7 to 10 days","Send sputum for culture and continue amoxicillin until the result is available"],"a":3,"x":["Combining two first-line antibiotics has no role.","Broader treatment is considered only in severe COPD with no response at 48 to 72 hours. She has mild disease and is improving.","Ciprofloxacin is for people with Pseudomonas in recent sputum who are not responding.","Correct. She is improving. In mild COPD the symptoms of an exacerbation commonly last 7 to 10 days, and no change of treatment is needed.","Sputum culture is not routine, and she has none of the indications for it. Therapy is not modified when symptoms are improving."],"e":"Problem: a lingering cough on day 4 in someone who is clearly getting better. Task: resist escalation. Every marker has moved the right way. What if she had severe COPD, was worse at 72 hours, and a bacterial cause still seemed likely? Then amoxicillin with clavulanate for 5 days.","k":"A slow-to-settle COPD exacerbation rarely needs a broader antibiotic. Symptoms last 7 to 10 days in mild disease.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations"],"c":["rs9-07","rs9-16","rs9-17","rs9-18","rs9-19"],"id":"rs-a53","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"COPD: exacerbations","nt":"rs-copd-exac#treat","s":"Harold Benson, aged 73 years, has moderate COPD. He attends your practice with a day of worse breathlessness and wheeze. He left his spacer at his daughter's house. The practice nurse suggests a salbutamol nebuliser and asks how you would like it set up. The treatment room has piped oxygen and a portable air compressor. His usual saturation is 93%.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Tiotropium-olodaterol, two puffs daily","Salbutamol as needed","Lercanidipine 10 mg daily","Metformin 500 mg twice daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Alert, speaking in sentences","Respiratory rate 22/min, heart rate 96/min","SpO2 91% on room air","Widespread wheeze"]}],"q":"How should the nebuliser be given?","o":["Ipratropium 500 micrograms, driven by oxygen at 6 L/min","Salbutamol 10 mg continuously, driven by oxygen","Salbutamol 5 mg with ipratropium 500 micrograms, driven by oxygen at 8 L/min, repeated every 20 minutes","Salbutamol 5 mg, driven by air from the compressor","Salbutamol 5 mg, driven by oxygen at 8 L/min"],"a":3,"x":["Ipratropium is not given to people taking a long-acting muscarinic antagonist, and the nebuliser should not be driven by oxygen.","Continuous high-dose nebulisation is for life-threatening asthma.","This is the severe asthma regimen. He takes a LAMA, so ipratropium is avoided, and oxygen should not drive the nebuliser.","Correct. If a nebuliser is needed in COPD, it is driven by compressed air, to avoid dangerous over-oxygenation. Salbutamol 2.5 to 5 mg is the dose.","The dose is right. The driving gas is wrong: 8 L/min of oxygen can cause carbon dioxide retention."],"e":"Problem: a nebuliser is needed for a COPD exacerbation in a patient on a LAMA. Task: choose drug and driving gas. Two rules apply: air, not oxygen, and no ipratropium with a LAMA. What if his saturation were 85%? Give controlled oxygen by nasal prongs alongside the air-driven nebuliser, aiming for 88 to 92%.","k":"Nebulisers in COPD are driven by air. Ipratropium is not used with a LAMA.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), COPD exacerbations"],"c":["rs9-01","rs9-02","rs9-03","rs9-04","rs9-05"],"id":"rs-a54","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"COPD: exacerbations","nt":"rs-copd-exac#hospital","s":"Elsie Thornton, aged 81 years, has severe COPD and lives alone in a retirement unit. Her neighbour has called you to a home visit. Over two days Elsie has become too breathless to walk from her bedroom to the kitchen, which she normally manages with a frame. She has not eaten since yesterday. She started prednisolone and salbutamol yesterday under her action plan. She has no advance care directive and says she wants 'whatever will help'.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide-glycopyrronium-formoterol, two puffs twice daily","Salbutamol 8 puffs by spacer, every 2 hours since last night","Prednisolone 37.5 mg daily, day 2","Warfarin as charted"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Alert, speaking in short phrases","Respiratory rate 26/min, heart rate 108/min, irregular","SpO2 87% on room air","New pitting oedema to both knees"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add amoxicillin and review tomorrow","Arrange a chest X-ray and blood tests for tomorrow morning, then decide","Arrange transfer to hospital by ambulance now","Increase prednisolone to 75 mg daily and salbutamol to hourly","Organise a home oxygen concentrator for delivery this afternoon"],"a":2,"x":["She may need an antibiotic, but she has failed treatment at home and has several indications for admission.","Waiting a day for tests leaves an unstable woman alone overnight.","Correct. No response to treatment at home, unable to walk between rooms or eat, low saturation, new oedema, a new irregular pulse and living alone are each indications for hospital.","Higher doses do not address the failure of home treatment, and hourly salbutamol with an irregular pulse adds risk.","Home oxygen is prescribed on blood gases when stable, not started during an exacerbation."],"e":"Problem: an exacerbation that has not responded to a day of correct home treatment. Task: recognise the indications for admission. There are six in the stem. Her stated wish for treatment matters too. What if she had an advance care plan declining hospital? Her preference would guide care at home, with palliative support.","k":"COPD exacerbation: hospital if not responding, cannot walk between rooms or eat, hypoxic, confused, new oedema or arrhythmia.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), COPD exacerbations","Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Handbook (2024)"],"c":["rs9-20","rs9-01","rs9-02","rs9-03","rs9-04"],"id":"rs-a55","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"COPD: exacerbations","nt":"rs-copd-exac#after","n":4,"s":"Albert Dimitriou, aged 70 years, was discharged four days ago after his first hospital admission for a COPD exacerbation. He attends your practice with his wife. He has stopped smoking since the admission.","q":"Which FOUR of the following should be addressed at this visit or arranged from it? Select four.","o":["A check of his inhaler technique and adherence","A discussion about advance care planning","A long course of oral prednisolone to prevent relapse","A referral to pulmonary rehabilitation","A review and update of his written action plan","A routine sputum culture","Prophylactic doxycycline through winter"],"sc":[1,1,0,1,1,0,0],"x":["Scores 1. Technique and adherence are reviewed after every exacerbation, before changing treatment.","Scores 1. A hospital admission is a sentinel event that should prompt this discussion.","Scores 0. The course is 5 days. Long-term oral corticosteroids cause harm without benefit.","Scores 1. Rehabilitation after an exacerbation reduces readmission and can start within 2 to 4 weeks of discharge.","Scores 1. The plan is updated and its use is checked.","Scores 0. Sputum culture is not routine.","Scores 0. There is no evidence for doxycycline as prophylaxis. A long-term macrolide is a specialist decision for frequent exacerbations."],"e":"The post-discharge visit happens within 7 days. It covers technique, adherence, whether maintenance treatment should step up, the action plan, rehabilitation, vaccination, smoking and advance care planning.","k":"After a COPD admission: review within 7 days, technique, action plan, pulmonary rehabilitation, advance care planning.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), COPD exacerbations","Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Handbook (2024)","Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations"],"c":["rs9-23","rs9-24","rs9-25","rs9-26","rs9-01"],"id":"rs-k09","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Pneumonia in adults","nt":"rs-cap#features","s":"Tina Marshall, aged 36 years, is a retail manager with five days of cough that began with a sore throat and runny nose. For two days she has coughed up yellow-green sputum and her chest feels sore when she coughs. She has had no fever at home and is still going to work. She does not smoke and has no medical conditions. She asks for antibiotics because 'it has gone to my chest'.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Combined oral contraceptive pill","Paracetamol and throat lozenges this week"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 37.2 degrees","Respiratory rate 14/min, heart rate 76/min","SpO2 98% on room air","Chest: a few scattered wheezes that clear with coughing, no focal signs"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange a chest X-ray today and start amoxicillin while waiting for the radiology result","Explain that this is acute bronchitis and that antibiotics will not help","Prescribe amoxicillin 1 g every 8 hours for 5 days","Prescribe doxycycline 100 mg every 12 hours for 5 days","Send sputum for culture and prescribe according to the result"],"a":1,"x":["Her observations and chest are normal. Imaging is for an unclear diagnosis, and antibiotics should not be started for a presumed viral illness.","Correct. Coloured sputum with normal temperature, pulse, breathing rate and saturation, and no focal signs, is acute bronchitis. It resolves without antibiotics.","This is the right regimen for low-severity pneumonia. She has no features of pneumonia.","This is the alternative for pneumonia with penicillin allergy or a suspected atypical organism. Neither applies.","Sputum culture has no role in acute bronchitis."],"e":"Problem: an acute cough with coloured sputum in a well young adult. Task: tell bronchitis from pneumonia. Sputum colour is the distractor. The deciding facts are the normal observations and the absence of focal signs. What if her temperature were 38.6 with a pulse of 108 and crackles at the right base? Pneumonia: assess severity and treat.","k":"Coloured sputum with normal observations and no focal signs is acute bronchitis: no antibiotics.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Community-acquired pneumonia in adults"],"c":["rs10-17","rs10-18","rs10-19","rs10-01","rs10-02"],"id":"rs-a56","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Pneumonia in adults","nt":"rs-cap#treat","s":"Andrew Phillips, aged 45 years, is a plumber with three days of fever, rigors and a cough productive of rust-coloured sputum, and pain on the right side of his chest when he breathes in. He lives with his wife, is eating and drinking, and has no medical conditions or drug allergies. He does not smoke. A chest X-ray this morning shows consolidation in the right lower lobe only.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Ibuprofen 400 mg as needed this week","No regular medicines"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 38.7 degrees, alert and oriented","Respiratory rate 20/min, heart rate 96/min, blood pressure 124/78 mmHg","SpO2 95% on room air","Bronchial breathing and crackles at the right base"]}],"q":"Which treatment is MOST appropriate?","o":["Amoxicillin 1 g orally every 8 hours for 5 days","Amoxicillin 1 g orally every 8 hours plus doxycycline 100 mg orally every 12 hours for 7 days","Amoxicillin 500 mg orally every 8 hours for 5 days","Amoxicillin with clavulanate 875+125 mg orally every 12 hours for 10 days","Roxithromycin 300 mg orally daily for 7 days"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. He has none of the features that suggest admission, so this is low-severity pneumonia. Amoxicillin 1 g every 8 hours for 5 days is the drug of choice.","Two drugs from the start are for people with significant comorbidity whose follow-up is uncertain. He has neither, and the course is 5 days.","This is the dose for a COPD exacerbation. Pneumonia needs 1 g.","Broader cover is not needed for low-severity pneumonia, and 10 days is twice the usual course.","Macrolide resistance in pneumococcus is rising, and roxithromycin is not the recommended agent."],"e":"Problem: lobar pneumonia in a fit 45-year-old with reassuring observations. Task: grade severity and choose the regimen. Check the list: respiratory rate under 22, pulse not over 100, systolic not under 90, no confusion, saturation not under 92%, one lobe. Low severity. What if he had had hives with amoxicillin? Doxycycline 100 mg every 12 hours for 5 days.","k":"Low-severity pneumonia: amoxicillin 1 g every 8 hours for 5 days.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Community-acquired pneumonia in adults"],"c":["rs10-01","rs10-02","rs10-04","rs10-05","rs10-06"],"id":"rs-a57","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Pneumonia in adults","nt":"rs-cap#severity","s":"Dorothy Evans, aged 79 years, lives alone in her own home. Her daughter brings her in because she has been 'muddled' since yesterday, calling her by her sister's name, and has a new cough. She has not eaten today. She has type 2 diabetes and hypertension and normally manages her own medicines and shopping. A chest X-ray taken on the way shows left lower lobe consolidation.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Metformin 1 g twice daily","Perindopril 5 mg daily","Atorvastatin 20 mg daily","Temazepam 10 mg at night"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 37.9 degrees; disoriented to day and place","Respiratory rate 24/min, heart rate 104/min, blood pressure 118/70 mmHg","SpO2 93% on room air","Crackles at the left base"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange admission to hospital for monitoring and treatment","Prescribe amoxicillin and ask her daughter to phone tomorrow with an update on how she is going","Prescribe amoxicillin with doxycycline and review at home in three days","Prescribe doxycycline alone and arrange a repeat chest X-ray in six weeks","Stop temazepam and review in the morning"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. New confusion, a respiratory rate of 24 and a pulse of 104 are each features that may need admission. She also lives alone and is not eating.","A phone call tomorrow is not enough observation for moderate-severity pneumonia in a confused 79-year-old who lives alone.","Adding a second antibiotic does not replace the monitoring she needs. Admission features call for support, not broader cover.","This would under-treat and under-observe her.","Sedatives are worth reviewing after recovery. Her confusion is new and is explained by the pneumonia."],"e":"Problem: pneumonia in an older woman whose main symptom is confusion. Task: grade severity and choose the place of care. Three admission features are present, and her circumstances add weight. What if she were lucid, with a respiratory rate of 18, a pulse of 88, and her daughter staying with her? Low severity: amoxicillin at home with review.","k":"Pneumonia with any of RR 22 or more, HR over 100, SBP under 90, new confusion or SpO2 under 92%: consider admission.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Community-acquired pneumonia in adults"],"c":["rs10-10","rs10-11","rs10-12","rs10-13","rs10-14"],"id":"rs-a58","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Pneumonia in adults","nt":"rs-cap#review","s":"Marcus Lee, aged 52 years, is an office worker treated for right middle lobe pneumonia with amoxicillin 1 g every 8 hours. He finished the 5-day course eleven days ago. His fever settled on the second day and his appetite is back. He returns because he is still coughing, mostly dry, gets tired by mid-afternoon and is a little puffed on the stairs. He has never smoked. He asks whether he needs another course of antibiotics.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Amoxicillin (completed eleven days ago)","Esomeprazole 20 mg daily","Fexofenadine as needed"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 36.7 degrees","Respiratory rate 14/min, heart rate 72/min","SpO2 98% on room air","Chest: a few crackles at the right base, no dullness"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange a CT pulmonary angiogram today","Prescribe a 7-day course of amoxicillin with clavulanate, as the first antibiotic was too narrow","Prescribe a second 5-day course of amoxicillin","Prescribe prednisolone 25 mg daily for 5 days","Reassure him that cough and fatigue can last weeks, and arrange follow-up"],"a":4,"x":["He has no pleuritic pain, tachycardia or hypoxia. Nothing suggests a pulmonary embolism.","He responded to amoxicillin: the fever settled and he is recovering. A broader antibiotic is not indicated.","Prolonged symptoms after a good response are not a reason to extend or repeat antibiotics.","Corticosteroids have no role in post-pneumonia cough.","Correct. Cough, breathlessness and sputum can take several weeks to settle, and fatigue can last months. He is afebrile with normal observations."],"e":"Problem: lingering cough and tiredness eleven days after a successful course. Task: separate slow recovery from treatment failure. His normal observations and settled fever are the deciding facts. What if his fever had returned with pleuritic pain and dullness at the base? Look for an effusion or empyema.","k":"After pneumonia, cough can last weeks and fatigue months. That is not a reason for more antibiotics.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Community-acquired pneumonia in adults"],"c":["rs10-03","rs10-23","rs10-24","rs10-25","rs10-01"],"id":"rs-a59","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Pneumonia in adults","nt":"rs-cap#treat","s":"Rachel Stein, aged 29 years, is a primary school teacher with a week of dry cough, headache, low-grade fever and tiredness. Several children in her class have been diagnosed with Mycoplasma infection this term. She had facial swelling and wheeze with amoxicillin as a teenager. A chest X-ray shows patchy change at the left base. She is not pregnant, and a pregnancy test today is negative.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Levonorgestrel intrauterine device","Paracetamol as needed"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 37.9 degrees, alert","Respiratory rate 16/min, heart rate 88/min, blood pressure 112/70 mmHg","SpO2 97% on room air","Fine crackles at the left base"]}],"q":"Which antibiotic is MOST appropriate?","o":["Amoxicillin 1 g every 8 hours for 5 days","Cefalexin 500 mg every 6 hours for 5 days","Cefuroxime 500 mg every 12 hours for 7 days, as she has only had a reaction to amoxicillin","Ciprofloxacin 500 mg every 12 hours for 5 days","Doxycycline 100 mg every 12 hours for 5 days"],"a":4,"x":["She has had a severe immediate reaction to amoxicillin, and beta-lactams do not treat Mycoplasma.","A cephalosporin is avoided after a severe immediate penicillin reaction, and it has no activity against atypical organisms.","Cefuroxime is an option after a non-severe reaction, as part of combination therapy. Hers was severe, and it would not cover Mycoplasma.","Ciprofloxacin is not a recommended treatment for community-acquired pneumonia.","Correct. Low-severity pneumonia with a suspected atypical organism, or with penicillin hypersensitivity, is treated with doxycycline 100 mg every 12 hours for 5 days."],"e":"Problem: low-severity pneumonia with two reasons to avoid amoxicillin: a likely atypical organism and a severe penicillin reaction. Task: choose the alternative. Either reason alone leads to the same answer. What if she had no allergy and no Mycoplasma contact? Amoxicillin 1 g every 8 hours.","k":"Low-severity pneumonia with penicillin allergy or a suspected atypical organism: doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 5 days.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Community-acquired pneumonia in adults"],"c":["rs10-01","rs10-02","rs10-04","rs10-05","rs10-06"],"id":"rs-a60","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Pneumonia in adults","nt":"rs-cap#notbetter","s":"Peter Vasquez, aged 63 years, is a retired fisherman with heavy alcohol use and poor dentition. He was started on amoxicillin 1 g every 8 hours six days ago for right lower lobe pneumonia, and has taken every dose. He still has fevers each evening and a heavy ache on the right side of his chest. His cough now brings up foul-tasting sputum. He stopped smoking ten years ago.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Amoxicillin 1 g every 8 hours, day 6","Thiamine 100 mg daily","Pantoprazole 40 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 38.4 degrees","Respiratory rate 20/min, heart rate 98/min, blood pressure 126/74 mmHg","SpO2 94% on room air","Dull percussion note and absent breath sounds at the right base"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add doxycycline and review in one week","Arrange a repeat chest X-ray and urgent hospital assessment for a complication","Continue amoxicillin for a further 5 days and reassure him","Stop the antibiotic, as the persistent fever is likely to be a drug reaction to amoxicillin","Switch to roxithromycin for atypical cover"],"a":1,"x":["Adding atypical cover does not address a likely collection, and a week is too long to wait.","Correct. Fever and chest discomfort that persist on appropriate treatment, with a dull base and foul sputum, suggest empyema or lung abscess. He needs imaging and hospital assessment.","He is not clinically stable at day 6, which carries higher risk. More of the same is not the answer.","Drug fever is possible in principle, but new dullness, absent breath sounds and foul sputum point to a complication.","Atypical organisms would not explain the examination findings."],"e":"Problem: no response to six days of correct treatment. Task: reassess the diagnosis instead of extending the antibiotic. The dull base, foul sputum, alcohol use and poor teeth point to an anaerobic complication. What if he were afebrile and simply still coughing? Slow recovery, with no change needed.","k":"Pneumonia with persistent fever and chest discomfort on treatment: look for effusion, empyema or abscess.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Community-acquired pneumonia in adults"],"c":["rs10-26","rs10-27","rs10-28","rs10-01","rs10-02"],"id":"rs-a61","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Pneumonia in adults","nt":"rs-cap#prevent","n":4,"s":"Gwen Holloway, aged 71 years, has recovered from her second episode of community-acquired pneumonia in 18 months. She smokes five cigarettes a day and takes temazepam nightly and esomeprazole, which was started for reflux four years ago and has not been reviewed.","q":"Which FOUR of the following measures would help to prevent a further episode? Select four.","o":["A daily low-dose antibiotic through winter","Advice and support to stop smoking","Pneumococcal vaccination, if not up to date","Review of the ongoing need for esomeprazole","Review of the ongoing need for temazepam","Routine chest X-ray every six months","Vitamin C 1 g daily"],"sc":[0,1,1,1,1,0,0],"x":["Scores 0. Prophylactic antibiotics are not a preventive strategy for community-acquired pneumonia.","Scores 1. Stopping smoking is a listed preventive measure.","Scores 1. Pneumococcal, influenza and COVID-19 vaccination are all reviewed.","Scores 1. Proton pump inhibitors are associated with a higher incidence of pneumonia.","Scores 1. Benzodiazepines and other sedatives are associated with a higher incidence of pneumonia.","Scores 0. Surveillance imaging does not prevent pneumonia.","Scores 0. Not a recommended preventive measure."],"e":"Prevention after pneumonia is mostly a review of what is already there: vaccines, smoking, and medicines that raise risk. Aspiration risk, exercise and nutrition are on the same list.","k":"Prevent pneumonia: vaccinate, stop smoking, and review proton pump inhibitors and sedatives.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Community-acquired pneumonia in adults"],"c":["rs10-29","rs10-30","rs10-01","rs10-02","rs10-03"],"id":"rs-k10","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"}]});
